hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"16801","Kurayb, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl. 681 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ayyub al-'Allaf, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam, informed us Muhammad ibn Abi Ja'far - informed me Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah - informed me Kurayb, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, he said: I rode behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the morning of Muzdalifah, and I kept hearing him uttering the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah."
"16802","682 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Madini, (h). And narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi 'Umar al-'Adani, narrated to us Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from Muhammad Abu Harmalah, from Kurayb, from Ibn 'Abbas - al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas narrated to me: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took him behind him from Muzdalifah, and kept uttering the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah."
"16803","Shu'bah, freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, from Ibn 'Abbas. 683 - Narrated to us Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri, narrated to me my father, narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Shu'bah, freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, from Ibn 'Abbas, from his brother al-Fadl, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took him behind him on the morning of Muzdalifah, and kept uttering the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah."
"16804","'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl. 684 - Narrated to us Musa ibn Harun, narrated to us Khallad ibn Aslam, narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, he said: I rode behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the morning of Muzdalifah, and he kept uttering the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah."
"16805","685 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Aban al-Asbahani, narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib, narrated to us 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb, informed me 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, from his brother al-Fadl, he informed him, he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he came to 'Arafah in the Farewell Pilgrimage. When the people departed, he said to the people: O people, calmly, with tranquility; for righteousness is not in hurrying."
"16806","Abu Ma'bad, freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl. 686 - Narrated to us Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, narrated to us 'Abd Allah ibn Salih, (h). And narrated to us Musa ibn Harun, narrated to us Qutaybah ibn Sa'id, (h). And narrated to us 'Amr ibn Hafs al-Sadusi, narrated to us 'Asim ibn 'Ali, they said: Narrated to us al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Ma'bad, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas - and he was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said to the people on the evening of 'Arafah and the morning of Muzdalifah when they stood: Hold to tranquility, while restraining his she-camel; until when he entered Muhassir, which is of Mina, he said: Take pebbles for pellet-throwing with which the Jamrah is stoned. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept uttering the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrah."
"16807","687 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Suwayd al-Shibami, informed us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us Ibn Jurayj, (h). And narrated to us Yahya ibn Ayyub al-'Allaf al-Misri, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam, informed us Nafi' ibn Yazid, informed me Ibn Jurayj, informed me Abu al-Zubayr, informed me Abu Ma'bad, freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said on the evening of 'Arafah and the morning of Muzdalifah to the people when they set out: Hold to tranquility, while restraining the she-camel; until when he entered Mina, descending at Muhassir, he said: Take pebbles for pellet-throwing with which the Jamarat are stoned. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proceeded as a man proceeds."
"16808","688 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Abu Khalid al-Ahmar, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Ma'bad, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"16809","689 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Abbar, narrated to us Hisham ibn 'Ammar, narrated to us Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Ma'bad, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stone with the like of pebbles for pellet-throwing."
"16810","690 - Narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, narrated to us Isma'il ibn Abi Uways, narrated to me my brother, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrated to me Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, that Abu Ma'bad, freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, informed him, that he heard Ibn 'Abbas, that 'Abbas, on the day of 'Arafah - while al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - 'Abbas said: When the people magnified Allah, I said: Al-Fadl will narrate to me what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. He said: When he set out on the evening of 'Arafah, the people set out with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began pulling his camel's head, restraining it, and calling: O people, hold to tranquility. Until when he reached Muhassir, he hastened a little, saying: Take pebbles for pellet-throwing."
"16811","691 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi, narrated to me my father, narrated to us Shu'ayb ibn Ishaq al-Awza'i, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amir al-Aslami, narrated to me Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Ma'bad, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said on the evening of 'Arafah and the morning of Muzdalifah when they set out: Hold to tranquility, while restraining his she-camel until he reached Muhassir; and said: Take pebbles for pellet-throwing with which the Jamarat are stoned."
"16812","692 - Narrated to us 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Miqlas al-Misri, narrated to me my father, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, informed me 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Ma'bad, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would magnify Allah, utter the tahlil and supplicate on the evening of 'Arafah. When the people set out, he said: Hold to tranquility. When he reached the mountain pass, he turned to it and wanted water, then performed ablution; the people mounted. When he came to Muzdalifah, he combined Maghrib and 'Isha'. When he prayed Fajr, he stood; when he departed, the people set out. He said, while restraining his mount: Hold to tranquility. Until when he came to Mina, he said: Take pebbles for pellet-throwing with which the Jamarat are stoned. He kept uttering the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah."
"16813","'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl. 693 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', he said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas invited al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas to food on the day of 'Arafah. He said: I am fasting. 'Abd Allah said: Do not fast, for a milk-bowl containing milk was brought near to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of 'Arafah and he drank it; so do not fast, for people are led astray by you."
"16814","694 - Narrated to us 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, (h). And narrated to us Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti, narrated to us Abu al-Sha'tha' 'Ali ibn al-Hasan, they said: Narrated to us Hafs ibn Ghiyath, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drinking from a worn waterskin on the day of 'Arafah."
"16815","695 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Shatwi, narrated to us 'Affan ibn Muslim, (h). And narrated to us Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Harb, they said: Narrated to us Shu'bah, from Mushash, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the weak of Banu Hashim and their children to depart from Muzdalifah by night."
"16816","696 - Narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl and Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Bara', they said: Narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Madini, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Sulayman Abu Isma'il al-Mu'addib, from Ya'qub ibn 'Ata', from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in two white Sahuli garments."
"16817","697 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Mansur, narrated to us Hashim, from Ibn Abi Layla, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas - al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas informed me, he said: I was the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Muzdalifah, and he poured forth with tranquility upon him."
"16818","698 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Humayd, narrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani', narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd, narrated to us 'Abd al-Malik, meaning Ibn Abi Sulayman, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poured forth from 'Arafat with Usamah ibn Zayd behind him. The she-camel moved about with him while he was standing at 'Arafat before pouring forth, raising his hands not beyond his head. When he poured forth, he proceeded gently until he came to Muzdalifah; and he poured forth from 'Arafah with al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas behind him."
"16819","699 - Narrated to us Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari, narrated to us Isma'il ibn Muslim, narrated to us 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas: that he was the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and kept uttering the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrah at al-'Aqabah on the day of sacrifice."
"16820","700 - Narrated to us al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, narrated to us al-Rabi' ibn Yahya al-Ushnani, narrated to us Shu'bah, from 'Amir al-Ahwal, Jabir al-Ju'fi and Ibn 'Ata', from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas: that he was the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and uttered the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah on the day of sacrifice."
"16821","701 - Ibrahim ibn Salih al-Shirazi narrated to us, Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated, Ibn Jurayj narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) continued pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah on the Day of Sacrifice."
"16822","702 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Qays ibn Sad, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl: that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pronouncing the talbiyah on the Day of Sacrifice until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16823","703 - Sulayman ibn al-Muafa ibn Sulayman narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Khattab ibn al-Qasim narrated, from Khusayf, from Ata and Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: Usamah was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Arafah to Muzdalifah, and I was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Muzdalifah to Mina, and I kept hearing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16824","704 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji and Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar narrated to us, they said: Jamil ibn al-Hasan narrated, Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan narrated, Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated, from Qatadah, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that he was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16825","705 - Al-Sari ibn Sahl al-Jundaysaburi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Rushayd narrated, Mujjaah ibn al-Zubayr narrated, from Qatadah, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, that he was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16826","706 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated, Firdaws ibn al-Ashari narrated, from Masud ibn Sulayman, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Said ibn Jubayr and Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned the jamrah."
"16827","707 - Ismail al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Naysaburi narrated, Umar ibn Abd Allah ibn Razin al-Sulami narrated, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated, from Matar al-Warraq, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not abandon the talbiyah until he stoned the farthest jamrah on the Day of Sacrifice."
"16828","708 - Ismail al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn al-Abbas, that he narrated to him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took al-Fadl ibn Abbas as his riding companion from Jam (Muzdalifah). Al-Fadl said: He kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16829","709 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to me, Abu Amir narrated, from Rabah ibn Abi Maruf, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pronounced the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16830","710 - Muhammad ibn Yaqub al-Khatib al-Ahwazi narrated to us, Abu Yusuf Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Qulusi narrated, Ubayd Allah ibn Abd al-Majid narrated, Rabah ibn Abi Maruf narrated, Ata narrated, from Ibn Abbas, that al-Fadl informed him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept pronouncing the talbiyah, he could hear him, until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16831","711 - Idris ibn Jafar al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated, Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pronounced the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16832","712 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Humayd ibn Masadah narrated, Sufyan ibn Habib narrated, from Ibn Jurayj and Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: and he was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned the jamrah."
"16833","713 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Yala ibn Ubayd narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poured forth from Arafat, with Usamah ibn Zayd as his riding companion, and the she-camel swerved with him while he was standing, so he struck her before he poured forth, while he was raising his hands not beyond his head. Then when he poured forth he moved at a gentle pace until he came to Jam (Muzdalifah), then he poured forth from Jam, then he pronounced the talbiyah from Jam, with al-Fadl as his riding companion. Al-Fadl said: He kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16834","714 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini al-Basri narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: I was the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah on the Day of Sacrifice."
"16835","715 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Azdi narrated, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Kathir ibn Shinzhir, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pronounced the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah on the Day of Sacrifice."
"16836","716 - Al-Hasan ibn al-Abbas al-Razi narrated to us, Sahl ibn Zanjalah narrated, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, from al-Awzai, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) continued the tahlil (talbiyah) until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16837","717 - Ishaq ibn Jamil al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mani narrated, Hushaym narrated, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, al-Fadl ibn Abbas informed me: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pronounced the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16838","718 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated; and Bishr ibn Musa narrated to me, al-Humaydi narrated, Man ibn Isa al-Qazzaz narrated, al-Harith ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abd Allah ibn Iyas al-Laythi narrated, from al-Qasim ibn Abd Allah ibn Yazid ibn Qusayt, from his father, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me, so I went out to him, and I found him feverish with his head bandaged, and he said: Take my hand, O Fadl. So I took his hand until he reached the pulpit, and he sat on it, then he said: Call out among the people. So I called out among the people, and people gathered to him, and he praised Allah and extolled Him, then he said: O people, rights owed by me among you have drawn near; whomever I flogged on his back, here is my back, let him take retaliation from it; and whomever I reviled in his honor, here is my honor, let him take retaliation from it. Let no man say: I fear ill-feeling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); ill-feeling is not of my nature nor of my character. The dearest of you to me is one who takes a right if he has one, or absolves me, so that I meet Allah with a contented soul; and I do not see that as availing me unless I stand among you repeatedly. Then he came down and prayed the noon prayer, then he returned to the pulpit and repeated his statement about ill-feeling and other matters, then he said: O people, whoever has anything with him, let him return it, and let him not say: the disgrace of this world; for the disgrace of this world is easier than the disgrace of the Hereafter. A man stood up to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I owe you three dirhams. He said: We neither belie a speaker nor put him to oath, so how did they become due to you from me? He said: Do you remember the day a poor man passed by you, and you ordered me to pay them to him? So he said: Pay them to him, O Fadl. Then another man stood up to him and said: I have three dirhams which I misappropriated in the path of Allah. He said: And why did you misappropriate them? He said: I was in need of them. He said: Take them, O Fadl. Then he said: O people, whoever fears something against himself, let him stand so I may supplicate for him. A man stood up to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a great liar, and I am a hypocrite, and I am very sleepy. He said: O Allah, grant him truthfulness and faith, and remove sleep from him when he intends. Then another man stood up to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a great liar, and I am a hypocrite, and there is nothing among things but I have committed it. Umar said to him: Man, you have disgraced yourself. So he said: Hush, O son of al-Khattab; the disgrace of this world is easier than the disgrace of the Hereafter. Then he said: O Allah, grant him truthfulness and faith, and turn his affair to good. Umar spoke to them a word, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umar is with me and I am with him, and the truth after me is with Umar wherever he is."
"16839","719 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani narrated, Ata ibn Muslim al-Khaffaf narrated, Jafar ibn Burqan narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bind my head, perhaps I may go out to the mosque. So I bound his head with a yellow band, then he went out to the mosque supported between two men."
"16840","720 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father was overtaken by Hajj while he is a very old man, unable to mount the camel; shall I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: Perform Hajj on his behalf."
"16841","721 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated to me, Wahb ibn Khalid narrated, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, that a woman from Khatham asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with al-Fadl as his riding companion, and she said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation of Hajj upon His servants overtook my father while he is a very old man; if they tie him on his mount he cannot hold firm; shall I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes, perform Hajj on his behalf."
"16842","722 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qanabi narrated, from Malik; and Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated, Malik informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, he said: Al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a woman from Khatham came to him seeking a ruling, and al-Fadl began looking at her, and she at him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began turning al-Fadl's face to the other side. She said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation upon His servants in Hajj overtook my father as an old man unable to remain firm on the mount; shall I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes; and that was during the Farewell Pilgrimage."
"16843","723 - Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, al-Hiql ibn Ziyad narrated, al-Awzai narrated to me, al-Zuhri narrated to me, Sulayman ibn Yasar narrated to me, that Abd Allah ibn Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took al-Fadl ibn Abbas as his riding companion on the Day of Sacrifice behind him on his mount, and al-Fadl was a handsome man. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped to give rulings to the people, and a beautiful woman from Khatham came seeking a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Fadl began looking at her, impressed by her beauty, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned, with al-Fadl looking at her, and he put his hand behind, took al-Fadl's chin, and turned his face away from looking at her. That woman said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation in Hajj upon His servants overtook my father as a very old man unable to sit upright on the mount; will it suffice if I perform Hajj on his behalf? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Yes, perform Hajj on his behalf."
"16844","724 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Salm al-Razi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Imran al-Asbahani narrated, Abu Dawud narrated, Zamah ibn Salih narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage and said: O Messenger of Allah, my father is a very old man, unable to hold himself on the mount; will it suffice for him if I perform Hajj on his behalf? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes."
"16845","725 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Sad narrated, my uncle narrated, my father narrated, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, that Sulayman ibn Yasar informed him, that Ibn Abbas informed him, that a woman from Khatham sought a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) during the Farewell Pilgrimage, with al-Fadl ibn Abbas as the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that Allah's obligation in Hajj upon His servants overtook my father as a very old man unable to sit upright on the mount; will it suffice for him if I perform Hajj on his behalf? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Yes. And al-Fadl began turning toward her, and she was a beautiful woman, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took al-Fadl and turned his face to the other side."
"16846","726 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah al-Majishun narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A woman from Khatham came seeking a ruling from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage, and she said: O Prophet of Allah, Allah's obligation upon His servants in Hajj overtook my father as a very old man unable to sit upright on the mount; will it suffice for him if I perform Hajj on his behalf? The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes."
"16847","727 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi al-Kufi, and Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated, Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ruwasi narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that a woman asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about her father who died without performing Hajj. He said: Perform Hajj on behalf of your father."
"16848","728 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Rashdin al-Misri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Jabir narrated, from his father Isa ibn Jabir, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that a woman from Khatham sought a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that Allah's obligation in Hajj upon His servants overtook my father as a very old man unable to sit upright on the mount; will it suffice for him if I perform Hajj on his behalf? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes."
"16849","729 - Ahmad ibn Rashdin narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sufyan al-Amiri narrated, Rishdin ibn Sad narrated, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a woman from Khatham sought his ruling during the Farewell Pilgrimage, and she said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation in Hajj upon His servants overtook my father as a very old man unable to sit upright on the mount; will it suffice for him if I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said to her: Yes."
"16850","730 - Ahmad ibn Rashdin narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his grandfather, from Qurrah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that a woman from Khatham sought a ruling from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) during the Farewell Pilgrimage, and she said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation in Hajj upon His servants overtook my father as a very old man unable to sit upright on the mount; will it suffice for him if I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said to her: Yes."
"16851","731 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Yahya al-Tujibi narrated, from al-Layth; and Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, or from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, or from both of them, from Ibn Abbas, that he informed him that a woman from Khatham said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation in Hajj upon His servants overtook my father as a very old man unable to sit upright on the mount; will it suffice for him if I perform Hajj on his behalf? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Yes."
"16852","732 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated, Sufyan narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"16853","733 - Yahya ibn Muhammad al-Hinnai narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Muadh narrated, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), likewise."
"16854","734 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Abd Allah al-Zubayri narrated to me, Aishah bint al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that a woman from Khatham said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation in Hajj overtook my father as a very old man unable to hold himself on the mount; shall I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes, perform Hajj on behalf of your father."
"16855","735 - Abu Usamah al-Halabi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Abi Mani al-Rusafi narrated, my grandfather narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Abd Allah ibn Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took al-Fadl ibn Abbas as his riding companion on the Day of Sacrifice behind him on the rear of his mount, and al-Fadl was a handsome man. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped for the people to give them rulings, and a beautiful woman from Khatham came seeking a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Fadl began looking at her, impressed by her beauty, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned, with al-Fadl looking at her, and he put his hand behind to al-Fadl's chin and turned his face away from looking at her. That Khathamite woman said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation in Hajj overtook my father as a very old man unable to sit upright on the mount; will it suffice for him if I perform Hajj on his behalf? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Yes, perform Hajj on his behalf."
"16856","736 - Abu Shuayb Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Jafar al-Raqqi narrated; and Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Nufayli narrated, they said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated, from Abd al-Karim, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: I was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16857","737 - Al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, Abu Israil al-Mulai narrated, from Fadl ibn Amr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, and one of them from the other, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends Hajj, let him hasten, for the sick may fall ill, the stray animal may be lost, and need may arise."
"16858","738 - Al-Abbas ibn Hamdan al-Hanafi al-Asbahani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hakim narrated, Kathir ibn Hisham narrated, from Furat ibn Sulayman, from Abd al-Karim, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, and one of them from the other, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever among you intends Hajj, let him hasten, for the sick may fall ill, the stray animal may go astray, and need may arise."
"16859","739 - Abdan ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated, al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Israil narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that he was the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he poured forth from Jam (Muzdalifah), and a bedouin accompanied him with a beautiful daughter of his, and I began looking at her, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned my face away from her, and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16860","740 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from his father, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: I was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a bedouin was with him, and he mentioned the hadith."
"16861","741 - Al-Miqdad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated; and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Shuayb al-Simsar narrated to us, Khalid ibn Khidash narrated, they said: Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, he said: Al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas was the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the youth began glancing at women and looking at them, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept turning his face away with his hand from behind, and the youth kept glancing at women. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O son of my brother, this is a day in which whoever controls his hearing, his sight, and his tongue will be forgiven."
"16862","742 - Ali ibn Said al-Razi narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated, Abd al-Razzaq narrated, Jafar ibn Sulayman informed us, from Awf, from Ziyad ibn Husayn, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, from his brother al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me on the morning of al-Aqabah: Come, gather pebbles for me. So I gathered for him seven pebbles, the pebbles of khadhf (small throwing). When he placed them in his palm he said: With the likes of these, with the likes of these; beware of excess, beware of excess, for those before you perished only through excess in religion. And a group narrated this hadith from Awf, among them Sufyan al-Thawri, and none said, from Ibn Abbas from his brother, except Jafar ibn Sulayman, and none narrated it from Jafar except Abd al-Razzaq."
"16863","743 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that Ibn Abbas used to report, that al-Fadl ibn Abbas informed him, that he entered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into the House (the Kaaba), and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray in the House when he entered it, but when he came out he bowed two rakahs at the door of the House."
"16864","744 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, Amr ibn Dinar informed us, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood in the Kaaba glorifying Allah, magnifying Him, supplicating, and seeking forgiveness, and he did not bow nor prostrate."
"16865","745 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that he entered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into the Kaaba, with Bilal at the door. He said: He did not pray; and Bilal said: He prayed."
"16866","746 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated, Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated, Shubah narrated, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: I was the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of Sacrifice, and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16867","747 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Ibn Awn, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Yala ibn Uqbah, he said: I woke up junub (in major impurity), so I came to Abu Hurayrah and asked him. He said to me: Break your fast. I said: Shall I not fast it and make it up? He said: No, but break your fast. So I came to Marwan ibn al-Hakam and asked him, and I informed him of Abu Hurayrah's statement. So he sent Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham to Aishah and asked her. She said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go out for the dawn prayer with his head dripping from intercourse, then he would fast that day. So he returned to Marwan and informed him of that. He said: Go to Abu Hurayrah and inform him. He said: I did not hear it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); al-Fadl ibn Abbas only narrated it to me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"16868","748 - Al-Husayn ibn al-Sumayda al-Antaki narrated to us, Musa ibn Ayyub al-Nasibi narrated, Damrah ibn Rabiah narrated, from al-Ala ibn Harun, from Ibn Awn, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Yala ibn Uqbah, he said: Major impurity befell me in Madinah in the month of Ramadan, so I woke up junub. I came to Abu Hurayrah and mentioned that to him. He said: Break your fast. I said: Shall I fast my day and make up a day in its place? He said: No, break your fast. So I came to Marwan ibn al-Hakam, while he was governor over Madinah, and I mentioned that. So he sent Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hakam to Aishah and she was asked about that. Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wake up in the month of Ramadan junub, not from a wet dream, and he would continue his fast. So Abu Bakr came to Marwan and informed him of Aishah's statement. Marwan said to him: I adjure you not to meet Abu Hurayrah but inform him of Aishah's statement. So I met him and informed him of Aishah's statement. He said: I did not hear it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); al-Fadl ibn Abbas only informed me of it. Ibn Awn said: I said to Raja ibn Haywah: Who narrated to you this hadith from Yala? He said: Yala himself narrated to me this hadith."
"16869","749 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is overtaken by dawn has no fast. So I and my father went to Aishah and Umm Salamah, and we asked them about that. They informed us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wake up junub, not from a dream, then he would fast. He said: So we went to Marwan and informed him of their statement and Abu Hurayrah's statement. Marwan said: I adjure you both to go to Abu Hurayrah and inform him. He said: So I met Abu Hurayrah at the door of the mosque, and my father said to him: The governor has adjured us concerning a matter to mention it to you. He said: And what is it? So my father narrated to him. The face of Abu Hurayrah changed color, then he said: Thus said al-Fadl ibn Abbas, and he knows best. Al-Zuhri said: So he shifted the hadith to another."
"16870","750 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Yahya ibn Said narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham narrated to me, from his father, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: Whoever wakes up junub has no fast. So Abu Bakr and his father Abd al-Rahman went until they entered upon Umm Salamah and Aishah, and both of them said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wake up junub, not from a wet dream, then he would fast. So Abu Bakr and his father Abd al-Rahman went to Marwan and narrated to him. He said: I adjure you both to go to Abu Hurayrah and narrate to him. So Abu Bakr and Abd al-Rahman went until they came to Abu Hurayrah and informed him. He said: Did they both tell you? They said: Yes. He said: They know best; al-Fadl ibn Abbas only narrated it to me."
"16871","751 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated, Ayyub narrated, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, he said: When it reached Marwan that Abu Hurayrah narrates from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever wakes up junub shall not fast that day, my father sent to Aishah to ask her. So I went with him to her and he asked her. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wake up junub, not from a wet dream, then he would wake up fasting. So he returned to Marwan and narrated to him. He said: Meet Abu Hurayrah and narrate to him. He said: He is my neighbor, and I dislike confronting him with what he dislikes. He said: I adjure you to come to him. So he came to him and said: O Abu Hurayrah, I disliked confronting you with what you dislike, but the governor has adjured me. So he narrated to him. He said: Al-Fadl ibn Abbas narrated it to me."
"16872","752 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Tamim ibn al-Muntasir narrated, Ishaq al-Azraq narrated, from Sharik, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, he said: Al-Fadl ibn Abbas was asked: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in the House (the Kaaba)? He said: No."
"16873","753 - Al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas al-Asfati narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated, Wuhayb narrated, Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated, Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas: that he informed him that he was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept pronouncing the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16874","754 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated, from Ibn Jurayh, from Muhammad ibn Umar, from al-Abbas ibn Ubayd Allah ibn al-Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited al-Abbas in a desert land of ours, and we had a gazelle doe and a she-donkey grazing, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with them in front of him, and they were neither moved back nor driven away."
"16875","755 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayh, Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Ali informed me, that al-Fadl ibn Abbas said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited, and he mentioned similar to it. And Abd al-Razzaq did not mention in his hadith al-Abbas ibn Ubayd Allah ibn al-Abbas."
"16876","756 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib narrated to me, that Abbas ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abbas narrated to him, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Abbas in a desert land of ours, and he prayed Asr with a bitch of ours or a she-donkey of ours in front of him, and he did not stop her, nor did we move her back."
"16877","757 - Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated to me, from Abd Rabbih ibn Said, from Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Abd Allah ibn Nafi ibn al-Amya, from Rabiah ibn al-Harith, from al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Prayer is two by two; you recite the tashahhud in every two rakahs, humble yourself, show reverence, show need, raise your hands, saying: you lift them to your Lord, facing Him with your face, and you say: O Lord, O Lord; whoever does not do that, it is defective."
"16878","758 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated, Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, that he was the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother is a very old woman; if I carry her she cannot hold firm, and if I tie her I fear I will kill her; shall I perform Hajj on her behalf? He said: What do you think, if your mother owed a debt, would you settle it for her? He said: Yes. He said: So perform Hajj on behalf of your mother."
"16879","759 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Abu Taqi Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated, Shubah narrated, from Ibn Abi Ishaq, that he heard Abd Allah ibn Shaddad narrate, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, my father is a very old man unable to perform Hajj and Umrah, nor to travel. He said: Perform Hajj on behalf of your father, and Umrah."
"16880","760 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki and Abd al-Rahman ibn Salm al-Razi narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Abu Israil, from Fudayl ibn Amr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, and he is not Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends Hajj, let him hasten, for therein are the stray animal and the thing."
"16881","761 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated, Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated, from Sharik ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir, from Kurayb, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: I spent a night with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to see how he prays. He stood to a waterskin hung on a peg in the wall, performed ablution, then prayed two rakahs, his standing like his bowing, and his bowing like his prostration, then he slept, then he woke and performed ablution and rinsed his nose, then he recited fifty verses from Al Imran: Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of night and day. He kept doing this until he prayed ten rakahs, then he stood and prayed a single prostration and made witr with it, and the caller called at that time. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood after the muadh-dhin fell silent and prayed two light rakahs, then he sat until he prayed the dawn prayer."
"16882","762 - Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Umari narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated, my brother narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Sharik ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir, from Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, that he heard al-Fadl ibn Abbas say: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night, and when he returned from the evening prayer, he mentioned the hadith."
"16883","763 - Ahmad ibn Hammad ibn Zughbah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Ahmad ibn Hazim, from Sharik ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir, from Kurayb, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not stand for night prayer except that he used the toothstick."
"16884","764 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated; and Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated, Hammam narrated, from Qatadah, from Azrah, from al-Shabi, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, that he was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Jam (Muzdalifah) until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah, and no mount lifted its foot going early until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah."
"16885","765 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Jawn ibn Bashir narrated, from Uqbah al-Tai, from Yahya ibn Ubayd, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: Nabidh would be prepared for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at night, and he would drink it the next day and the night after, until the third day, then he would withhold."
"16886","766 - Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid al-Bayruti narrated to us, Safwan ibn Salih narrated, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, from al-Awzai, from Asad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abd Allah ibn Muhayriz, that Fadlalah ibn Ubayd said: Some people want to turn me away from my religion, and that will not be until I meet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions: Whoever sells food or fodder from what was acquired in the land of Rome for gold or silver, the fifth due to Allah and the shares of the Muslims become obligatory in it."
"16887","767 - Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid al-Bayruti narrated to us, Safwan ibn Salih narrated, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, from Marwan ibn Janah, from Yunus ibn Maysarah ibn Halbas, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that when his companions came to him, he would say: Study together, receive glad tidings, and increase; may Allah increase you in good, love you, and love those who love you. Return questions to us, for the reward of the last of them is like the reward of the first. Mix your discourse with seeking forgiveness."
"16888","768 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated, Muawiyah ibn Hafs narrated, from Dawud ibn al-Muhajir, from Ibn Muhayriz, he said: I accompanied Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I said: Advise me, may Allah have mercy on you. He said: Retain from me three traits by which Allah will benefit you: if you can know and not be known, do so; if you can listen and not speak, do so; and if you can sit and none sits to you, do so."
"16889","769 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me; Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Affan narrated; and Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, they said: Umar ibn Ali al-Muqaddami narrated, he said: I heard al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrate, from Makhul, from Abd Allah ibn Muhayriz, he said: I asked Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, and he was among those who pledged under the tree, and I asked him: Is hanging the thief's hand from the Sunnah? He said: A thief was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered, and his hand was cut, then he ordered it and it was hung on his neck."
"16890","770 - Al-Husayn ibn Ahmad ibn Yunus al-Ahwazi narrated to us, Hafs ibn Amr al-Rabbali narrated, Muhammad ibn Umar al-Waqidi narrated, Harithah ibn Abi Imran narrated, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hayyan narrated, from Ibn Muhayriz, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted at a stop on a journey or entered his house, he would not sit until he bowed two rakahs."
"16891","771 - Abu Shuayb al-Harrani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd Allah al-Bablutti narrated, Safwan ibn Amr narrated, Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrated to me, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) campaigned the campaign of Tabuk, and the mounts were strained severely. They complained to him of that, and he saw them as men not resting their mounts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at a narrow pass through which the people passed, and he stood upon it while the people passed, and he blew in them and said: O Allah, carry upon them in Your path, for You carry the strong and the weak, the moist and the dry, in sea and land. So they continued, and we did not enter Madinah but they were pulling at their reins."
"16892","772 - Ahmad ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi narrated to us, my father narrated; and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Ali ibn Bahr narrated, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, al-Awzai narrated, from Ismail ibn Abd Allah, from Maysarah, the freedman of Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that Fadlalah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah listens more keenly to a man with a beautiful voice reciting the Quran than the master of the singing girl listens to his singing girl."
"16893","773 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy and Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah ibn Masruh, from Rabiah ibn Yura, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wants the treasure of speech, upon him is: there is no power nor strength except with Allah."
"16894","774 - Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated to me, from Abu Shuja Said ibn Yazid al-Himyari, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran al-Maafiri, from Hanash al-Sanani, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari, he said: On the day of Khaybar I bought a necklace of gold and beads for twelve dinars, and I separated it, and its gold turned out to be more than twelve dinars. I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Gold is not sold for gold until it is separated."
"16895","775 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Said ibn Yazid, he said: I heard Khalid ibn Abi Imran narrate, from Hanash, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought on the day of Khaybar a necklace with beads strung with gold, which a man had bought for seven or nine; so he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. He said: No, until what is between them is distinguished. He said: I only intended the stones. He said: No, until what is between them is distinguished. He said: So it was returned until it was distinguished."
"16896","776 - Ahmad ibn Rashdin narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr and Ahmad ibn Salih narrated; and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Miqlas al-Misri narrated to us, my father narrated to me, they said: Ibn Wahb narrated, Amr ibn al-Harith, Ibn Lahi'ah, and Qurrah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Haywa'il informed me, from Amir ibn Yahya al-Sharani, from Hanash ibn Abd Allah, he said: We were with Fadlalah ibn Ubayd in the campaign of Rudis, and a necklace with jewels, pearls, and gold fell to me and my companions. My companions said to me: Buy it from us, we will accommodate you. I said: No, until I ask Fadlalah ibn Ubayd. So I asked him, and he said: Remove the gold, put it in one pan, put the gold in another pan, and take only like for like, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him take only like for like."
"16897","777 - Al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Hanash, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Place between you and the Fire a barrier, even with half a date."
"16898","778 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Hassan ibn Abd Allah narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Hanash ibn Abd Allah, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam is a spacious house; whoever enters it, it accommodates him. Hijrah is a spacious house; whoever enters it, it accommodates him. Whoever is called to Islam and accepts, and is called to Hijrah and emigrates, has left no avenue of good unsought, nor any escape from evil."
"16899","779 - Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Hanash, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he called for water and drank. I said: Were you not fasting? He said: Yes, but I vomited."
"16900","780 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, Abd Allah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, from Hanash ibn Abd Allah al-Sabai, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There are three in which jesting is not permitted: divorce, marriage, and emancipation."
"16901","781 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Hassan ibn Abd Allah narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Qays ibn al-Hajjaj, from Hanash, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no habs (endowment with retained benefit)."
"16902","782 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated; and Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated, they said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated, my father narrated, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Ayyub narrate, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi al-Sabah, from Hanash, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever grows a white hair in Islam, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection. A man said: Some men pluck white hair. So he said: Whoever wishes may pluck his white hair, or he said: his light."
"16903","783 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Said narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi al-Sabah, from Hanash, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever grows a white hair in Islam in the path of Allah, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection. A man said at that: Some men pluck white hair. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes, let him pluck his light."
"16904","784 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Haywah ibn Shurayh and Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, they said: Abu Hani narrated, that Abu Ali al-Janbi narrated to him, that he heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever dies upon a rank among these ranks will be raised upon it on the Day of Resurrection."
"16905","785 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani narrated to me, from Amr ibn Malik, that he heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever dies upon a rank among these ranks will be raised upon it on the Day of Resurrection."
"16906","786 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, Abu Hani informed me, that Abu Ali al-Janbi was informed that he heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd say: He heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Blessed is he who is guided to Islam, whose livelihood is sufficient, and who is content with it."
"16907","787 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated; and Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Isa al-Misri narrated, they said: Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani informed me, from Abu Ali al-Janbi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Successful is he who is guided to Islam, whose livelihood is sufficient, and who is content with it."
"16908","788 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, Abu Hani informed me, that Abu Ali Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi narrated to him, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Three, do not ask about them: a man who abandons the community, disobeys his leader, and dies disobedient; a slave girl or slave who flees from his master and dies; and a woman whose husband is absent from her while he has sufficed her the provision of this world, and she adorns herself after him; do not ask about them."
"16909","789 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated, Abu Hani informed me, that Abu Ali al-Janbi narrated to him, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three, do not ask about them: a man who contends with Allah for His grandeur, for His cloak is grandeur and His lower garment is might; a man who doubts Allah's decree; and one who despairs of Allah's mercy."
"16910","790 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani narrated to me, from Amr ibn Malik, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Three, do not ask about them... and he mentioned the like of it."
"16911","791 - Harun ibn Mallul al-Misri narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated, Abu Hani informed me, from Abu Ali al-Janbi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man supplicating in his prayer without glorifying his Lord nor sending blessings upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This one has hastened. When one of you prays, let him begin with glorifying his Lord and praising Him, then let him send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then let him supplicate with whatever he wishes."
"16912","792 - Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Said narrated, Rishdin ibn Sad narrated, from Abu Hani, from Abu Ali al-Janbi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was seated, a man entered and prayed, then said: O Allah, forgive me and have mercy on me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: You have hastened, O praying one. When you pray and sit, praise Allah with what He deserves, then send blessings upon me, then call upon Him. Then another prayed, praised Allah and sent blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Ask, you will be given."
"16913","793 - Harun ibn Mallul al-Misri narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated, Abu Hani informed me, from Abu Ali al-Janbi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man supplicating in his prayer without glorifying his Lord nor sending blessings upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This one has hastened. When one of you prays, let him begin with glorifying his Lord and praising Him, then let him send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then let him supplicate with whatever he wishes."
"16914","794 - Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Said narrated, Rishdin ibn Sad narrated, from Abu Hani, from Abu Ali al-Janbi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was seated, a man entered and prayed, then said: O Allah, forgive me and have mercy on me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: You have hastened, O praying one. When you pray and sit, praise Allah with what He deserves, then send blessings upon me, then call upon Him. Then another prayed, praised Allah and sent blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Ask, you will be given."
"16915","795 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani narrated to me, that Abu Ali Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi narrated to him, that he heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd say: A man entered into prayer without praising, glorifying, nor sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have hastened, O praying one. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught them; and he heard a man praying, praising Allah alone and sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O praying one, supplicate, you will be answered; and ask, you will be given."
"16916","796 - Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated to me, Abu Hani al-Khawlani narrated to me, from Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said during the Farewell Pilgrimage: Shall I inform you of the believer? One whom people trust with their wealth and lives; the Muslim is one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe; the mujahid is one who strives against his soul in obedience to Allah; and the emigrant is one who abandons errors and sins."
"16917","797 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, Abu Hani al-Khawlani informed me, that he heard Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi say: I heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The mujahid is one who strives against his soul for Allah, mighty and exalted."
"16918","798 - Harun ibn Mallul al-Misri narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated, Abu Hani informed me, from Amr ibn Malik, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, men would fall from their standing due to the hardship in them, and they were the companions of the Suffah, until the bedouins would say: These are mad. When he finished the prayer he turned to them and said: If you knew what is for you with Allah, you would love to increase in poverty and need. Fadlalah said: And I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day."
"16919","799 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani narrated to me, that Abu Ali Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi narrated to him, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: I heard him say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people, men would fall from their standing in prayer due to privation in them, and they were among the companions of the Suffah; the Arabs would say: These are mad. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer he turned to them and said: If you knew what is for you with Allah, you would love that you increase in poverty and need. Fadlalah ibn Ubayd said: And I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day."
"16920","800 - Abd al-Malik ibn Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, my father narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Abu Hani, that Abu Ali al-Janbi narrated to him, that he heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people, men would fall from their standing due to privation, and they were the companions of the Suffah, until the bedouins would say: These are mad. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer he turned to them and said to them: If you knew what is for you with Allah, you would love that you increase in poverty and need. Fadlalah ibn Ubayd said: And I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day."
"16921","801 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani narrated to me, that Abu Ali al-Janbi narrated to him, that he heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I am a guarantor, and the guarantor is the surety, for whoever believes, submits, and emigrates, of a house in the outskirts of Paradise, a house in the middle of Paradise, and a house in the highest part of Paradise. Whoever does that has left no avenue of good unsought, nor any escape from evil; so let him die whenever he wishes to die."
"16922","802 - Muhammad ibn Hatim al-Marwazi narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Nasr and Hayyan ibn Musa narrated, they said: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, from Abu Hani, from Amr ibn Malik, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no dead person who dies except his deed is sealed, except one who dies guarding the frontier in the path of Allah, for his deed grows for him, and he is safe from the trial of the grave."
"16923","803 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani narrated to me, from Amr ibn Malik, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every dead person has his deed sealed except the one guarding the frontier in the path of Allah, for his deed grows for him on the Day of Resurrection, and he is secured from the tormentor of the grave."
"16924","804 - Harun ibn Mallul al-Misri narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated, from Abu Hani, from Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The rider greets the one standing, and the few greet the many."
"16925","805 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani narrated to me, that Amr ibn Malik informed him, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The horseman greets the walker and the one standing, and the few greet the many."
"16926","806 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah and Ibn Wahb narrated, from Abu Hani Humayd ibn Hani, from Abu Ali al-Janbi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed on the Day of Sacrifice and said: Your blood and your honors are sacred, like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours."
"16927","807 - Harun ibn Mallul al-Misri narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated, Abu Hani informed me, from Amr ibn Malik, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a necklace with gold and beads from the spoils, offered for sale. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) removed what gold was in the necklace and sold it alone, then said: Gold for gold, weight for weight."
"16928","808 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated, Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated, from Said ibn Abi Ayyub, from Abu Hani, from Amr ibn Malik, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, whoever believes in You and testifies that I am Your Messenger, make meeting You beloved to him, ease Your decree for him, and lessen for him of this world. And whoever does not believe in You and testify that I am Your Messenger, do not make meeting You beloved to him, do not ease Your decree for him, and increase for him of this world."
"16929","809 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Thumamah ibn Shufi, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us to level graves."
"16930","810 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Yahya narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Abu Ali al-Hamdani informed him, that he saw Fadlalah ibn Ubayd order the graves of the Muslims to be leveled in the land of Rome, and he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Level your graves."
"16931","811 - Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Miqlas al-Misri narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Wahb narrated, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Thumamah ibn Shufi narrated to him, he said: We were with Fadlalah ibn Ubayd in the land of Rome, and a companion of ours died. Fadlalah ibn Ubayd ordered his grave to be leveled, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: He commands leveling them."
"16932","812 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab al-Jumahi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Jumahi narrated, Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated, Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated, Abu Ibrahim al-Siyai informed me, from Abu Salih Ali al-Hamdani, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Level graves upon the face of the earth when you bury."
"16933","813 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Abu Hani narrated to me, that he heard Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi say: I heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought on the day of Khaybar a necklace with beads and gold, from the spoils, offered for sale. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the gold in the necklace to be removed alone, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Gold for gold, weight for weight."
"16934","814 - Harun ibn Mallul al-Misri narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Qabath ibn Razin narrated, from Ulayy ibn Rabah, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that they were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of his campaigns, and they acquired a necklace with gold and jewels, and they sold it for dinars. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell thus, and he forbade them that, and he said: The jewels separately, and the gold separately."
"16935","815 - Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi narrated to us, my father narrated, Abd Allah ibn Yahya al-Maafiri narrated, from Said ibn Abi Ayyub, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Amir ibn Yahya, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever boycotts his brother for more than three days is in the Fire, unless Allah overtakes him with His generosity."
"16936","816 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Muawiyah al-Ghanawi and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya, the scribe of al-Umari, narrated, Rishdin narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Muradi, from Abu Marwan, from Sahl ibn Alqamah, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hujayrah, from Uqbah ibn Amir and Fadlalah ibn Ubayd: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wake up junub, then continue and fast."
"16937","817 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Ibn Aishah narrated, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomited and broke his fast."
"16938","818 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Muhammad and Yala narrated, Ubayd narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq, he said: I heard Fadlalah ibn Ubayd narrate that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to them on a day he was fasting, and he called for a vessel with water and drank. We said: O Messenger of Allah, this is a day you were fasting? He said: Yes, but I vomited."
"16939","819 - Ahmad ibn Rashdin al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Umayrah ibn Abi Najiyah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq, from Hanash ibn Abd Allah, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for food, and it was said: O Messenger of Allah, were you not fasting this day? He said: Yes, but I vomited."
"16940","820 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Qays ibn Bahri al-Sadafi, from Man ibn Humayd, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, that he entered upon Aishah, and she brought out hairs from the hair of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it was red, dyed."
"16941","821 - Abu Shuayb Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd Allah al-Bablutti narrated, Safwan ibn Amr narrated, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd... (the chain breaks off here in the source; no matn preserved)."
"16942","822 - Ahmad ibn al-Husayn ibn Nasr al-Hadhdha narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Abi Karimah al-Harrani narrated, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Abu Abd al-Rahim, from Abu Abd al-Malik, from al-Qasim, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Islam is three dwellings: a lower, an upper, and a chamber. As for the lower: Islam, upon which the generality of Muslims have entered; none of them is asked but says: I am a Muslim. As for the upper: the superiority of their deeds; some Muslims are better than others. As for the highest chamber: jihad in the path of Allah; none attains it but the best of them."
"16943","823 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated, Said ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated, from Makhul, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earth is Allah's earth, and the servants are Allah's servants; whoever revives dead land, it is his."
"16944","824 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Usayd, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah, and Ahmad ibn Ali al-Jarudi al-Asbahaniyyun narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Isam ibn Yazid narrated, my father narrated, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Nuaym ibn Dhi Hubab, from Fadlalah ibn Ubayd, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three are barren (ruinous): a leader who, if you do good, is not grateful, and if you do evil, does not forgive; a neighbor who, if he sees good, buries it, and if he sees evil, spreads it; and a wife who, when you are present, harms you, and when you are absent from her, betrays you."
"16945","825 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Amr ibn Uthman narrated, my father narrated, Muhammad ibn Muhajir narrated, from Yunus ibn Maysarah ibn Halbas, from Umm al-Darda, that Fadlalah ibn Ubayd used to say: O Allah, I ask You for contentment after decree, coolness of life after death, the delight of looking at Your Face, and longing to meet You, without harmful adversity nor misleading trial. And he claimed that they were supplications with which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate."
"16946","826 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated, Khalid narrated, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, from Abd Allah ibn Fadlalah al-Laythi, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me, and among what he taught me he said: Guard the five prayers. I said: These are hours in which I have occupations, so command me with a comprehensive matter which, if I do it, will suffice me. He said: Guard the two Asrs. I said: And what are the two Asrs? He said: A prayer before the rising of the sun, and a prayer before its setting."
"16947","827 - Al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Adi ibn al-Fadl narrated, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad al-Dili, Abd Allah ibn Fadlalah al-Laythi informed me, from his father, he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and whoever had a registrar (arif) stayed with his registrar, and whoever had no registrar stayed at the Suffah. So I stayed at the Suffah. He said: A man called out to him on Friday and said: O Messenger of Allah, dates have burnt our stomachs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Soon, or whoever among you lives, bowls will be brought to him morning and evening, and you will clothe walls as the Kaaba is covered."
"16948","828 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali narrated; and Muhammad ibn Ruzayq ibn Jami al-Misri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated, they said: Yusuf ibn Khalid al-Samti narrated, Abu Jafar al-Khatmi narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Uqbah ibn al-Fakih ibn Sad, from his grandfather al-Fakih ibn Sad, and he was a Companion: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe on Friday, on the day of Arafah, and on the day of Sacrifice."
"16949","829 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Uqbah ibn Wahb ibn Uqbah al-Amiri narrated, he said: I heard my father narrate, from al-Fujay al-Amiri, that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: What is lawful for us of carrion? He said: What is your food? He said: We drink milk in the evening and in the morning. He said: That is hunger. So he permitted them carrion in this condition. Abu Nuaym said: Uqbah explained it to me, he said: A cup in the evening, and a cup in the morning."
"16950","830 - Abu Zurah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Ata al-Amiri al-Bakkai brought out to us a letter from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them, and he said to us: Write it, but he did not dictate it to us; he claimed that the son of al-Fujay's daughter narrated to him: This is a letter from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to al-Fujay, whoever follows him and whoever submits, whoever establishes prayer and gives zakah, obeys Allah and His Messenger, gives a fifth of the spoils, supports Allah's Prophet, testifies to his Islam, and dissociates from the polytheists, for he is secure with the security of Allah and Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"16951","831 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Hammam al-Dallal narrated, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudrib, from Furat ibn Hayyan, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had ordered his killing, while he was a spy for Abu Sufyan and an ally. He passed by a circle of the Ansar and said: I am a Muslim. A man among them said: O Messenger of Allah, he says: I am a Muslim. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among you are men whose faith appears to me, among them is Furat ibn Hayyan."
"16952","832 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin narrated, Umar ibn Sahl al-Mazini narrated, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Furat ibn Hayyan al-Ijli, that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the alliance of Jahiliyyah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps you ask about the alliance of Lakhm and Tamim? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Islam only increases it in strength."
"16953","833 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna and al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Fasawi narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yunus Abu Muslim al-Mustamli narrated; and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, they said: Sufyan ibn Waki narrated, they said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated, from Umar ibn Muraqqa, from Qays ibn Zuhayr, he said: We went with Hanzalah ibn al-Rabi to the mosque of Furat ibn Hayyan, and prayer came. He said to him: Lead. He said to him: I would not lead you, while you are older than me in age, earlier in emigration, and the mosque is your mosque. Furat said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say something about you, so I shall never lead you. He said: Did you witness him the day I came to him at Taif and he sent me as a scout? He said: Yes. So Hanzalah stepped forward and led them in prayer. Furat said: O sons of Ijlan, I only put this one forward because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him as a scout to Taif, and he came and informed him of the news. He said: You have spoken the truth; return to your home, for you have witnessed the night. When he turned away, he said to us: Follow this one and his likes in prayer."
"16954","834 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn Hubab narrated to us, Abu Hammam al-Dallal Muhammad ibn Muhabbab narrated, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated, from Abu Janab, from Yahya ibn Hani, from Farwah ibn Musayk, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I fight, with those of my people who come forward, those of them who turn back? He said: Yes. When I turned away, he called him and said: Call them to Islam; if they refuse, fight them. I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about Saba: is it a man or a woman? He said: He is a man of the Arabs who fathered ten; six of them went south (Yemen), and four of them went north (Sham). As for those who went south: al-Azd, Kindah, Madhhij, the Ashariyyun, Himyar, and Anmar, among whom is Bajilah. As for those who went north: Amilah, Ghassan, Lakhm, and Judham."
"16955","835 - Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Nufayli narrated, Abbad ibn Kathir al-Ramli narrated, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from al-Bara ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Farwah ibn Musayk, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we have neighbors of Saba, people of might, dominion, and power; permit me to call them to Islam, and if they refuse, permit me to fight them with my people and whoever obeys me. So he permitted him. Then it occurred to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: You mentioned concerning Saba what you mentioned; call them to Islam, and if they answer you, accept from them and refrain from them; and if they refuse, do not confront them until my command comes to you. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you say of Saba: land or woman? He said: It is neither land nor woman, but a man who fathered ten tribes; six of them went south and four went north. The southerners: Madhhij, al-Azd, Kindah, Himyar, the Ashariyyun, and Anmar. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, and what is Anmar? He said: The father of Bajilah and Khatham. And four of them went north: Lakhm, Judham, Ghassan, and Amilah."
"16956","836 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated; and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, they said: Usamah narrated, al-Hasan ibn al-Hakam al-Nakhai narrated, Abu Sabrah narrated, from Farwah ibn Musayk al-Muradi, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I not fight those of my people who turn back, with those of them who come forward? He said: Yes. Then it occurred to me, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, no; rather the people of Saba are mightier and stronger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me and permitted me to fight Saba. When I left him, Allah revealed concerning Saba what He revealed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What has the Ghatafani done? So he sent to my house and found that I had departed, so he brought me back. When I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) I found him seated among his Companions. He said: Call the people; whoever answers you among them, accept, and whoever refuses, do not hasten until you speak to me. A man among the people said: O Messenger of Allah, what is Saba: land or woman? He said: It is neither land nor woman, but he fathered ten of the Arabs. As for six, they went south; as for four, they went north. As for those who went north: Lakhm, Judham, Amilah, and Ghassan. As for those who went south: al-Azd, Kindah, Himyar, the Ashariyyun, Anmar, and Madhhij. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, what is Anmar? He said: They are those of Bajilah and Khatham."
"16957","837 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated; and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, they said: Abu Usamah narrated, Mujalid narrated to me, from al-Shabi, from Farwah ibn Musayk al-Muradi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Were your two days and the day of Hamdan hateful to you? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah; the perishing of family and kin. He said: Yet it is better for those of you who remain."
"16958","838 - Ahmad ibn Abi Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Amr al-Adani narrated, Faraj ibn Said ibn Alqamah ibn Said ibn Abyad ibn Hammal narrated, my uncle Thabit ibn Said narrated to me, from his father, that Farwah ibn Musayk al-Muradi narrated to him, that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Saba and said: O Messenger of Allah, what is Saba: a man, a mountain, or a valley? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, rather a man who fathered ten; four went north and six went south. Lakhm, Judham, Amilah, and Ghassan went north; Himyar, Madhhij, al-Azd, Kindah, the Ashariyyun, and Anmar, of whom is Bajilah, went south."
"16959","839 - Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Saigh narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Jafar al-Jafari narrated, Abd Allah ibn Salamah al-Rabi narrated to me, from Muslim ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah ibn Masud, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Farwah ibn Amir al-Judhami sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his Islam, and he gifted him a white mule. Farwah was a servant of Caesar, king of Rome, over the Arabs near him, and his abode was Amman and around it. When that of his affair reached Rome, they imprisoned him, and he spoke in his prison... When they resolved to crucify him, they crucified him at a water called Afra of Palestine. When he was raised on the wood he said... (poetic fragments in the source are garbled and incomplete)."
"16960","840 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, concerning the naming of those of the Ansar who witnessed al-Aqabah, then from Banu Bayadah: Farwah ibn Amr; and he witnessed Badr."
"16961","841 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Ibn Jabir: from Jabir: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send a man of the Ansar of Banu Bayadah called Farwah ibn Amr to estimate the fruit of the people of Madinah."
"16962","842 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Sahl, from Rafi ibn Khadij: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send Farwah ibn Amr to estimate the date palms. When he entered the garden he counted what qinw (bunches) were in it, then struck some against others according to what he saw in them, and he would not err."
"16963","843 - Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Abu Wahb al-Jayshani, that he heard al-Dahhak ibn Fayruz al-Daylami narrate, from his father, that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have submitted, and under me are two sisters. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Divorce whichever of them you wish."
"16964","844 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah, from Abu Wahb al-Jayshani, from Abu Khirash al-Ruayni, from al-Daylami, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I had two sisters whom I had married in Jahiliyyah. He said: When you return, divorce one of them."
"16965","845 - Abd Allah ibn Ayyub al-Qarabi narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman al-Nashiti narrated, Jarir ibn Hazim narrated, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Ayyub narrate, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Wahb al-Jayshani, from al-Dahhak ibn Fayruz, from his father, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have submitted, and under me are two sisters. He said: Divorce whichever of them you wish."
"16966","846 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah narrated to us, my father narrated; and Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated; and Idris ibn Abd al-Karim al-Haddad al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated; they said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani, from Abd Allah ibn Fayruz al-Daylami, from his father, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we are people of grapes and vineyards, and the prohibition of wine has come down, so what should we do? He said: Make it into raisins. They said: What should we do with the raisins? He said: Soak them for your morning meal and drink them at your evening meal, and soak them at your evening meal and drink them at your morning meal. They said: O Messenger of Allah, should we not leave it until it becomes strong? He said: Do not put it in large jars nor in gourds; put it in waterskins, for if it stays past its pressing time it becomes vinegar. I said: O Prophet of Allah, we are as you know, and our clan is where you know, and we live among those you know, so who is our guardian? He said: Allah and His Messenger."
"16967","847 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Amr ibn Uthman narrated, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani, from Abd Allah ibn Fayruz al-Daylami, from his father, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Isma'il ibn Ayyash."
"16968","848 - Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi narrated to us, Abu Umayr ibn al-Nahhas narrated, Damrah ibn Rabi'ah narrated, from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani, from Abd Allah ibn Fayruz al-Daylami, from his father, who said: We brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the head of the Absi liar."
"16969","849 - Muhammad ibn Rashid al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Khalid al-Missisi narrated, Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated, Abd Allah al-Zubayri narrated, from Imran ibn Abi al-Fadl, Ibn al-Daylami narrated to us, from his father, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Our land is a cold land and we have vineyards, so what should we do with them? He said: Make them into raisins, and he mentioned the like of it."
"16970","850 - Idris ibn Abd al-Karim al-Haddad narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated, Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah, from Ruzayq ibn Hakim, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from al-Daylami, who said: I came as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: We make food and drink and feed it to our cousins. He said: Does it intoxicate? I said: Yes. He said: It is unlawful. When it was time for me to bid him farewell I mentioned it to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, they will not be patient without it. He said: Whoever is not patient without it, strike his neck. I said: O Prophet of Allah, I have two sisters under me in marriage. He said: Divorce whichever of them you wish."
"16971","851 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated; and Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Maqdisi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir al-San'ani narrated; they said: al-Awza'i narrated, from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani, from Abd Allah ibn Fayruz al-Daylami, from his father Fayruz, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we are as you know, and our clan is where you know, and we have settled among those you know, so who is our guardian? He said: Allah and His Messenger. He said: I said: That suffices me."
"16972","852 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Bazzaz al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Qudamah al-Jawhari narrated, al-Muharibi narrated, from Abu Raja Muhriz ibn Abd Allah, from Sadaqah, from Urwah ibn Ruwaym, from Ibn al-Daylami, who is the nephew of al-Najashi and had served the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites Say: He is Allah, the One one hundred times in prayer or otherwise, Allah writes for him an acquittal from the Fire."
"16973","853 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn al-Dahhak narrated, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from al-Awza'i, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Fayruz al-Daylami, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be a cry in Ramadan. They said: O Messenger of Allah, in its beginning, its middle, or its end? He said: No, rather in the middle of Ramadan; when the night of the middle, a Friday night, comes, there will be a cry from the sky by which seventy thousand will fall stunned, seventy thousand will be made mute, seventy thousand will be made blind, and seventy thousand will be made deaf. They said: O Messenger of Allah, who among your ummah will be safe? He said: Whoever stays in his house, seeks refuge in prostration, and proclaims the takbir for Allah aloud. Then another cry will follow it; the first cry is the cry of Jibril and the second is the cry of Satan. The cry is in Ramadan, the turmoil in Shawwal, the tribes are distinguished in Dhu al-Qa'dah, the pilgrims are raided in Dhu al-Hijjah and in Muharram. And what is Muharram? Its beginning is a trial upon my ummah and its end is joy for my ummah. A riding camel with its saddle on which the believer escapes in that time is better for him than a palace yielding one hundred thousand."
"16974","Al-Falatan ibn Asim. 854 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Sawti narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated; and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated, my father narrated to me, from al-Falatan ibn Asim, who said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, and his gaze fixed upon a man walking in the mosque, so he said: O so-and-so. He said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And no one contested him in speech except that he said: O Messenger of Allah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you bear witness that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: No. He said: Do you read the Torah? He said: Yes, and the Gospel. He said: And the Qur'an? He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if I wished I could read it. He said: Then he adjured him, saying: Do you find me in the Torah and the Gospel? He said: I find one like you, of your appearance and your place of emergence; we had hoped he would be from us, but when you came out we were uncertain whether you are he, then we looked and behold, you are not he. He said: Why is that? He said: With him there are of his ummah seventy thousand who will enter Paradise without reckoning or punishment, while with you there is only a small group. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I am indeed he, and they are indeed my ummah, more than seventy thousand and seventy thousand."
"16975","855 - Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated, from Salih ibn Umar, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his maternal uncle al-Falatan ibn Asim, who said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when his gaze fixed upon a man, so he called him, and a Jewish man came forward wearing a shirt, trousers, and sandals gathered upon him. He kept saying: O Messenger of Allah, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: Do you bear witness that I am the Messenger of Allah? But he refused. He would say nothing except: O Messenger of Allah, and he would say: Do you bear witness that I am the Messenger of Allah? But he refused. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you read the Torah? He said: Yes. He said: And the Gospel? He said: Yes, and the Furqan, by the Lord of Muhammad, if I wished I could read it. He said: I ask you by the One Who sent down the Torah and the Gospel and the things He created with them: do you not find me in them? He said: Yes, we find one with your description emerging from your place of emergence, until we had hoped he would be among us. When you came out we thought you are he, but when we looked, behold, you are not he. He said: On what basis? He said: We find that of his ummah seventy thousand will enter Paradise without reckoning, while you are only few. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned, proclaimed the takbir and the tahlil twice, then said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I am indeed he, and my ummah is indeed more than seventy thousand and seventy thousand."
"16976","856 - Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari narrated to us, Affan narrated; and Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated; and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated; they said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated, my father narrated to me, al-Falatan ibn Asim narrated, who said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when revelation came down upon him, and when revelation came down upon him his gaze would grow fixed, his eyes open, and his hearing and sight would be emptied for what came from Allah. When it ended he said to a scribe: Write: Not equal are those of the believers who sit and those who strive, the verse. So the blind man stood and said: What is our fault? Then revelation came down upon him, so we said to the blind man: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is receiving revelation. He remained standing, saying: I repent to Allah. When it ended, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Write: Except those with a disability."
"16977","857 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated, Khalid reported, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his maternal uncle al-Falatan ibn Asim, who said: We came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the Bedouins who came to him, and we sat waiting for him. He came out with anger on his face and sat for a long time without speaking, then said: I came out to you while the Night of Decree and the false messiah had become clear to me, and I came out to explain them to you and give you glad tidings of them. Then I met at the threshold of the mosque two men quarreling, with Satan alongside them, so I intervened between them, and it was forgotten by me and snatched away from me; I shall give you only fragments of it."
"16978","858 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated, Za'idah narrated, from Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi, from his father, that his maternal uncle al-Falatan ibn Asim reported to him that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for the Night of Decree, seek it in the last ten nights, on the odd nights."
"16979","859 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from al-Falatan ibn Asim, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was shown the Night of Decree and then made to forget it, so seek it in the last ten nights."
"16980","860 - Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated, from Salih ibn Umar, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his maternal uncle al-Falatan ibn Asim, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while we were sitting waiting for him, when he came out to us with anger on his face. He sat for a long time without speaking, then it was lifted from him, and he said: I came out to you while the Night of Decree and the false messiah had become clear to me, and I came out to you to explain them to you. Then I met in the mosque two men quarreling, with Satan between them, so I intervened between them, and it was snatched away from me. So seek it in the last ten nights. As for the false messiah, he is bright of forehead, effaced of eye, broad of chest, with blood in him, as though he is Ibn al-Uzza or Ibn al-Uzza ibn so-and-so."
"16981","861 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh narrated, Sharik narrated, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his maternal uncle, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in winter and found them praying in hooded cloaks and garments, with their hands inside them."
"16982","Fudayk. 862 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Sufyan al-Qaysarani narrated to us, Fudayk ibn Sulayman narrated, al-Awza'i narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Salih ibn Bashir ibn Fudayk, that his grandfather Fudayk came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Establish the prayer, give the zakat, abandon evil, and dwell among your people's land wherever you wish."
"16983","The chapter of Qaf: those named Qays. Qays ibn Asim al-Minqari, and he is Qays ibn Asim ibn Sinan ibn Khalid ibn Mundhir ibn Ubayd ibn Muqa'is, and the name of Muqa'is is al-Harith; he was only called Muqa'is because he lagged behind when Banu Sa'd ibn Amr ibn Ka'b ibn Sa'd ibn Zayd Manat ibn Tamim allied. 863 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Mahdi al-Ubulli narrated, Abd al-Razzaq narrated, Isra'il reported, Simak ibn Harb narrated, who said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir saying: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying, when asked about His saying, And when the buried infant shall be asked: Qays ibn Asim came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I buried eight daughters of mine alive in the Jahiliyyah. He said: Free a slave for each one of them. I said: I am a man of camels. He said: Sacrifice, if you wish, a camel for each one of them."
"16984","What Qays ibn Asim narrated. 864 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me; and Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, my father narrated, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid reported, both from Mughirah, from his father, from Shu'bah ibn al-Taw'am, from Qays ibn Asim, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about alliances, and he said: Whatever alliance there was in the Jahiliyyah, hold to it, and there is no alliance in Islam."
"16985","865 - Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated, Sabalan narrated, Abbad ibn Abbad al-Muhallabi narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Mughirah, from Shu'bah ibn al-Taw'am, from Qays ibn Asim, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"16986","866 - al-Hasan ibn Sahl al-Mujawwiz al-Basri narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated, Sufyan reported, from al-Agharr ibn al-Sabbah, from Khalifah ibn Husayn, from Qays ibn Asim, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to wash with water and lote leaves."
"16987","867 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated, from al-Agharr ibn al-Sabbah, from Khalifah ibn Husayn, from Qays ibn Asim, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sought privacy with him, then accepted Islam, so the Prophet commanded him to wash with water and lote leaves. He washed, the prayer was established, and he entered between Abu Bakr and Umar and stood between them. When he finished the prayer, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Qays ibn Asim asked me about three things that none but Abu Bakr asked me about."
"16988","868 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated, from al-Agharr ibn al-Sabbah, from Khalifah ibn Husayn, that Qays ibn Asim said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I buried alive in the Jahiliyyah twelve daughters, or thirteen daughters, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Free a soul for each one of them."
"16989","869 - Ahmad ibn Isma'il al-Adawi al-Basri narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated, Shu'bah reported, from Qatadah, who said: I heard Mutarrif narrating from Hakim ibn Qays ibn Asim al-Tamimi, that his father gave a bequest at his death, saying: My sons, fear Allah, and make your eldest your chief, for when a people make their eldest their chief they succeed their father, and when they make their youngest their chief they are belittled among your peers. Adhere to acquiring wealth, for it awakens generosity and frees one from need of the vile. Beware of begging people, for it is among the last of a man's earnings. When I die, do not wail over me, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not wailed over. And when I die, bury me in a land where Bakr ibn Wa'il does not learn of my burial, for I used to raid them in the Jahiliyyah."
"16990","870 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me; and Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj and Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Judhu'i al-Qadi narrated to us; they said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated, they said: Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Wasiti narrated, Ziyad al-Khassas narrated, from al-Hasan, Qays ibn Asim al-Minqari narrated to me, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when he saw me I heard him saying: This is the chief of the people of the desert. When we alighted I came to him and began speaking to him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, the wealth for which there will be no liability upon me from a guest who visits me and a large family. He said: The best of wealth is forty, and more is sixty, and woe to the owners of two hundred, except one who gives from its milk and its aid, lends its back, slaughters its fat ones and feeds the contented and the petitioner. I said: O Prophet of Allah, how noble and fine are these traits! O Messenger of Allah, no valley I am in is ever free of people because of the multitude of my camels. He said: How do you manage? I said: The camels go out in the morning and the people go out, and whoever wishes takes a camel's head and goes off with it. He said: What do you do about lending the back? I said: I do not lend the young nor the old toothless she-camel. He said: Is your wealth dearer to you or the wealth of your freedmen? I said: My wealth is dearer to me than the wealth of my freedmen. He said: Of your wealth you have only what you ate and consumed, or wore and wore out, or gave and sent forth; otherwise it belongs to your freedmen. I said: By Allah, if I live I shall spend their number away. Al-Hasan said: By Allah, he did so. When death came to Qays he said: O my sons, take from me; I find none more sincere to you than me: when I die, make your elders your chiefs, and do not make your young ones your chiefs, lest people deem you foolish and you become insignificant to them. Adhere to improving wealth, for it awakens the generous and frees one from need of the vile. Beware of begging, for it is the last of a man's earnings; no one begs but that he abandons his earning. When I die, do not wail over me, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding wailing. Shroud me in the clothes in which I used to pray and fast. When you bury me, do not bury me in a place where anyone looks upon me, for there were skirmishes between me and Bakr ibn Wa'il in the Jahiliyyah, and I fear they will exhume me and do therein what will take away your religion and your worldly life. Al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him, said: He advised them in life and advised them in death."
"16991","871 - Muhammad ibn Zakariyya al-Ghallabi narrated to us, al-Ala ibn al-Fadl ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sawiyyah al-Minqari narrated, my father al-Fadl ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to me, from his father Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sawiyyah al-Minqari, who said: I heard Ibn Asim, while giving a bequest, gather his sons, thirty-two males, and say: O my sons, when I die, make your eldest your chief and you will succeed your fathers, and do not make your youngest your chief, lest that belittle you before your peers. Do not set up a wailing woman over me, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding wailing. Adhere to improving wealth, for it awakens the generous and frees one from need of the vile. Do not give the necks of camels except their due, nor withhold from them their due. Beware of every evil stock, for whatever pleases you one day, what grieves you is more. Beware of the sons of your enemies, for they are your enemies upon the way of their fathers. When I die, bury me in a place where this clan of Bakr ibn Wa'il does not look upon me, for there were violent quarrels between me and them in the Jahiliyyah, and I fear they will exhume me and ruin for them their worldly life and ruin for you your hereafter. Then he called for his quiver and commanded his eldest son, who was called Ali, saying: Take out an arrow from my quiver. He took it out, and he said: Break it, so he broke it. Then he said: Take out two arrows, so he took them out, and he said: Break them, so he broke them. Then he said: Take out three arrows, so he took them out, and he said: Break them, so he broke them. Then he said: Take out thirty arrows, so he took them out, and he said: Bind them with a bowstring, so he bound them. Then he said: Break them, but he could not break them. He said: O my sons, thus you are with unity, and thus you are with division. Then he began reciting poetry."
"16992","Qays ibn Makhramah ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf ibn Qusayy ibn Kilab ibn Murrah ibn Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik. 872 - Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi, Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, and Ibrahim ibn Na'ilah al-Asbahani narrated to us; they said: Ja'far ibn Mihran al-Sammak narrated, Abd al-A'la narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Muttalib ibn Abd Allah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was born in the Year of the Elephant, and between al-Fijar and the Elephant were twenty years. He said: They called it al-Fijar because they transgressed and permitted things they used to hold sacred. Between al-Fijar and the building of the Ka'bah were fifteen years, and between the building of the Ka'bah and the commissioning of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were five years, so our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commissioned when he was forty years old."
"16993","873 - Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham al-Sadusi narrated, Ziyad ibn Abd Allah al-Bakka'i narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, al-Muttalib ibn Abd Allah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather Qays ibn Makhramah, who said: I and the Messenger of Allah were born in the Year of the Elephant, so we are of the same age."
"16994","Qays ibn al-Nu'man al-Sakuni. 874 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated; and Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated; they said: Abd Allah ibn Iyad ibn Laqit narrated, Iyad ibn Laqit narrated, who said: I heard Qays ibn al-Nu'man al-Sakuni saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out with Abu Bakr, hiding from Quraysh, and they passed by a shepherd. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Is there a ewe mounted by the ram? He said: No, but here is a ewe left behind by exhaustion. He said: Bring it to me. He brought it to him, so he wiped its udder and prayed for blessing, then milked and gave Abu Bakr to drink, then milked and gave the shepherd to drink, then milked and drank. He said to him: By Allah, I have never seen the like of you; who are you? He said: If I tell you, will you conceal it for me? He said: Yes. He said: I am Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah. He said: Are you the one whom Quraysh claim has left the religion? He said: They say that. He said: I bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah, and none but a messenger could do what you did. Then he said to him: Shall I follow you? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Not today, but when you hear that we have prevailed, come to us. So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after he had appeared in Medina."
"16995","Qays ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah al-Ansari, then al-Khazraji, an Aqabi and a Badri. 875 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, in naming those of the Ansar who attended al-Aqabah, then from Banu Mazin ibn al-Najjar: Qays ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah, and he attended Badr; and the name of Abu Sa'sa'ah is Amr ibn Zayd ibn Awf ibn Mabdhul."
"16996","876 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham al-Sadusi narrated, Ziyad al-Bakka'i narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, in naming those of the Ansar who attended Badr, then of al-Khazraj, then of Banu Mazin ibn al-Najjar: Qays ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah; and the name of Abu Sa'sa'ah is Amr ibn Zayd ibn Awf ibn Mabdhul ibn Amr ibn Ghanm ibn Mazin ibn al-Najjar."
"16997","877 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated; and Abu al-Zinba' Ruh ibn al-Faraj and Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh, the two Egyptians, narrated to us; they said: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated, they said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Habban ibn Wasi' al-Ansari, from his father, from Qays ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, in how many days should I recite the Qur'an? He said: In fifteen. He said: I find myself stronger than that. He said: In a week. He said: I find myself stronger than that. He said: So he kept reciting it thus for a time until he grew old and would bind his eyes, then returned and would recite it in fifteen. He said: Would that I had accepted the first concession of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"16998","Qays ibn Kharashah al-Qaysi. 878 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih al-Misri narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, Harmalah ibn Imran narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that he heard him narrating to Muhammad ibn Abi Ziyad al-Thaqafi, saying: Qays ibn Kharashah and Ka'b, the two learned men, traveled together until when they reached Siffin, Ka'b stopped a while and said: There is no god but Allah; in this place there shall be shed of the blood of the Muslims what is not shed in any place on earth. Qays grew angry and said: How do you know, O Abu Ishaq? What is this? This is part of the unseen that Allah has reserved to Himself. Ka'b said: There is nothing on earth but that it is written in the Torah that Allah sent down to Musa: what will happen upon it and what will come forth in it until the Day of Resurrection. Muhammad ibn Yazid said: And who is Qays ibn Kharashah? He said: A man of Qays whom you do not know, a man from your land. He said: By Allah, I do not know him. He said: Qays ibn Kharashah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I pledge to you upon what came to you from Allah and upon speaking the truth. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Qays, if time extends you such that rulers after me rule you with whom you cannot speak the truth. Qays said: By Allah, I shall not pledge to you upon anything but that I fulfill it to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then nothing will harm you. He said: So Qays used to criticize Ziyad and his son Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad, so he sent to him saying: Are you the one who fabricates against Allah and His Messenger? He said: No, but if you wish I shall tell you who fabricates against Allah and His Messenger: one who abandons acting upon the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic, and the Sunnah of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"16999","Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah al-Ansari; from the reports of Qays. 879 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated, my father narrated to me, from Thumamah ibn Abd Allah ibn Anas, who said: The station of Qays ibn Sa'd with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was like the station of the chief of police with the commander."
"17000","880 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated, my father narrated to me, from Thumamah ibn Abd Allah ibn Anas, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Mecca, Qays ibn Sa'd was in his vanguard before him, in the station of the chief of police. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was spoken to concerning Qays, to move him from the position he had placed him in, fearing he might advance upon something, so he moved him from that."
"17001","881 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Rishdin ibn Sa'd narrated, my father narrated to me, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, Tha'labah ibn Abi Malik al-Qurazi reported to me, that Qays ibn Sa'd al-Ansari, who was the bearer of the banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), intended Hajj, so he combed one side of his head; then his sacrificial animal had been garlanded, so he entered ihram and undid the other side."
"17002","882 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated, Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Yarim ibn As'ad, who said: I was with Qays ibn Sa'd, who had served the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ten years. He performed ablution and wiped over his leather socks, and I shall never forget the trace of his finger upon the two socks because they were new."
"17003","883 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Sa'd ibn Ubadah divided his wealth among his sons in his lifetime, then a son was born to him after he died. Umar met Abu Bakr and said: I did not sleep tonight on account of the son of Sa'd, this newborn, for whom he left nothing. Abu Bakr said: By Allah, I did not sleep tonight either, because of him. Come with us to Qays ibn Sa'd to speak to him about his brother. So they came to him and spoke to him, and Qays said: As for anything Sa'd has concluded, I shall never reverse it, but I call you both to witness that my share is for him."
"17004","884 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, Ata reported to me, that Sa'd ibn Ubadah divided his wealth among his sons, then died while his wife was pregnant without his knowing of her pregnancy. She gave birth to a boy, so Umar and Abu Bakr sent concerning that to Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah. He said: As for a matter Sa'd divided and concluded, I shall never return to it, but my share is for him. I said: Did he divide according to the Book of Allah? He said: We do not find them dividing except according to the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic."
"17005","885 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, Amr ibn Dinar reported to me, that Dhakwan Abu Salih reported to him this report, the report of Qays ibn Sa'd, that he divided his wealth among his sons, then set out for Syria and died."
"17006","What Qays ibn Sa'd narrated. 886 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah narrated to me, from Abu Ammar, who said: We asked Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah about Zakat al-Fitr, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us with it before Zakat was revealed, then he neither commanded us nor forbade us, so we do it."
"17007","887 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abu Ammar, from Qays ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to fast Ashura before the month of Ramadan was revealed. When the month of Ramadan was revealed, he neither commanded us nor forbade us, and we do it. And he commanded us with Zakat al-Fitr; when Zakat was revealed, he neither commanded us nor forbade us, and we do it."
"17008","888 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Husayn ibn Numayr narrated, Ibn Abi Layla narrated, from al-Hakam, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah, who said: We were commanded to fast Ashura before Ramadan was revealed. When Ramadan was revealed, we were neither commanded with it nor forbidden from it, and we would fast it. And we were commanded with half a sa'; every person, free and slave, young and old, male or female. When Zakat was revealed, we were neither commanded with it nor forbidden from it, and we would give it."
"17009","889 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Waki' narrated, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn As'ad ibn Zurarah, from Muhammad ibn Shurahbil, from Qays ibn Sa'd, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, so we placed water for him, then he bathed. Then we brought him a sheet dyed with wars, and he wrapped himself in it, and it is as though I am looking at the trace of the wars dye upon his sides."
"17010","890 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated; and Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated; they said: Ali ibn Hashim ibn al-Burayr narrated, Ibn Abi Layla narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn As'ad ibn Zurarah, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Qays ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the house of Sa'd, stood at its door and greeted, and Sa'd returned it faintly. Then he greeted, and Sa'd returned it faintly. Then he greeted, and Sa'd returned it faintly. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned back returning, so Sa'd followed him hastening after him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransomed for you; by Allah, nothing prevented me from returning the greeting to you but to make you increase the greeting for us. He entered, water was placed to cool, and he bathed. He was brought a sheet dyed with wars and wore it; I saw the stain of the wars dye upon his sides. Then he came and sat and said: O Allah, bless the Ansar, and the offspring of the Ansar, and the offspring of the offspring of the Ansar. When he intended to leave, a donkey was brought, a velvet cloth placed upon it that was neither silk nor fine wool, and he sent his son with him to return the donkey. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Carry him in front of me. Sa'd said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I carry him in front of you? He said: Yes, he is most entitled to the front of his donkey. He said: O Messenger of Allah, the donkey is yours. He said: Carry him behind me."
"17011","891 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated, Husayn ibn Abd Allah ibn Dumayrah narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, from Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah ibn Daylam, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The owner of the mount is most entitled to its front."
"17012","892 - Habbush ibn Rizq Allah al-Misri narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Rabi' ibn Tariq narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah reported, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Mulayl, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Umayyah, that Habib ibn Maslamah came to Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah while he was upon a horse of his, and Habib withdrew for him from the saddle, saying to him: Ride. But Qays refused, and Qays said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The owner of the mount is most entitled to its front. Habib said: I am not ignorant of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, but I fear for you."
"17013","893 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Ziyad Sabalan narrated, Abbad ibn Abbad narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from Qays ibn Sa'd, who said: My father entrusted me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to serve him. He came upon me after I had prayed two rak'ahs, or he said the two rak'ahs, while I was lying down, so he nudged me with his foot and said: Shall I guide you to a gate of the gates of Paradise? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah."
"17014","894 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated, Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated, my father narrated, saying: I heard Mansur ibn Zadhdhan narrating from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah, who said: My father had entrusted me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to serve him. He came upon me after I had prayed two rak'ahs, so he struck me with his foot and said: Shall I guide you to a gate of the gates of Paradise? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah."
"17015","895 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated, Ishaq al-Azraq narrated, from Sharik, from Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, from Qays ibn Sa'd, who said: I came to al-Hirah and saw them prostrating to a marzuban of theirs, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, you are more entitled that we prostrate to you. He said: Do not do so; had I commanded that, I would have commanded women to prostrate to their husbands, because of the right Allah has placed upon them for them."
"17016","896 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, my father narrated, Amr ibn Muhammad al-Anqazi narrated, Isra'il narrated, from Jabir, from Amir al-Sha'bi, from Qays ibn Sa'd, who said: There was nothing in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but that I saw it, except that entertainment would be performed for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Eid; and taqlis means play."
"17017","897 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Rabi' ibn Tariq narrated, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated, from Ubayd Allah ibn Zahr, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My Lord has forbidden me wine, the drum, and singing slave girls. Then he said: Beware of ghubayra, for it is wine."
"17018","898 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, Ibn Hubayrah narrated to me, saying: I heard an old man of Himyar saying: Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah al-Ansari addressed us, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every intoxicant is wine, and every intoxicant is unlawful."
"17019","899 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Tahir ibn Khalid ibn Nizar narrated, my father narrated to me, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from Qays ibn Sa'd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The worst of usury is a man's indulging at length in reviling his brother, and the greatest of major sins is a man's reviling his parents. They said: How would he revile them, O Messenger of Allah? He said: He reviles a man, so he reviles them both."
"17020","900 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If faith were suspended from the Pleiades, men of Persia would attain it."
"17021","901 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from Qays ibn Sa'd, a narration like it."
"17022","902 - Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Safwan ibn Salih narrated, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, al-Awza'i narrated, saying: I heard Yahya ibn Abi Kathir saying: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn As'ad ibn Zurarah narrated to me, from Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited us in our house and said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah. Sa'd returned it faintly. Then he said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, and Sa'd returned it faintly. Then he said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, and Sa'd returned it faintly. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned back, so Sa'd followed him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, I was hearing your greeting and returning it to you faintly, so that you would increase the greeting upon us. His guest, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), left, and Sa'd ordered washing for him, so he washed and bathed. Then he, or the woman, handed him a sheet dyed with wars and saffron, and he wrapped himself in it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands, saying: O Allah, place Your blessings and mercy upon the family of Sa'd ibn Ubadah. Then he partook of the food. When he intended to leave, Sa'd brought near to him a donkey, placed a velvet cloth upon it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode. Sa'd said: O Qays, accompany the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Qays, ride. But I refused. Then he said: Either you ride or you turn back. So I turned back."
"17023","Qays ibn Abi Gharazah al-Ghifari, also said al-Juhani, also said al-Bajali. 903 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Mughirah, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us at the market and said: O company of merchants, oaths accompany this market, so mix it with charity."
"17024","904 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, Abu Kamil al-Jahdari narrated, Abu Awanah narrated, from al-Mughirah, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us to the market and said: O company of merchants, idle talk and oaths attend this market, so mix it with charity."
"17025","905 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated, Shu'bah reported, from Sulayman al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, who said: I heard Qays ibn Abi Gharazah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us at the market while we were called brokers, so he named us with a name better than what we named ourselves, saying: O company of merchants, oaths and idle talk mingle with this trade of yours, so mix it with charity."
"17026","906 - Hafs ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us while we were selling slaves in Medina, and we called ourselves brokers, so he named us with what is better than what we named ourselves, saying: O company of merchants, this trade is attended by idle trade talk and oaths, so mix it with charity."
"17027","907 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Jarir narrated, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"17028","908 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Waki' narrated, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, who said: From Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: We used to trade in Medina and called ourselves brokers, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and named us with a name better than what we named ourselves, saying: O company of merchants, this trade is attended by idle talk and oaths, so mix it with something of charity."
"17029","909 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Amr ibn Hakam narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Wa'il, who said: I heard Qays ibn Abi Gharazah; and Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, who said: I heard Abu Wa'il narrating from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were selling in the market, and we called ourselves brokers, so he said: O company of merchants, idle talk mingles with this market of yours, so mix it with charity, or with something of charity. The wording is that of the hadith of Ali ibn al-Ja'd."
"17030","910 - Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from al-Hajjaj, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, that they used to be called brokers."
"17031","911 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated, Firdaws ibn al-Ash'ari narrated, Mas'ud ibn Sulayman narrated, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were called brokers, so he said: O company of merchants, this trade is attended by idle talk and oaths, so mix it with charity."
"17032","912 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated; and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated; and Yusuf narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated; they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon the merchants while they were called by a name other than it, saying: O company of merchants, your trade is attended by idle talk and oaths, so mix it with something of charity. The wording is that of the hadith of Hajjaj."
"17033","913 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated; and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna and Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us; they said: Musaddad narrated, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were trading in the market, saying: O company of merchants. The people rejoiced and craned toward him. We were called brokers, and he was the first to name us merchants, saying: Satan and sin attend trade, so mix your trade with charity."
"17034","914 - Abu al-Zinba' Ruh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi narrated, Sufyan narrated, Jami' ibn Abi Rashid, Abd al-Malik ibn A'yan, and Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated; and Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud and Jami' ibn Abi Rashid, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us while we were trading at al-Aqiq, and we were called brokers, so he named us with a name better than what we named ourselves, saying: O company of merchants, this trade is attended by oaths and lying, so mix it with something of charity."
"17035","915 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ubadah ibn Ziyad al-Asadi narrated, Abu Maryam ibn Abd al-Ghaffar ibn al-Qasim narrated, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were called brokers, saying to us: O company of merchants, this trade of yours is attended by oaths and Satan, so mix it with charity."
"17036","916 - Ibrahim ibn Na'ilah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated, al-Nu'man ibn Abd al-Salam narrated, from Habib ibn Hassan, Shaqiq ibn Salamah narrated, Qays ibn Abi Gharazah narrated to me, who said: We used to trade in the market of Medina and call ourselves brokers, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and named us with the best name better than what we called ourselves, saying: O company of merchants, this trade is attended by idle talk and oaths, so mix it with something of charity, meaning mix it."
"17037","917 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yusuf al-Sayrafi narrated, Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated, from Ubaydah ibn Mu'attib, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"17038","918 - Ahmad ibn Ali al-Jarudi al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Rashid al-Adami narrated, Abu Bakr al-Kalbi narrated, Ubaydah ibn Mu'attib and Yazid al-Fakhm narrated, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: O company of merchants, and he mentioned the like of it."
"17039","919 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated, my father narrated, from Abu Hamzah, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: We were called brokers in Medina, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O company of merchants, trade is attended by oaths and lying, so mix it with charity."
"17040","920 - Ahmad ibn Ali al-Jarudi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Rashid al-Adami narrated, Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Habib ibn Zayd al-Ansari, from Abu Wa'il, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"17041","921 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated; they said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Mu'awiyah ibn Maysarah ibn Shurayh narrated, al-Hakam narrated, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man selling food and said: O owner of this food, is its bottom like its top? He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cheats the Muslims is not of them."
"17042","Qays ibn al-Harith al-Asadi, also said al-Harith ibn Qays. 922 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated, Hushaym reported, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Humaydah ibn al-Shamardal, from Qays ibn al-Harith, or al-Harith ibn Qays al-Asadi, who said: I accepted Islam while I had eight wives, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Choose four of them."
"17043","923 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, my father narrated, Jarir narrated; and Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated, Hushaym narrated; they said: al-Kalbi narrated, from Humaydah ibn al-Shamardal, from al-Harith ibn Qays ibn Umayrah, who said: I accepted Islam while I had eight wives, and they accepted Islam with me and emigrated with me. He said: Choose four of them. So I would say to the one I wished to keep: Come forward, and to the one I did not wish to keep: Go back."
"17044","Qays ibn A'idh Abu Kahil. 924 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me; and Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, my father narrated; and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated; they said: Waki' narrated, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from his brother, from Abu Kahil. Isma'il said: And I saw Abu Kahil, who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivering a sermon upon a she-camel with cropped ears, while an Abyssinian held its rein."
"17045","925 - Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated, Abu Usamah narrated, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from his brother, from Qays ibn A'idh, who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivering a sermon upon a she-camel with cropped ears, while an Abyssinian slave held its halter."
"17046","926 - Abd Allah ibn al-Husayn al-Missisi narrated to us, Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated, al-Haytham ibn Jammaz narrated; and al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, al-Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated, Uthman ibn Matar narrated, al-Haytham ibn Jammaz narrated, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Kahil, that he said: I passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was performing ablution, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has given us much good through you. He washed his hands, rinsed his mouth three times, sniffed water three times, washed his face three times and his forearms three times, wiped his head without specifying a number, and the tops of his feet without specifying a number, then said: O Abu Kahil, place the purification water in its places, keep the remainder of your purification water for your family, do not leave your family thirsty, and do not burden your servant."
"17047","927 - Sahl ibn Musa al-Shirazi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated, Sulayman ibn Karrar narrated, Sadaqah ibn Musa al-Daqiqi narrated, Nufay' ibn al-Harith narrated, from Abu Kahil, who said: Words occurred between two men of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until they struck one another. I met one of them and said: What is the matter between you and so-and-so? I heard him praising you well and making much supplication for you. I met the other and said to him the like of that. I kept walking between them until they made peace. I said: What have I done? I have ruined myself and made peace between them. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him the matter, saying: O Messenger of Allah, by the One Who sent you with the truth, I heard nothing from this one nor from that one. He said: O Abu Kahil, make peace between people even with such-and-such words I did not understand. I said: What did he mean by them? He said: He meant lying."
"17048","928 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Ma'mari narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated, Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Mu'addab narrated, al-Fadl ibn Ata narrated, from al-Fadl ibn Shu'ayb, from Abu Manzur, from Abu Mu'adh, from Abu Kahil, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Abu Kahil, shall I tell you of a decree Allah decreed upon Himself? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: May Allah give life to your heart and not cause it to die until your body dies. Know, O Abu Kahil, that the Lord of Might will never be angry with one in whose heart is fear, and the Fire will not consume even an eyelash of him. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever conceals his private parts out of modesty before Allah, secretly and openly, it is binding upon Allah to conceal his private parts on the Day of Resurrection. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever lets the sweetness of prayer enter his heart until he completes its bowing and prostration, it is binding upon Allah to please him on the Day of Resurrection. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever prays forty days and forty nights in congregation, catching the first takbir, it is binding upon Allah to write for him an acquittal from the Fire. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever fasts three days of every month along with the month of Ramadan, it is binding upon Allah to quench his thirst on the day of thirst. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever withholds his harm from people, it is binding upon Allah to withhold from him the torment of the grave. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever is dutiful to his parents, alive and dead, it is binding upon Allah to please him on the Day of Resurrection. I said: How is one dutiful to his parents when they are dead? He said: Dutifulness to them is to seek forgiveness for his parents, and not to revile anyone's parents lest they revile his parents. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever pays the zakat of his wealth when it falls due, it is binding upon Allah to make him among the companions of the prophets. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever has few good deeds and great evil deeds, it is binding upon Allah to make his scale heavy on the Day of Resurrection. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever strives for his wife, his child, and what his right hand possesses, establishing Allah's command among them and feeding them from the lawful, it is binding upon Allah to place him with the martyrs in their ranks. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever sends blessings upon me three times each day and three times each night, out of love for me and longing for me, it is binding upon Allah to forgive him his sins of that night and that day. Know, O Abu Kahil, that whoever testifies that there is no god but Allah alone, certain of it, it is binding upon Allah to forgive with each one the sins of a year."
"17049","Qays ibn al-Sa'ib ibn A'idh ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Umar ibn Mahrum, the partner of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Jahiliyyah. 929 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Khalid ibn Nizar narrated, Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, Mujahid narrated to me, saying: I heard Qays ibn al-Sa'ib saying: The month of Ramadan may be ransomed by a person, feeding a poor person for each day, so feed on my behalf a poor person a sa' for each day. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was my partner in the Jahiliyyah, the best of partners who neither disputes nor quarrels."
"17050","930 - Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Miqlas narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Wahb narrated, Yazid ibn Iyad reported to me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Ubayd, from Mujahid, that Qays ibn al-Sa'ib grew old until he passed sixty over one hundred and became too weak to fast, so he fed on its behalf."
"17051","931 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj narrated to us, Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated, Abu Malik al-Janbi Amr ibn Hashim narrated, Muslim al-Mula'i narrated, from Mujahid, who said: I used to lead my master Qays ibn al-Sa'ib, and he would say: Has the sun declined? When I said yes, he would pray Zuhr, saying: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Asr while the sun was white and alive, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Maghrib while the fasting person was still debating breaking the fast, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Fajr when light covered the sky."
"17052","Al-Nabighah al-Ja'di, whose name is Qays ibn Abd Allah and whose kunyah is Abu Layla. 932 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sallam al-Jumahi, who said: Abu Layla, the Nabighah of Banu Ja'dah, and he is Qays ibn Abd Allah ibn Ghadir ibn Rabi'ah ibn Udus ibn Ka'b ibn Rabi'ah ibn Amir ibn Sa'sa'ah."
"17053","933 - al-Husayn ibn Fahm al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Harun ibn Abi Bakr al-Zubayri narrated, Yahya ibn Harun al-Bahri narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from his uncle, from Abd Allah ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: A year of drought drove al-Nabighah of Banu Ja'dah out, so he came to Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr while he was sitting in Medina and recited to him in the mosque. Ibn al-Zubayr said: Come to me, Abu Layla, for poetry is the least of your requests with us. As for the choicest of our wealth, it is for the family of al-Zubayr, and as for its surplus, Banu Asad and Tamim preoccupy it from you. But you have two rights in Allah's wealth: a right for your having seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a right for your sharing with the people of Islam. Then he commanded concerning him, so he was admitted to the house of livestock, and he ordered for him seven young she-camels, a load, and horses, and loaded his mounts with wheat and dates. Al-Nabighah began hurrying, eating the grain unground. Ibn al-Zubayr said: Woe to Abu Layla! Hardship has reached him. Al-Nabighah said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: As long as Quraysh rule justly, show mercy when asked for mercy, fulfill covenants, and keep promises, I and the prophets shall be at the forefront of the swift."
"17054","Qays ibn Zayd. 934 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, Abu Imran al-Jawni narrated, from Qays ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced Hafsah with one divorce. Her maternal uncles Qudamah and Uthman, the sons of Maz'un, came to her, and she said: By Allah, he did not divorce me for satiety. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and entered, and she veiled herself. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril, peace be upon him, came to me and said: Take back Hafsah, for she is fasting and praying at night, and she is your wife in Paradise."
"17055","Qays ibn Zayd al-Juhani. 935 - Muhammad ibn Nuh ibn Harb al-Askari narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yazid al-Ahwazi narrated, Amir ibn Mudrik narrated, Jarir ibn Ayyub narrated, from al-Sha'bi, from Qays ibn Zayd al-Juhani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts a day voluntarily, a tree is planted for him in Paradise, its fruit smaller than the pomegranate and larger than the apple, its freshness like the freshness of honeycomb and its sweetness like the sweetness of honey; Allah shall feed the fasting person from it on the Day of Resurrection."
"17056","Qays Abu Muhammad. 936 - Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Rasibi narrated to us, Abu Maysarah al-Nahawandi narrated, Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from his father, from Uthman ibn Muhammad ibn Qays, who said: My father saw in my hand a whip without a strap, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: Make the strap of your whip good, for Allah is Beautiful and loves beauty."
"17057","Qays al-Tamimi. 936 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Hakim al-Awdi narrated, Talq ibn Ghannam narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated, from Jabir, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubayl, from Qays al-Tamimi, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a yellow garment, and I saw him greeting women."
"17058","Qays ibn Qahd al-Ansari, the grandfather of Yahya ibn Sa'id; there is disagreement over his father's name; also said Qays ibn Amr, also said Qays ibn Sahl. 937 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me; and Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated; they said: Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated, Sa'id narrated, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from Qays ibn Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man praying two rak'ahs after the dawn prayer, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is the dawn prayer twice? The man said: I had not prayed the two before it, so I prayed them now. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent."
"17059","938 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated; and Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Sa'd ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Qays ibn Sa'd, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was praying two rak'ahs after dawn, saying: What is this prayer? I said: I had not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, so these are they. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent."
"17060","939 - Ibrahim ibn Mattawayh al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn al-Walid ibn Burd al-Antaki narrated, Ayyub ibn Sahl narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that Qays ibn Sahl al-Ansari reported that he entered the mosque while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying, and he had not prayed the two rak'ahs, so he prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he finished his prayer, he stood and performed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him and said: O Qays, what are these two? He told him what he had done, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not disapprove of that."
"17061","Those named Qabisah. Qabisah ibn Mukhariq al-Hilali. 940 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, Abu Zafar Abd al-Salam ibn Mutahhar narrated, Nafi' ibn Abd Allah Abu Hurmuz narrated, saying: I heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrating from Ibn Abbas, who said: Qabisah ibn Mukhariq al-Hilali came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), greeted him, and he returned it and welcomed him. Then he said to him: What has brought you, O Qabisah? He said: O Messenger of Allah, my age has advanced, my skin has grown thin, my strength has weakened, I have grown feeble toward my family, and I am unable to do things I used to do, so teach me words by which Allah may benefit me, briefly. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Qabisah, say three times when you have prayed the dawn prayer: Glory be to Allah and with His praise, glory be to Allah the Mighty and with His praise, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah. If you say that, you will be safe, by Allah's permission, from blindness, leprosy, and vitiligo. And say: O Allah, guide me from Yourself, pour upon me of Your bounty, spread upon me of Your mercy, and send down upon me of Your blessings. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying them while Qabisah counted them upon his fingers."
"17062","941 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated, Awf narrated, from Hayyan, from Qatan ibn Qabisah, from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Divining with birds, drawing lines, and omens are part of sorcery."
"17063","942 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Awf, from Hayyan, from Qatan ibn Qabisah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Divining with birds, drawing lines, and omens are part of sorcery."
"17064","943 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Awf al-Abdi, from Hayyan, from Qatan ibn Qabisah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Divining with birds, drawing lines, and omens are part of polytheism."
"17065","944 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Awf, Hayyan narrated to me, from Qatan ibn Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Divining with birds, drawing lines, and omens are part of sorcery. Awf said: Divining is driving birds, and drawing lines is marking in the earth."
"17066","945 - Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Awf al-A'rabi, from Hayyan Abu al-Ala, from Qatan ibn Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, that his father said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Divining with birds, omens, and drawing lines are part of sorcery."
"17067","946 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq reported, Ma'mar reported, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah al-Adawi, who said: I was with Qabisah ibn Mukhariq when a group of his people came to him seeking his help in the marriage of a man among them, but he refused to give them anything, so they left him. Kinanah said: I said to him: You are the chief of your people, and they came asking you but you gave them nothing. He said: In this I give nothing; were he to bite a leather strap until it dries out, that would be better for him than asking people in such a case. I shall tell you about that: I undertook a blood-money obligation among my people, so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have undertaken an obligation among my people and have come to you to help me in it. He said: Rather, we shall bear it for you, O Qabisah, and pay it to them from the charity. Then he said: O Qabisah, begging has been forbidden except in one of three cases: a man struck by a calamity that sweeps away his wealth, so he asks until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood, then refrains; a man struck by need until three discerning men of his people testify that begging has become lawful for him, so he asks until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood, then refrains; and a man who undertakes a blood-money obligation, so he asks until he reaches it, then refrains. Whatever is besides that is unlawful earnings he eats unlawfully."
"17068","947 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, Harun ibn Ri'ab narrated, Kinanah ibn Nu'aym al-Adawi narrated to me, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq al-Hilali, who said: I undertook an obligation, so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him concerning it. He said: Stay, O Qabisah, until the charity comes to us and we give you from it. Then he said: O Qabisah, begging is not lawful except for one of three: a man who undertakes an obligation, so begging becomes lawful for him until he obtains it, then refrains; a man struck by poverty until three discerning men of his people say: Such-and-such has been struck by poverty, so begging has become lawful for him, until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood, or he said a soundness of livelihood. Besides these, begging, O Qabisah, is unlawful earnings its owner eats unlawfully."
"17069","948 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Sallam narrated; and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated; they said: Isma'il ibn Ullayah narrated; and Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Yahya Abu al-Khattab narrated, Hatim ibn Wardan narrated; and Muhammad ibn Ruzayq ibn Jami' al-Misri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Sadusi narrated, Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi narrated, all from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq al-Hilali, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to ask him concerning an obligation, and he said: Stay until the charity comes to us; either we grant you or we bear it for you. He said concerning it: Begging is not lawful except for three: a man who undertakes an obligation among people, so he asks concerning it until he discharges it, then refrains; a man struck by need until three discerning men of his people testify that what permits has befallen him, or he said: begging has become lawful for him, so it becomes lawful until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood or a soundness of livelihood, then refrains. Besides that of requests, O Qabisah, it is unlawful earnings its owner eats unlawfully. The wording is that of the hadith of Ibn Ullayah."
"17070","949 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym al-Adawi, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq al-Hilali, who said: I undertook an obligation on behalf of my people, so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have undertaken an obligation on behalf of my people, so help me in it. He said: Rather, we shall bear it for you, O Qabisah; it is for you in the charity when it comes. Then he said: O Qabisah, begging is not lawful except for one of three: a man who undertakes an obligation on behalf of his people intending reconciliation, so he asks, and when he reaches it he refrains; a man struck by poverty, so three discerning men of his people attest, and he asks until he obtains a sufficiency or soundness, then refrains. Besides that of begging, O Qabisah, it is unlawful earnings, twice."
"17071","950 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated, from Harun ibn Ri'ab; and Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Harun ibn Ri'ab; and al-Haytham ibn Khalid al-Sadusi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mansurzaj narrated, Abd al-Malik al-Juddi narrated, from Abu al-Yasa', and Shu'bah, from Harun ibn Ri'ab; and Salih ibn Muqatil al-Baghdadi narrated to us, my father narrated, Abu Hammam Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan narrated, from Ubayd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Anbari al-Qadi, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq, who said: I undertook an obligation, and he mentioned the like of it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"17072","951 - Abu Quraysh Muhammad ibn Jum'ah al-Quhustani narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Idris al-Harawi narrated, Khalid ibn al-Hayyaj ibn Bistam narrated, my father narrated, Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"17073","952 - Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Maqdisi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, al-Awza'i narrated, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"17074","953 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Hurayth ibn al-Sa'ib narrated, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"17075","954 - Ahmad ibn Abd Allah al-Bazzaz al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Zayd al-Madhari narrated, Amr ibn Asim al-Kilabi narrated, my grandfather Ubayd Allah ibn al-Wazi' narrated, from Harun ibn Ri'ab al-Asadi, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym al-Adawi, who said: We were with Qabisah ibn Mukhariq when a cousin of his came to him seeking his help in the dowry of his wife, and he promised to give him. Then Qabisah said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his help in an obligation, and he said: O Qabisah, begging is not lawful except for one of three. He mentioned the like of it."
"17076","955 - Abd Allah ibn Ali al-Jarudi al-Naysaburi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Hafs narrated, my father narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated, from al-Hajjaj, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, who said: I undertook an obligation, so I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"17077","956 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated; and Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated; they said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated, Sulayman al-Taymi narrated; and Yahya ibn Muhammad al-Hinna'i narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Mu'adh narrated, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated, from his father, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Zuhayr ibn Amr, and from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq al-Hilali, they said: When this verse came down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And warn your nearest kin, the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to a smooth tract of a mountain, climbed its highest stone, then said: O sons of Abd Manaf, I am a warner; my likeness and yours is like a man who saw the enemy and went to warn his family, fearing they would outrun him to his family, so he began crying: O my companions, O my companions, you are attacked, you are attacked."
"17078","957 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Imran al-Jarmi narrated, Unays ibn Sawwar al-Jarmi narrated, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Hilal ibn Amr, that Qabisah al-Hilali narrated to him that the sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in Medina, until the stars appeared to them, and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed two rak'ahs, prolonging the standing in them. When he finished his prayer he turned to the people and said: When you see anything of these signs, it is only a frightening from Allah, mighty and majestic; when you see them, pray like the most recent prayer you prayed."
"17079","958 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Mujahid ibn Musa narrated, Rayhan ibn Sa'id narrated, Abbad ibn Mansur narrated, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Hilal ibn Amir, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, who said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"17080","Qabisah ibn Waqqas al-Sulami. 959 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab, and Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Sawrah al-Baghdadi narrated to us; they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, Abu Hashim Umarah ibn Ammar, the companion of al-Za'faran, narrated, Salih ibn Ubayd narrated to me, from Qabisah ibn Waqqas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There shall be rulers over you after me who delay the prayers; they are for you and against them, so pray with them as long as they pray facing the qiblah with you."
"17081","Qabisah ibn Burmah al-Asadi. 960 - Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari narrated to us, Ali ibn Tibrakh narrated, Nusayr ibn Amr ibn Yazid ibn Qabisah ibn Burmah al-Asadi al-Kufi narrated to me, saying: I heard Burmah ibn Layth saying: I heard Qabisah ibn Burmah saying: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him saying: The people of kindness in this world are the people of kindness in the Hereafter, and the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter."
"17082","In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, and His blessings upon our master Muhammad and his family, and peace. Those named Qatadah. Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man al-Ansari, a Badri, kunyah Abu Uthman, also said Abu Umar. From his reports, and mention of his death. 1 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, in naming those of the Ansar who attended Badr, then from Banu Sawwad ibn Ka'b, whose name is Ka'b Zafar: Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man ibn Zayd."
"17083","2 - al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, in naming those of the Ansar who attended Badr, then from Banu Zafar: Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man."
"17084","3 - Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham al-Sadusi narrated, Ziyad ibn Abd Allah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, in naming those of the Ansar who attended Badr, then of al-Aws, then of Banu Zafar: Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man ibn Zayd ibn Amir ibn Sawwad ibn Ka'b ibn Zafar ibn al-Khazraj ibn Amr ibn al-Aws."
"17085","4 - Abu al-Zinba' Ruh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated, saying: Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, kunyah Abu Uthman, died in the year twenty-three; Umar ibn al-Khattab prayed over him; his age was sixty-five years. Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, Muhammad ibn Maslamah, and al-Harith ibn Hazmah, also said Khazmah, descended into his grave."
"17086","What Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man narrated. Abu Sa'id al-Khudri from Qatadah. 5 - Zakariyya ibn Hamdawayh al-Saffar narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn Abu Ja'far narrated, from Abd Allah ibn Khabbab, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had forbidden eating the meat of sacrifices beyond three days. I went out on a journey and came to my family, and there was the woman with a pot in it dried meat she had brought me. I said to her: Where did you get this dried meat? She said: From sacrifices. We said: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not forbid us to eat them beyond three? She said: He has permitted eating them. I was not satisfied with her word until I went to my brother Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man and asked him about that, and he sent to me: Eat your food, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted that. So I ate it."
"17087","6 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Khuza'i al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bukayr al-Hadrami narrated, Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Dimashqi narrated, Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah narrated, Iyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh narrated to me, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever fasts the day of Arafah is forgiven a year before it and a year after it."
"17088","7 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, Abu al-Zubayr narrated, from Zubayd, that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri reported to him that he came to his family from a journey and found with them a bowl of tharid of sacrificial meat, but refused to eat it. He came to Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people at the time of Hajj: I had commanded you not to eat sacrificial meat beyond three days so that people would be satiated, but I now permit it to you, so eat as you wish."
"17089","8 - Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated, Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever fasts the day of Arafah is forgiven a year before it and a year after it."
"17090","Umar ibn Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man from his father. 9 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated, Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Kathir narrated, Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah reported to me, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from his father, from his grandfather Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who said: There was a night of intense darkness and rain, and I said: If only I would seize this night to attend the night prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I did so. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he saw me, with a palm stalk in his hand he was walking upon, saying: What brings you here, O Qatadah, at this hour? I said: I seized the opportunity to attend prayer with you, O Messenger of Allah. He gave me the palm stalk and said: Satan has stayed behind you among your family, so go with this palm stalk and hold it until you reach your house; take it from behind the house and strike him with the palm stalk. I left the mosque, and the palm stalk lit up like a candle with light, and I used it for light. I came to my family and found them asleep. I looked in the corner, and there was a hedgehog. I kept striking it with the palm stalk until it left."
"17091","10 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Had, from Ja'far ibn Abd Allah ibn Aslam, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from his father, from his grandfather, that he spoke against Quraysh as though he disparaged them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gently, O Qatadah; do not revile Quraysh, for soon there shall be seen among them men beside whose deeds you will deem your deed insignificant and your action insignificant beside their actions. Were Quraysh not to transgress, I would tell them what they have with Allah."
"17092","11 - al-Walid ibn Hammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl ibn Asim narrated, from Umar ibn Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man ibn Zayd al-Ansari, my father al-Fadl narrated to me, from his father Asim, from his father Umar, from his father Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man ibn Zayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah sent down to me Jibril in the finest form in which he would come to me, saying: Peace conveys peace to you, O Muhammad, saying: I have inspired the world to be bitter and turbid, narrow and hard upon My allies until they love meeting Me, and to be easy, spacious, and pleasant for My enemies until they hate meeting Me. I have made it a prison for My allies and a paradise for My enemies."
"17093","12 - al-Walid ibn Hammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl narrated, my father narrated to me, from his father Asim, from his father Umar, from his father Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who said: A bow was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he handed it to me on the day of Uhud. I shot with it before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until it broke at its tip. I did not leave my station facing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meeting the arrows with my face; whenever an arrow of them veered toward the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I turned my head to shield the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), without shooting back. The last of them was an arrow by which my eyeball protruded upon my cheek, and the crowd dispersed. I took my eyeball in my palm and hastened with it in my palm to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw it in my palm, his eyes filled with tears, saying: O Allah, Qatadah has shielded Your Prophet with his face, so make it his finest eye and sharpest in sight. And it became his finest eye and sharpest in sight."
"17094","13 - al-Walid ibn Hammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl narrated, my father narrated to me, from his father Asim, from his father Umar, from his father Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who said: I was facing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, shielding the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with my face, while Abu Dujanah Simak ibn Kharashah shielded the back of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his back in the battle of Uhud until his back was filled with arrows; that was on the day of Uhud."
"17095","14 - al-Walid ibn Hammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl narrated, my father narrated to me, from his father Asim, from his father Umar, from his father Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Uhud: Who will take this sword with its right? Ali stood and said: I, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Sit down, so he sat. Then he said a second time: Who will take this sword with its right? Abu Dujanah stood the second time, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed him his sword Dhu al-Faqar. Abu Dujanah stood, tied a red band over his eyes, raised his eyebrows from his eyes out of pride, then walked before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the sword."
"17096","15 - Abu Shu'ayb Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Shu'ayb narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from his father, from his grandfather Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who said: There was a household called Banu Ubayriq: Bishr, Bushayr, and Mubashshir. Bushayr was a hypocrite who would compose poetry satirizing the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then attribute it to some Arab, saying: So-and-so said such, and so-and-so said such. When the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard that poetry they would say: By Allah, none but the vile one says this poetry. He would say: Whenever a man says a poem they say Ibn Ubayriq said it. They were a household of poverty and need in the Jahiliyyah and Islam. The people's food in Medina was only dates and barley; when a man had means he would eat wheat. Food came from Syria, so my uncle Rifa'ah ibn Zayd bought a load of fine flour and placed it in a store chamber of his; in the chamber were weapons of his, two coats of mail, their two swords, and what is worn with them. He was raided at night, the chamber was breached, and the food and weapons were taken. My uncle Rifa'ah came to me and said: Nephew, you know that we were raided this night of ours; the chamber was breached, and our food and weapons were taken. We searched in the quarter and asked, and it was said: We saw Banu Ubayriq kindling fire this night, and we think it is only over some of your food. Banu Ubayriq would say while we were asking in the quarter: By Allah, we think your man is none but Labid ibn Sahl, a man of ours of righteousness and Islam. When Labid heard that he drew his sword and said: Do I steal? By Allah, this sword shall mix with you or you shall clarify this theft. They said: Away from us, man; by Allah, you are not its owner. We asked in the quarter until there was no doubt they were its owners. My uncle said to me: Nephew, if only you would go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mention that to him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, a harsh household of ours deliberately raided my uncle Rifa'ah ibn Zayd, breached a chamber of his, and took his weapons and food; let our weapons be returned to us; as for the food, we have no need of it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall look into that. When Banu Ubayriq heard that, they came to a man of theirs called Usayd ibn Urwah and spoke to him concerning that, and thirty of the quarter gathered for him. They came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man and his uncle have deliberately accused a household of ours, people of Islam and righteousness, of theft without proof or certainty. Qatadah said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and spoke to him, and he said: Have you deliberately accused a people of noted Islam and righteousness of theft? I returned, wishing I had given up some of my wealth and not spoken to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that. My uncle Rifa'ah came to me and said: Nephew, what did you do? I told him what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me, and he said: Allah is the One Whose help is sought. We had not waited long before the Qur'an came down: Indeed, We have sent down to you the Book in truth that you may judge between people by what Allah has shown you, and do not be an advocate for the treacherous, Banu Ubayriq; and seek Allah's forgiveness, that is for what you said to Qatadah; indeed, Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. And do not argue for those who betray themselves, Banu Ubayriq; indeed, Allah does not love one who is treacherous, sinful. They hide from people but do not hide from Allah, while He is with them when they plot by night what He does not approve of speech; and Allah encompasses what they do. Here you are, you have argued for them in the life of this world, but who will argue with Allah for them, the verse, to His saying: Forgiving, Merciful, that is, had they sought Allah's forgiveness He would have forgiven them. And whoever earns sin earns it only against himself; and Allah is Knowing, Wise. And whoever earns a fault or sin then casts it upon an innocent has borne slander and manifest sin, their saying concerning Labid. But for Allah's bounty upon you and His mercy, a party of them would have sought to mislead you, but they mislead only themselves and harm you not at all, to the end of the verse. When the Qur'an came down, the weapons were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he returned them to Rifa'ah. Qatadah said: When I brought the weapons to my uncle, he was an old man who had gone blind in the Jahiliyyah, and I used to consider his Islam tainted. When I brought him the weapons he said: Nephew, it is in the path of Allah. I knew his Islam was sound. When the Qur'an came down, Bushayr joined the polytheists and alighted upon Sulafah bint Sa'id ibn Suhayl. Allah sent down concerning him: Whoever opposes the Messenger after guidance has become clear to him and follows other than the way of the believers, We shall turn him to what he has turned to and roast him in Hell, an evil destination. Indeed, Allah does not forgive association with Him and forgives less than that for whom He wills; whoever associates with Allah has strayed far. When he alighted upon Sulafah bint Sa'id he cast verses of poetry at her, so she took his baggage, placed it upon her head, went out with it, and cast it in al-Batha. He blew upon a people of them to steal their goods, so Allah cast a rock upon him and it became his grave."
"17097","16 - Ahmad ibn Sahl ibn Ayyub al-Ahwazi narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Harish narrated, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated, Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated, Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray dawn in the light, for it is greater in reward."
"17098","Mahmud ibn Labid from Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man. 17 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated, Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated, Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated, from Umarah ibn Urwah, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah loves a servant He protects him from the world as one of you keeps protecting his sick one with water."
"17099","Ubayd ibn Hunayn from Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man. 18 - Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Nawfali, Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri, and Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us; they said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated, saying: Muhammad ibn Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated, from his father, from Sa'id ibn al-Harith, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, who said: While I was sitting, Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man came to me and said to me: Come with us, O Ibn Hunayn, to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, for I have been told he has fallen ill. We went to Abu Sa'id and found him reclining, raising his right foot over his left. We greeted and sat. Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man raised his hand to the foot of Abu Sa'id and pinched it severely. Abu Sa'id said: Glory be to Allah! Son of Adam, you have hurt me. He said to him: That is what I intended. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah, mighty and majestic, finished creation He reclined and placed one of His feet over the other, saying: None of My creation should do this. Abu Sa'id said: Certainly not; by Allah, I shall never do it again."
"17100","Iyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Sarh from Qatadah. 19 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Khuza'i al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bukayr al-Hadrami narrated, Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh, from Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, who said: I went out one dark night, saying: If only I would go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and attend prayer with him and keep him company. I did so. When I entered the mosque the sky flashed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me and said: O Qatadah, what has roused you? I said: I intended, may my father and mother be ransomed for you, to keep you company. He said: Take this palm stalk and fortify yourself with it; when you go out it shall light for you ten cubits before you and ten behind you. Then he said: When you enter your house, strike with it like the rough stone upon the dweller of the house, for that is Satan. He said: I went out and it lit for me, then I struck like the rough stone until it left my house."
"17101","Qatadah Abu Hashim al-Rahawi. 20 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Waqid al-Harrani narrated, Qatadah ibn al-Fadl ibn Qatadah al-Rahawi narrated, from his father, the uncle of my father Hashim ibn Qatadah al-Rahawi narrated to me, from his father, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and accepted Islam. He said to me: O Qatadah, wash with water and lote leaves, and shave off the hair of disbelief. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would command whoever accepted Islam to be circumcised, and he was eighty years old."
"17102","21 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Waqid al-Harrani narrated, Qatadah ibn al-Fadl al-Rahawi narrated, from his father, from the uncle of his father Hisham ibn Qatadah, from Qatadah ibn A'ish al-Jurashi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The servant shall remain in an expanse of his religion as long as he does not drink wine; when he drinks it, Allah tears away His covering from him, and Satan becomes his guardian, his hearing, his sight, and his leg, driving him to every evil and turning him from every good."
"17103","22 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Ali ibn Bahr narrated, Qatadah ibn al-Fadl narrated, my father al-Fadl ibn Abd Allah ibn Qatadah narrated to me, from his uncle Hisham ibn Qatadah, from his father Qatadah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed me over my people, I took his hand and bade him farewell, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah make piety your provision, forgive your sin, and direct you to good wherever it is."
"17104","Qatadah ibn Milhan al-Qaysi, of Banu Qays ibn Tha'labah. 23 - Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi and Zakariyya ibn Hamdawayh narrated to us; they said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated; and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated; and Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated; and Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn al-Mundhir al-Qazzaz narrated to us, Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi narrated; and al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Sawrah al-Baghdadi, and Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us; they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated; they said: Hammam ibn al-Harith narrated, Anas ibn Sirin narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Qatadah ibn Milhan al-Qaysi, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would command us to fast the white days: the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth, saying: It is the fast of a lifetime. Their wording is one."
"17105","24 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Yazid ibn Harun narrated, Shu'bah reported, from Anas ibn Sirin, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Minhal, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he would command us to fast the white days, the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth, saying: The fast of a lifetime, or like the fast of a lifetime. Thus Shu'bah narrated it from Anas, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Minhal, from his father, erring in it; the correct is the hadith of Hammam."
"17106","Those named Qutbah. Qutbah ibn Malik al-Tha'labi. 25 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated, Mis'ar narrated, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from his uncle, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in Fajr: And tall palm trees."
"17107","26 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the first rak'ah of the Fajr prayer: And tall palm trees with arranged clusters."
"17108","27 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated; and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated; and Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, Abu Amr al-Hawdi narrated; they said: Shu'bah narrated, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, who said: I heard my uncle, that he prayed the Fajr prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he recited: And tall palm trees."
"17109","28 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated, Za'idah narrated, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik, who said: I prayed Fajr with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he recited in the first rak'ah: Qaf, by the Glorious Qur'an."
"17110","29 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Shayban Abu Mu'awiyah and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik, who said: I prayed Fajr with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he recited: And tall palm trees."
"17111","30 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the dawn prayer: {Qaf. By the Glorious Quran}, and when he reached {and tall date-palms with arranged clusters}, I said to myself: how does he carry it on?"
"17112","31 - Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the dawn prayer: {and tall date-palms with arranged clusters}."
"17113","32 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Imran ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Sawwar, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from his uncle who said: I prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him reciting: {and tall date-palms with arranged clusters}."
"17114","33 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Sharik and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the dawn prayer: {and tall date-palms with arranged clusters}."
"17115","34 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik who said: I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the morning prayer, and he recited {Qaf. By the Glorious Quran} until he reached {and tall date-palms with arranged clusters}, and I kept repeating it, not knowing what he said."
"17116","35 - Muhammad ibn al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Abi Thawr al-Hamdani narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the dawn prayer in the first rakah: {Qaf}."
"17117","36 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us. And Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us - both said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, Misar narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from his uncle Qutbah ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, keep me away from evil morals, evil deeds, evil desires and evil diseases."
"17118","Qutbah ibn Qatadah al-Sadusi; what Qutbah ibn Qatadah narrated. 37 - Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani and Ahmad ibn al-Husayn ibn Nasr ibn al-Hadhdha al-Baghdadi narrated to us, both said: Shabab al-Usfuri narrated to us, Awn ibn Kahmas ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, Imran ibn Hudayr narrated to us, a man called Muqatil narrated to us, from Qutbah ibn Qatadah al-Sadusi who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, stretch out your hand so I may pledge allegiance to you on behalf of myself and my daughter al-Huwaysilah, and if you were to lie against Allah He would deceive you. He said: And Khalid ibn al-Walid charged against us with his cavalry, so we said: We are Muslims! So he left us, and we raided al-Ubullah with him and divided it and filled our hands."
"17119","38 - Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sahl ibn Muhammad al-Sukkari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Thalabah ibn Sawa narrated to us, my uncle Muhammad ibn Sawa narrated to me, Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, from Qatadah, from a man of Banu Sadus, from Qutbah ibn Qatadah who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) breaking his fast when the sun had set."
"17120","Those named Qurrah; Qurrah ibn Iyas al-Muzani; Qatadah ibn Diamah from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah from his father. 39 - Ubayd ibn Khalaf al-Qatii narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Muslim ibn Qutaybah narrated to us. And Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Mudrik narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us - both said: Harun ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: We were forbidden from praying between the pillars in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"17121","40 - Muhammad ibn al-Walid ibn Salih al-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Muslim - a shaykh who used to be in the mosque of Hammam - narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: We were driven away in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from praying between the columns."
"17122","Urwah ibn Abdullah ibn Qushayr from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 41 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Ghassan and Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Imran al-Huddani narrated to us, my father narrated to us - they said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Urwah ibn Abdullah ibn Qushayr narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Qurrah narrated to us, from his father who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a group of Muzaynah and we pledged allegiance to him, and I put my hand inside the opening of his shirt and touched the Seal (of prophethood). Urwah said: I never saw Muawiyah nor his son in winter or summer except with their waist-wrappers loose, never buttoning them. And the wording is that of the hadith of Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"17123","Ziyad al-Jassas from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 42 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Nuaym al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yazid narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad al-Jassas narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and I touched the Seal between his shoulders and kissed his hands, then I came, and he said: From whom are you? I said: From Muzaynah."
"17124","43 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Nuaym al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad al-Jassas, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about vessels, so he said: Vessels do not forbid anything, so make nabidh in whatever seems fit to you, and avoid every intoxicant."
"17125","Abdullah ibn al-Mukhtar and Hajjaj al-Aswad from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 44 - Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Mahan ibn Abi Hanifah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hajjaj al-Aswad and Abdullah ibn al-Mukhtar, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, if a sheep lies down for me to slaughter it and I feel mercy for it? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And the sheep, if you show mercy to it, Allah will show mercy to you."
"17126","Ziyad ibn Mikhraq from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 45 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us. And Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us. And Abdan ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us - they said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Mikhraq, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father that a man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I slaughter a sheep and I feel mercy for it! He said: And the sheep, if you show mercy to it, Allah will show mercy to you."
"17127","46 - Abu al-Ghayth Bishr ibn Ali al-Qummi al-Antaki - from Lakhm - narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Hurr al-Antaki narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Isa al-Tabba narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ziyad ibn Mikhraq, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I slaughter the sheep while I feel mercy for it! He said: And the sheep, if you show mercy to it, Allah will show mercy to you."
"17128","Yunus ibn Ubayd from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 47 - Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I take the sheep to slaughter it and I feel mercy for it! He said: And the sheep, if you show mercy to it, Allah will show mercy to you."
"17129","Khalid ibn Abi Karimah from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 48 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Buhlul al-Kufi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, Khalid ibn Abi Karimah narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent his father, the grandfather of Muawiyah, to a man who had married his father's wife, and he struck his neck and took a fifth of his property."
"17130","Qurrah ibn Khalid al-Sadusi from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 49 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Qurrah ibn Khalid, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Show me the Seal! So I put my hand inside the opening of his shirt until I placed my hand upon it."
"17131","50 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, both said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and I put my hand in his jubban (collar opening), and I did not stop supplicating for me while I was touching the Seal, and I saw upon his shoulders something like a silaah (a lump), the Seal of Prophethood."
"17132","Bistam ibn Muslim al-Awdhi from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 51 - Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Bistam ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: We spent a period of our time with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and we had no food except the two black ones: dates and water."
"17133","52 - Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Muadh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Bistam ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: O my son, if you are in a gathering whose good you hope for and a need hastens you, and you say: Peace be upon you - then you are their partner in what they attain in that gathering."
"17134","Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 53 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting the white days is like fasting the lifetime, and breaking it."
"17135","54 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father that a man used to come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and with him was a son of his, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you love him? He said: May Allah love you as I love him! Then his son died, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is so-and-so? They said: O Messenger of Allah, his son has died! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Would it not please you that you not come to any gate of Paradise but he comes to open it for you? They said: O Messenger of Allah, is it for him specially or for all of us? He said: No, rather for all of you. And the wording is that of the hadith of Amr ibn Marzuq."
"17136","55 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us - they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah who said: I heard my father narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There will remain people of my ummah victorious, those who abandon them will not harm them until the Hour comes."
"17137","56 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from Shubah, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the people of Sham become corrupt, there is no good in you."
"17138","57 - Abdan ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, Shabbabah ibn Sawwar informed us. And Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Shabbabah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, from Shubah, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped his head and sought forgiveness for him."
"17139","58 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he had milked and tied the udder."
"17140","59 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Sahl ibn Hammad Abu Attab al-Dallal narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: Ibn Masud was upon a tree gathering from it for them, then the wind blew and uncovered his legs, so they laughed at the thinness of his legs, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, they are both heavier in the Balance on the Day of Resurrection than Uhud."
"17141","60 - Talib ibn Qurrah al-Adhani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isa al-Tabba narrated to us, Abu Sufyan al-Mamari narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of Aishah over women is like the excellence of tharid over all other food."
"17142","Hammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Maliki from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 61 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Umar ibn Yazid al-Shaybani narrated to us, Hammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Maliki narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah who said: My father said: If you pass by a gathering, greet its people, for if they are in good, you are their partner, and if they are in other than that, you have reward; thus I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say."
"17143","Iyas ibn Muawiyah ibn Qurrah from his father from his grandfather. 62 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Zakariya al-Abdasi narrated to us, Fudayk ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Khalifah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father, from his grandfather who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says a takbir at sunset upon the seashore raising his voice, Allah gives him in reward for every drop in the sea ten good deeds, and wipes from him ten evil deeds, and raises for him ten degrees, between each two degrees is a journey of one hundred years on a swift horse."
"17144","63 - Abdullah ibn Wuhayb al-Ghazzi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani narrated to us, Bakr ibn Bishr al-Tirmidhi narrated to us - and he was our imam at Asqalan, he died in the year 182 - Abd al-Hamid ibn Sawwar narrated to me, Iyas ibn Muawiyah ibn Qurrah narrated to me, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather Qurrah who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and modesty was mentioned in his presence, so they said: O Messenger of Allah, is modesty part of religion? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather it is the whole religion. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Modesty, chastity, and reticence - reticence of the tongue, not reticence of the heart - and deeds are part of faith, and they increase in the Hereafter and decrease from this world, and what they increase in the Hereafter is more than what they decrease in this world; and miserliness and obscenity are part of hypocrisy, and they increase in this world and decrease in the Hereafter, and what they decrease in the Hereafter is more than what they increase in this world."
"17145","al-Fudayl ibn Talhah from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 64 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Said ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Karabisi narrated to us, al-Furat ibn Abi al-Furat narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Fudayl ibn Talhah narrating from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: I set out with my father to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and we found him with his waist-wrapper loosened, so he turned me from behind him and his hand fell upon the Seal."
"17146","Khalid ibn Maysarah from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 65 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Suwayd al-Dari narrated to us, Said ibn Sallam al-Attar narrated to us, Khalid ibn Maysarah narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of these two trees, let him not approach us in our mosque, and if you must eat them, kill them by cooking."
"17147","66 - Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Missisi narrated to us, Harun ibn Zayd ibn Abi al-Zarqa narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Maysarah who said: I heard Muawiyah ibn Qurrah narrating from his father who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sat, a group of his Companions would sit with him, and among them was a man who had a small son who would come to him from behind his back and sit in front of him, until the boy died, so the man refrained from attending the circle, remembering Allah and grieving for him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) missed him and said: Why do I not see so-and-so? They said: O Messenger of Allah, his little son whom you saw has died, and that kept him from attending the circle! So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met him and asked him about him, and he was informed that he had died, so he consoled him for him, then said: O so-and-so, which was more beloved to you: to enjoy him throughout your life, or not to come tomorrow to any gate of Paradise but you find him having preceded you to it, opening it for you? He said: O Prophet of Allah, rather that he precede me to the gates of Paradise and open them for me is more beloved to me! He said: Then that is yours. So a man of the Ansar stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom, is this for so-and-so specially, or for whoever of the Muslims loses a farat (a young child who predeceases)? He said: Rather, whoever of the Muslims loses a farat, that is his."
"17148","Shabib ibn Muhammad ibn Wasi from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 67 - Abdan ibn Ahmad and Jafar ibn Ahmad ibn Sinan al-Wasiti narrated to us, both said: Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim al-Anbari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated to us, al-Azhar ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Shabib ibn Muhammad ibn Wasi, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father Qurrah who said: I went to embrace Islam when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent, and I wanted to bring two or three men with me into Islam, so I came to the water where the people gathered, and there I was with the shepherd of the village who grazed their sheep, and he said: I will not graze your sheep for you. They said: Why? He said: The wolf comes every night and takes a sheep, while this idol of ours stands, neither harming nor benefiting, neither changing nor disapproving! So they turned back, and I hoped they would embrace Islam. When morning came, the shepherd came running, saying: Glad tidings have come, glad tidings have come; the wolf has come and it lies bound before the idol! So I went with them, and they kissed it and prostrated to it, and said: Do thus! So I entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and narrated this hadith to him, and he said: Satan played with them."
"17149","al-Muhabbar ibn Qahdham from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 68 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad Nizak narrated to us. And Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Azdi narrated to us - both said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar ibn Qahdham narrated to us, my father al-Muhabbar ibn Qahdham narrated to me, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earth will be filled with wrongdoing and injustice as it was filled with equity and justice, until Allah sends a man from me whose name is my name and whose father's name is my father's name, and he will fill it with equity and justice as it was filled with wrongdoing and injustice. He will remain among you seven or eight years; if more, then nine. Neither the sky will withhold its rain nor the earth anything of its vegetation."
"17150","Salm ibn Kathir from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah. 69 - Abdan ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Ismah al-Jazari informed us, Bishr ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Salim ibn Kathir, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever is attended by death and places his will in accordance with the Book of Allah, that is an expiation for what he neglected of zakat in his life."
"17151","Qurrah ibn Hubayrah al-Qushayri. 70 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Khallal al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Umar ibn Abd al-Wahid narrated to us - both from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, a shaykh on the coast narrated to us, from a man of Banu Qushayr called Qurrah ibn Hubayrah, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: We had lords and ladies whom we worshipped besides Allah, so we called upon them and they did not answer, and we asked them and they did not give, so we came to you and Allah has guided us through you, and we worship Allah! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Successful is he who is granted understanding! He said: O Messenger of Allah, clothe me with two garments of yours that you have worn - so he clothed him. When he was at the standing place at Arafat, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Repeat to me your statement. So he repeated it to him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Successful is he who is granted understanding!"
"17152","Qurrah ibn Dumuṣ Numayri, and he is Qurrah ibn Dumuṣ ibn Rabiah ibn Awf ibn Muawiyah ibn Qartha ibn al-Harith ibn Numayr ibn Amir. 71 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us, Musa ibn Ismail Abu Salamah narrated to us, Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, who said: An old man sat with us in the shop of Ayyub wearing a woolen jubbah, and he heard people talking, so he said: My master narrated to me from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I said: Then name him! He said: Qurrah ibn Dumuṣ al-Numayri! He said: I came to Madinah and came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and with him were people, and I kept wanting to draw near to him but could not, so I called out to him: O Messenger of Allah, seek forgiveness for the Numayri lad! He said: May Allah forgive you! He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent al-Dahhak ibn Qays as a collector over my people, and when he returned he came with mature camels, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You went to Hilal ibn Amir and Numayr ibn Amir and Amir ibn Rabiah and took the best of their wealth! He said: O Messenger of Allah, I heard you mentioning raiding, so I wanted to bring you camels to ride and carry loads upon! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What you left is more beloved to me than what you took; return them and take their charities from the edges of their wealth! He said: So I heard the Muslims calling those camels the mujahidat (the striving ones)."
"17153","Those named Qabath; Qabath ibn Ashyam al-Laythi, and he is Qabath ibn Ashyam ibn Amir ibn al-Malmuh ibn Yamar ibn Awf ibn Kab ibn Amir ibn Layth ibn Bakr ibn Abd ibn Kinanah ibn Qabath ibn Khuzaymah ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar ibn Nizar. 72 - Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi narrated to us, Asbagh ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his grandfather Aban, from his father Sulayman who said: The Islam of Qabath ibn Ashyam al-Laythi was that a man of his people and others of the Arabs came to him and said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Muttalib has come out calling to a religion other than our religion! So Qabath stood until he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when he entered upon him he said to him: Sit, O Qabath! So Qabath was alarmed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Are you the one who says: If the women of Quraysh came out with their baskets they would turn back Muhammad and his Companions? So Qabath said: By the One Who sent you with the truth, my tongue did not move with it, nor did my lips murmur it, nor did anyone hear it from me; it was nothing but something that occurred in my mind. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah! He said: And that what you have come with is truth."
"17154","73 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Yunus ibn Sayf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Qabath ibn Ashyam al-Laythi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The prayer of two men, one of them leading the other, is purer with Allah than the prayer of four separately, and the prayer of four led by one of them is purer with Allah than the prayer of eight separately, and the prayer of eight led by one of them is purer with Allah than the prayer of a hundred separately."
"17155","74 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Yunus ibn Sayf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Qabath ibn Ashyam that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer of two men, one of them leading the other, is purer with Allah than the prayer of four separately, and the prayer of four led by one of them is purer with Allah than the prayer of eight separately, and the prayer of eight led by one of them is purer with Allah than the prayer of a hundred separately. Ishaq ibn Rahawayh said: Qabath met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"17156","75 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Thabit al-Zuhri narrated to us, from al-Zubayr ibn Musa, from Abi al-Huwayrith who said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan saying to Qabath ibn Ashyam al-Laythi: O Qabath, are you older or is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is greater than me, and I am older than him in age; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was born in the Year of the Elephant, and he received prophethood at the head of forty years after the Elephant."
"17157","Those named Qudamah; Qudamah ibn Mazun al-Jumahi, a Badri. 76 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr: concerning the naming of those who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Quraysh, then of Banu Jumah: Qudamah ibn Mazun."
"17158","Qudamah ibn Abdullah al-Kilabi. 77 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq. And Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, from Ayman ibn Nabil, from Qudamah ibn Abdullah who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoning Jamrat al-Aqabah upon a reddish she-camel, with no beating, no driving, and no 'away, away'."
"17159","78 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from Ayman ibn Nabil, from Qudamah ibn Abdullah who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoning Jamrat al-Aqabah upon a reddish she-camel of his, with no beating, no driving, and no 'away, away'."
"17160","79 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to me, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Ayman ibn Nabil narrated to us, from Qudamah ibn Abdullah al-Kilabi who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoning the jamrah from the bottom of the valley."
"17161","80 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, and Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Surayj ibn Yunus and Mahmud ibn Awf and Abbad ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Qurran ibn Tammam narrated to us, from Ayman ibn Nabil, from Qudamah ibn Abdullah ibn Amir al-Amiri who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) circumambulating the House upon a she-camel and touching the Corner with his mihjan (crooked staff). And this is the wording of Abbad ibn Musa."
"17162","81 - Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Aqil narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi narrated to us - both said: Yaqub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri narrated to us, Arif ibn Ibrahim al-Thaqafi narrated to me, Humayd ibn Kilab narrated to us, who said: I heard my uncle Qudamah al-Kilabi say: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivering a sermon on the evening of Arafah wearing a hibarah hullah (a Yemeni striped garment)."
"17163","Those named Qarazah; Qarazah ibn Kab al-Ansari. 82 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abi Ishaq, from Amir ibn Sad who said: I entered an arsh (a shaded structure) in Madinah, and in it were Qarazah ibn Kab and Abu Masud al-Ansari - and he mentioned a hadith of theirs in which they both said: And we have been permitted weeping at calamity without wailing."
"17164","Those named Quhayd; Quhayd ibn Mutarrif al-Ghifari. 83 - Abdan ibn Ahmad al-Marwazi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Amr and Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us. And Zakariya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, Bundar narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Muttalib narrated to us, my father al-Muttalib narrated to us, from his father, from Quhayd ibn Mutarrif al-Ghifari that a beggar asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: If an aggressor attacks me - so he ordered him to fight him. He said: How about us? He said: If he kills you, you are in Paradise, and if you kill him, he is in the Fire."
"17165","al-Qaqa ibn Abi Hadrad al-Aslami. 84 - Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Said al-Maqburi, from his father, from al-Qaqa ibn Abi Hadrad al-Aslami who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Live like Mad (adopt the hardy life), be rough, and walk barefoot."
"17166","Those named Qutham; Qutham ibn al-Abbas; what Qutham narrated. 85 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Rawasibi narrated to us, from his father, from Abi Ishaq who said: It was said to Qutham ibn al-Abbas: How did Ali inherit the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the exclusion of al-Abbas? He said: Because he was the first of us to join him and the closest of us in attachment to him."
"17167","86 - Sulayman ibn al-Muafa ibn Sulayman narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abi Ishaq who said: It was said to Qutham ibn al-Abbas: How did Ali inherit the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to your exclusion? He said: He was the first of us to join him and the closest of us in attachment to him."
"17168","Chapter of Kaf; Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari, Aqabi, his kunyah Abu Abdullah, and it is said Abu Abd al-Rahman. 87 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Urwah: concerning the naming of those who witnessed al-Aqabah of the Ansar, then of Banu Salimah: Kab ibn Malik ibn Abi al-Qayn ibn Kab ibn Sawad."
"17169","88 - al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those who witnessed al-Aqabah of the Ansar, then of Banu Salimah: Kab ibn Malik."
"17170","What Kab ibn Malik narrated; what Abu Umamah al-Bahili narrated from Kab ibn Malik. 89 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Zahr narrated to me, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abi Umamah, from Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari who said: My last memory of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was five nights before his death, and I heard him saying: There was no prophet but he had a close friend in his ummah, and my close friend is Abu Bakr ibn Abi Quhafah, and Allah - Mighty and Majestic - has taken your companion as a close friend. Verily the nations before you used to take the graves of their prophets as mosques, and I forbid you from that. O Allah, have I conveyed - three times, then he said: O Allah, bear witness - three times, then he fainted for a while, then said: Allah, Allah regarding what your right hands possess; fill their stomachs, clothe their backs, and speak gently to them."
"17171","Ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father; al-Zuhri from Ibn Kab. 90 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri who said: Ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me, from his father who said: I did not stay behind from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any raid he raided until the raid of Tabuk, except Badr; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not blame anyone who stayed behind from Badr. He had only gone out seeking the caravan, then Quraysh came out hastening to aid their caravan, so they met without appointment as Allah Almighty said. By my life, the noblest of the scenes of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the people is Badr, yet I would not love to have witnessed it in place of my pledge on the night of al-Aqabah when we pledged upon Islam. Then I did not stay behind after that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any raid he raided until Tabuk, and it was the last raid he raided. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) announced the departure to the people, wanting them to prepare the preparation for their raid, and that was when the shades were pleasant and the fruits were ripe. He rarely intended a raid but he concealed its news, and he used to say: War is deception. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the raid of Tabuk wanted the people to prepare fully. I was at my easiest ever, having gathered two mounts, and I felt most able in myself for jihad and light of flank, yet I inclined toward the shades and the sweetness of fruits. I remained thus until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose going forth in the morning, and that was Thursday, and he loved to go out on Thursday. In the morning he went forth, so I said: I shall go tomorrow to the market and buy my equipment then join them! So I went to the market the next day, but part of my affair became difficult for me, so I returned, and said: I shall return tomorrow if Allah wills! I kept thus until sin enveloped me, and I stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I would walk in the markets and wander in Madinah, and it grieved me that I saw no one except a man who had stayed behind, suspected of hypocrisy. There was no one who stayed behind but thought that would remain hidden for him; the people were many, no diwan gathered them. All who stayed behind from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were some eighty-odd men. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not mention me until he reached Tabuk, and when he reached Tabuk he said: What has Kab ibn Malik done? A man of my people said: His two cloaks and looking at his sides have kept him back, O Prophet of Allah! So Muadh ibn Jabal said: Evil is what you said; by Allah, O Prophet of Allah, we know only good! While they were thus, there appeared a man with the mirage moving him along, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be Abu Khaythamah! And it was Abu Khaythamah. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the raid of Tabuk and turned back and drew near Madinah, I began to think by what I might escape the anger of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), seeking help for that from every man of judgment among my family, until it was said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) will reach you tomorrow morning. Falsehood left me, and I knew I would not be saved except by truthfulness. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered in the forenoon and prayed two rakahs in the mosque, for when he came from a journey he did that: he came to the mosque and prayed two rakahs in it, then sat. Those who had stayed behind began coming to him, swearing to him and excusing themselves to him, so he sought forgiveness for them and accepted their outward, entrusting their secrets to Allah. I entered the mosque and he was sitting; when he saw me he smiled the smile of one angry. I came and sat before him, and he said: Had you not bought a mount? I said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. He said: Then what kept you back? I said: By Allah, had I sat before anyone of the people other than you, I would have escaped his anger upon me with an excuse, for I have been given argument. But I know, O Prophet of Allah, that if I tell you today a word in which you find against me, though it be true, I hope for Allah's good consequence; and if I narrate to you today a tale in which you are pleased with me, though it be a lie, Allah will soon expose me to you. By Allah, O Prophet of Allah, I was never easier nor lighter of flank than when I stayed behind from you! He said: As for this one, he has told you the truth; stand until Allah decides concerning you. So I stood, and men of my people rose after me blaming me, saying: By Allah, we never knew you committed any sin before this; why did you not excuse yourself to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with an excuse that would please him, and the seeking of forgiveness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have followed that, and you would not have put yourself in a position in which you do not know what will be decided for you! They kept blaming me until I intended to return and belie myself. So I said: Has anyone else said this saying? They said: Yes, Hilal ibn Umayyah and Murarah ibn Rabiah said it; they mentioned two righteous men who had witnessed Badr, in whom I have example. So I said: By Allah, I shall never return to him in this nor belie myself! He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade speaking to us three. I would go out to the market and no one spoke to me; the people became estranged from us until they were not those we knew, the walls became estranged to us until they were not the walls we knew, the earth became estranged to us until it was not the earth we knew. I was the strongest of my companions; I would go out and wander in the market and come to the mosque and enter, and come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greet him, saying: Did he move his lips with the greeting? When I stood praying toward a pillar and turned to my prayer, he looked at me from the corner of his eye, and when I looked at him he turned away from me. My two companions submitted and wept night and day, not raising their heads. While I was wandering in the market, a Christian man came with food of his to sell, asking: Who will guide me to Kab ibn Malik? The people pointed him to me, and he brought me a letter from the king of Ghassan, in which was: As for after, it has reached me that your companion has forsaken you and cast you off; you are not in a land of waste nor humiliation, so join us and we shall console you! I said: This too is trial and evil, so I heated the oven for it and burned it in it. When forty nights passed, a messenger of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me and said: Withdraw from your wife! I said: Shall I divorce her? He said: No, but do not approach her! The wife of Hilal ibn Umayyah came and said: O Messenger of Allah, Hilal ibn Umayyah is an old weak man; will you permit me to serve him? He said: Yes, but let him not approach you! She said: O Prophet of Allah, by Allah, he has no movement for anything; he has remained reclining weeping night and day since his affair happened! Kab said: When the trial grew long upon me, I leapt over the wall of Abu Qatadah, my cousin, and greeted him but he did not return it to me. So I said: I adjure you by Allah, O Abu Qatadah, do you know that I love Allah and His Messenger? He was silent. Then I said again: I adjure you by Allah, O Abu Qatadah, do you know that I love Allah and His Messenger? He said: Allah and His Messenger know best! I could not restrain myself from weeping, then I leapt out over the wall. When fifty nights passed since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the people from speaking to us, I prayed the dawn prayer on the roof of a house of ours then sat, in the state which Allah Mighty and Majestic described: the earth narrowed upon us spacious as it was, and our souls narrowed upon us, when I heard a call from the peak of Sal: Glad tidings, O Kab ibn Malik! So I fell prostrate, and knew Allah Mighty and Majestic had brought relief. Then a man came hastening on a horse to give me glad tidings, and the voice was swifter than his horse. So I gave him my two garments as glad tidings and wore two other garments. Our repentance had been sent down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the last third of the night, and Umm Salamah said: O Prophet of Allah, shall we not give glad tidings to Kab ibn Malik? He said: Then the people will crowd you and keep you from sleep all night! And Umm Salamah was kindly disposed toward my affair, grieving at my matter. So I set out to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was sitting in the mosque with the Muslims around him, shining like the shining of the moon, for when he was pleased with a matter he shone. I came and sat before him, and he said: Glad tidings, O Kab ibn Malik, of the best day that has come upon you since your mother bore you! I said: O Prophet of Allah, is it from Allah or from you? He said: Rather from Allah! Then he recited to them: {Allah has turned to the Prophet and the Emigrants and the Helpers} until he reached {the Oft-Returning, the Merciful}. And he said: And concerning us was also revealed: {Fear Allah and be with the truthful}. He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, part of my repentance is that I shall not speak but truth, and that I shall strip myself of all my wealth as charity to Allah and to His Messenger! He said: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you! He said: I said: Then I shall keep my share at Khaybar. He said: No blessing has Allah bestowed upon me in Islam greater than my truthfulness to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I told him truth, I and my two companions, that we did not lie to him, lest we perish as they perished. I hope Allah has not tried anyone in truthfulness as He tried me; I have not deliberately told a lie since, and I hope Allah will preserve me in what remains. Mamar said: al-Zuhri said: This is what has reached us of the hadith of Kab ibn Malik."
"17172","91 - Abu Shuayb Abdullah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Nufayli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq who said: al-Zuhri mentioned, from Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab al-Ansari, then al-Sulami, that his father Abdullah ibn Kab said - and he was the guide of his father Kab when he lost his sight - he said: I heard my father Kab narrating his hadith when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the raid of Tabuk, and the hadith of his two companions. He said: I never stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any raid except it, save that I had stayed behind from him in the raid of Badr, and neither Allah nor His Messenger blamed anyone who stayed behind from it. That was because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had only gone out seeking the caravan of Quraysh, until Allah gathered him and his enemy without appointment. I witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the night of al-Aqabah when we pledged upon Islam, and I would not love to have in exchange for it the scene of Badr, though the raid of Badr is more mentioned among the people than it. Part of my tale when I stayed behind from him in that raid: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rarely went out in a raid he raided but he alluded to another, until that raid, which he raided in severe heat and faced a numerous enemy, so he made plain to the people their affair so they might prepare that preparation for their raid, and he told them the direction he intended. The Muslims who followed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were many, no preserving book gathered them - Kab thereby meant the diwan, saying: no written diwan gathered them. Kab said: Few were the men who wished to be absent but thought that would be hidden, unless revelation from Allah Mighty and Majestic descended concerning it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided that raid when fruits were ripe and shades had come, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prepared and the Muslims with him prepared. I would go early to prepare with them, then return having accomplished nothing, saying to myself: I am able to do that when I wish. That kept dragging on with me until the people girded for departure. In the morning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went forth going, with the Muslims with him, while I had accomplished nothing. Then I went early and returned having accomplished nothing, and it remained thus until they hastened. I intended to mount and catch them; would that I had done so, but I did not. When I went out among the people after the departure of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it grieved me to see only a man suspected of hypocrisy or a man excused as among the weak. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not mention me until he reached Tabuk. He said while sitting among the people: Do you know what Kab ibn Malik has done? A man of Banu Salimah said: Yes, his two cloaks and looking at his side have detained him! So Muadh ibn Jabal said: Do you know what you said? By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we know only good of him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silent. When it reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had turned back from Tabuk, evil attended me, and I began to recall lying, saying: By what shall I escape the anger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tomorrow? Seeking help for that from everyone of my family I saw. When it was said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has overshadowed you, coming, falsehood left me, and I knew I would not be saved from him except by truthfulness, so I resolved to tell him truth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached Madinah in the morning; when he came from a journey he began with the mosque, prayed two rakahs in it, then sat for the people. When he did that, those left behind came to him, excusing themselves to him and swearing to him, and they were some eighty-odd men. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted their outward and their oaths, seeking forgiveness for them, and left their secrets to Allah Mighty and Majestic. Until I came and greeted him; when I greeted him he smiled the smile of one angry, then said: Come! So I came walking until I sat before him, and he said to me: What kept you back? Had you not bought a mount? I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, had I sat with anyone of the people of this world, I would have thought I would escape his anger, for I have been given argument. But by Allah, I know that if I narrate to you today a lie that you are pleased with me for, Allah will soon make you angry with me; and if I narrate to you a truthful tale in which you find against me, I hope for Allah's pardon in it. By Allah, I had no excuse; by Allah, I was never stronger nor easier than when I stayed behind from you! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for this one, he has told you truth; stand until Allah decides His command concerning you! So I stood, and men of Banu Salimah rose with me and followed me, saying: By Allah, we never knew you committed a sin before this; you have failed not to have excused yourself to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as the hypocrites excused themselves to him. The seeking of forgiveness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have sufficed you. By Allah, they kept blaming me until I wished to return to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and belie myself. Then I said to them: Has anyone else met this? They said: Yes, two men said as you said and were told as you were told! I said: Who are they? They said: Murarah ibn al-Rabi and Hilal ibn Umayyah al-Waqifi - they mentioned two righteous men in whom there is example, so I was firm when they mentioned them to me. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade speaking to us three apart from those who stayed behind from him. The people avoided us and changed toward us, until my soul and the earth became estranged to me, and it was not the earth I knew. We remained thus fifty nights. My two companions submitted and sat in their houses, while I was the strongest and hardiest of the group; I would go out and attend prayer with the Muslims and wander in the markets, but no one spoke to me. I would come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in his مجلس after prayer and greet him, saying to myself: Did he move his lips returning the greeting upon me or not? Then I would pray near him, stealing glances at him; when I turned to prayer he looked at me, and when I turned toward him he turned away from me. When that lengthened upon me from the estrangement of the Muslims, I walked until I climbed the wall of Abu Qatadah, my cousin and most beloved of people to me, and greeted him; by Allah, he did not return the greeting to me. So I said: O Abu Qatadah, I adjure you by Allah, do you know that I love Allah and His Messenger? He was silent. I returned to him and adjured him, and he was silent. I returned to him and adjured him, and he said: Allah and His Messenger know best! So my eyes overflowed, and I went and climbed back over the wall. Then I went early to the market; while walking in the market, a Nabataean of the Nabataeans of Sham asked about me, of those who had come selling food in Madinah, saying: Who will guide me to Kab ibn Malik? The people pointed to me, until he came to me and handed me a letter from the king of Ghassan, written in a piece of silk, in which was: As for after, it has reached us that your companion has forsaken you; Allah has not placed you in a land of humiliation nor waste, so join us and we shall console you! When I read it I said: This too is trial; I have reached a state where a man of the people of idolatry hopes for me. So I went with it to an oven and kindled it with it. We remained thus until forty nights passed, when the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commands you to withdraw from your wife! I said: Divorce, or what? He said: Rather withdraw from her and do not approach her. And he sent the like to my two companions. So I said to my wife: Join your family and be with them until Allah decides in this affair what He will decide. The wife of Hilal ibn Umayyah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Hilal ibn Umayyah is an old wasted man with no servant; do you dislike me serving him? He said: No, but let him not approach you. She said: O Messenger of Allah, he has no movement toward me; by Allah, he has not ceased weeping since his affair happened until this day, and I fear for his sight! Some of my family said to me: Would that you sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for your wife, for he has permitted the wife of Hilal ibn Umayyah to serve him! I said: No, by Allah, I shall not seek his permission concerning her; what do I know what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say if I sought his permission concerning her, while I am a young man. We spent ten nights thus, completing fifty nights since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade speaking to us. I prayed the dawn of the fiftieth night on the roof of one of our houses, in the state Allah mentioned: the earth narrowed upon me spacious as it was, and my soul narrowed upon me. I had built a tent on the back of Sal, and I would be in it, when I heard a crier at the top of Sal calling at the top of his voice: O Kab ibn Malik, glad tidings! So I fell prostrate and knew relief had come. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed the people of Allah's turning to us when dawn appeared. Men went to give us glad tidings: a bearer of glad tidings went toward my two companions, a man hastened to me on a horse, and a runner of Aslam hastened until he ascended the mountain, and the voice was swifter than the horseman. When he who I heard his voice giving me glad tidings came to me, I stripped my two garments and clothed him with them as glad tidings; by Allah, I owned no others that day, and I borrowed two garments and wore them. I set out seeking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the people met me congratulating me on repentance, saying: May Allah's turning to you delight you! Until I entered the mosque with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting and the people around him. Talhah ibn Ubaydullah stood to me and greeted me and congratulated me; by Allah, no man of the Emigrants stood to me besides him. Kab would never forget it for him. Kab said: When I greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his face was radiant with joy, and he said: Glad tidings of the best day that has passed over you since your mother bore you! I said: From you, O Messenger of Allah, or from Allah Blessed and Exalted? He said: Rather from Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when pleased, would beam until his face was as a piece of the moon, and we knew that of him. When I sat before him I said: O Messenger of Allah, part of my repentance is that I strip myself of my wealth as charity to Allah and to His Messenger. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you. I said: I shall keep my share of Khaybar. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has saved me by truthfulness, and part of my repentance to Allah is that I shall not speak but truth as long as I remain. By Allah, I know no one of the people whom Allah has tried in truthful speech as He has tried me since I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until this day, and I hope Allah will preserve me in what remains. And Allah Mighty and Majestic revealed: {Allah has turned to the Prophet and the Emigrants and the Helpers who followed him in the hour of hardship, after the hearts of a party of them had almost swerved, then He turned to them; He is to them Kind, Merciful * And to the three who were left behind, until - } to His saying: {and be with the truthful}. Kab said: By Allah, no blessing has Allah bestowed upon me after guiding me to Islam greater in myself than my truthfulness to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day, save that I might have lied to him and perished as those who lied to him perished. For Allah said concerning those who lied to him, when revelation was sent down, the worst said to anyone. He said: {They will swear by Allah to you when you return to them, that you turn away from them; so turn away from them, they are filth, and their abode is Hell, recompense for what they earned * They swear to you that you be pleased with them; if you are pleased with them, Allah is not pleased with the defiantly disobedient people}. He said: We three were left behind apart from the affair of those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted when they swore to him, believing them and seeking forgiveness for them, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deferred our affair until Allah decided therein what He decided. That is the saying of Allah Mighty and Majestic: {And upon the three who were left behind}; and what Allah mentioned is not for our staying behind from the raid, but for his leaving us behind and deferring our affair apart from those who swore to him and excused themselves to him, so he accepted from them."
"17173","92 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qatii narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik who said: Kab said: I witnessed al-Aqabah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I did not stay behind from any raid he raided except the raid of Badr, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not blame anyone who stayed behind from Badr; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had only gone out seeking the caravan of Quraysh. And he mentioned the hadith."
"17174","93 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us. And Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Sad narrated to us, my uncle narrated to us, the nephew of al-Zuhri narrated to us, from his uncle, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab narrated to me that Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik - and he was the guide of Kab among his sons - said: I heard Kab narrating his hadith when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he mentioned the like."
"17175","94 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Raqqi narrated to us, Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Murrah, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, when Allah turned to him, he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, only truthfulness has saved me; part of my repentance is that I shall never speak a lie, and that I strip myself of my wealth to Allah Mighty and Majestic and His Messenger. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you. He said: I shall keep the share of Khaybar."
"17176","95 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam al-Kufi narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ansari narrated to me, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Kab, from his father Kab who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended the Banu al-Asfar to raid them, he made plain to the people their affair; he rarely intended a raid but he alluded to another than it, until that raid, when he faced severe heat, a distant journey and a fresh enemy, so he disclosed to the people the direction in which he went out with them so they might prepare therein the preparation for their enemy. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prepared and the people with him prepared. I would go early to prepare, then return having accomplished nothing, until the people took fright and it was said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is going forth to his destination. So I said: I shall prepare after him by a day or two, then catch them! I had two mounts; never had two mounts gathered with me before them, and I felt able in myself, strong in equipment. I kept going early after him, returning having accomplished nothing, until the people went far and hastened. I would go early for talk, and men busied me, so I resolved to sit until the people outran me. I would go early and see only a man excused by Allah, Glorious is His Name, or a man suspected of hypocrisy, and that grieved me. I began preparing an excuse for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he comes, readying speech. It was decreed for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not to mention me until he alighted at Tabuk. He said to the people while sitting at Tabuk: What has Kab ibn Malik done? A man of my people rose to him and said: His two cloaks and looking at his sides have busied him! Another man spoke and said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we know only good! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silent. When it was said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had come, falsehood and what I had gathered of lying and excuse left me, and I knew nothing would save me from him except truthfulness, so I resolved upon his truthfulness. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached Madinah in the morning; he came and I went early to him, and he was among the people sitting in the mosque; rarely did he look at me but he called me. He said: Come, O Kab, what kept you back from me? Smiling the smile of one angry. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have no excuse; I was never stronger nor easier than when I stayed behind from you! The left-behind had come swearing, and he accepted from them, sought forgiveness for them, and entrusted their secrets in that to Allah Mighty and Majestic. When he told him truth he said: As for this one, he has told truth; stand until Allah decides concerning you what He will decide. So I stood, and a man of Banu Salimah rose to me and said: By Allah, you have done nothing; by Allah, the seeking of forgiveness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for you would have sufficed for your sin which you committed, as he did that with others: he accepted their excuse from them and sought forgiveness for them. They kept blaming me until I wished to return and belie myself. Then I said to them: Has anyone said this saying and excused himself as I excused myself? They said: Yes. I said: Who? They said: Hilal ibn Umayyah al-Waqifi, and Rabiah ibn Murarah al-Amri - they mentioned two righteous men who had witnessed Badr - they excused themselves as you excused yourself, and were told as you were told. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade speaking to us. We would go early among the people and no one spoke a word to us, none greeted us nor returned our greeting, until when forty nights neared, the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us: withdraw from your women. As for Hilal ibn Umayyah, his wife came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: He is a weak-sighted old man; do you dislike me making food for him? He said: No, but let him not approach you. She said: By Allah, he has no movement toward anything; by Allah, he has not ceased weeping since his affair happened until this day. He said: My family said to me: Would that you sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as the wife of Hilal ibn Umayyah sought permission to serve him! He said: I said: By Allah, I shall not seek his permission concerning her; what do I know what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say if I sought his permission, while he is an old man and I am young! So I said to my wife: Join your family until Allah decides what He will decide. We would walk among the people and no one spoke to us, no greeting was returned to us. I came until I climbed a wall of my cousin in his garden, and greeted him, but he did not move his lips returning the greeting to me. So I said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know that I love Allah and His Messenger? He did not speak a word to me. Then I returned and he did not speak to me; then I returned and he did not speak to me, until when the fourth or third was complete he said: Allah and His Messenger know best! So I returned. While walking in the market, the people pointed at me with their hands, and a Nabataean of the Nabataeans of Sham asked about me; they pointed him to me until he came to me and handed me a letter from some of my people in Sham: It has reached us what your companion has done to you and his estrangement from you, so join us; Allah Mighty and Majestic has not placed you in a land of humiliation nor a land of waste; we shall share our wealth with you. He said: I said: To Allah we belong; the people of disbelief hope for me! So I made for an oven and kindled it with it. By Allah, I was in that state which Allah Almighty described - the earth narrowed upon us spacious as it was, and our souls narrowed upon us - on the morning of a night since speaking to us was forbidden, repentance was sent down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) announced it to us when he prayed dawn. The people went to give us glad tidings; a man hastened to a horse, or a runner of Aslam hastened and ascended the mountain, and the voice was swifter than the horse. He called: O Kab ibn Malik, glad tidings! So I fell prostrate and knew relief had come. When he whose voice I heard came to me, I gave him two garments as his glad tidings; by Allah, I owned no two garments besides them that day, and I borrowed two garments and went out toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The people met me in groups congratulating me on Allah's turning to me, until I entered the mosque. Talhah ibn Ubaydullah stood to me hastening until he shook my hand and congratulated me; no man of the Emigrants stood to me besides him, and Kab recognized that for Talhah. Then I came until I stood upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his face as a piece of the moon, for when he was pleased his face shone for that. He called me: Come, O Kab, glad tidings of the best day that has passed over you since your mother bore you! He said: I said: From Allah or from you? He said: No, rather from Allah; you told Allah truth, so He told you truth. He said: I said: Part of my repentance to Him is that I go out of my wealth as charity to Allah and to His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep some of your wealth. I said: I shall keep my share at Khaybar. Kab said: By Allah, Allah has not tried any man in truthful speech as He tried me."
"17177","96 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me, from his father that he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he was turned to: O Messenger of Allah, I strip myself of my wealth as charity to Allah and His Messenger. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you."
"17178","97 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me, who said: Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik - and he was the guide of Kab among his sons when he went blind - said: I heard Kab ibn Malik. Ahmad ibn Salih said: And he mentioned the hadith of the three who were left behind. Ahmad ibn Salih said: And at the end of this hadith of his there is not the speech which is in the first hadith. Ahmad said: And I think al-Zuhri heard this speech from Abdullah ibn Kab abridged, and heard the hadith in its sequence from his father from him."
"17179","98 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Abdullah al-Kurayzi narrated to us, Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri that he heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrating from his uncle Ubaydullah ibn Kab - and he was the guide of Kab when he went blind - who said: Kab said: I pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of al-Aqabah when we pledged to him upon Islam, and I did not witness Badr, nor would I love to have Badr for it, though it is more mentioned among the people than it. It was a raid in which Allah blamed no one who stayed behind from it; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had only gone out seeking the caravan until Allah gathered him and his enemy without appointment. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to raid a direction, we would alter it, until the raid of Tabuk; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided - in the hadith of Zayd - and he mentioned the hadith."
"17180","99 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to me, Hammad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ismail ibn Umayyah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik said, from Kab ibn Malik when Allah turned to him: he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, only truthfulness has saved me; part of my repentance is that I shall never speak a lie, and that I withdraw from my wealth as charity to Allah and to His Messenger. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you. Kab said: Then I shall keep my share of Khaybar."
"17181","100 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik that his grandfather Kab ibn Malik, when Allah turned to him in his staying behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: O Messenger of Allah, part of my repentance is that I strip myself of my wealth as charity to Allah and to His Messenger. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you."
"17182","101 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik that Kab ibn Malik said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he was one of the three who stayed behind, then Allah turned to them - Kab said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Part of my repentance is that I shall not speak but truth, and that I strip myself of all my wealth as charity to Allah and His Messenger. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep some of your wealth."
"17183","102 - Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abi Shuayb al-Harrani narrated to us, Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the hadith of the repentance of Kab ibn Malik in full."
"17184","103 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir al-Bakhtari al-Tai al-Himsi narrated to us, Bishr ibn Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from his father, from al-Zuhri who said: Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me that he heard Kab one night - and he was one of the three who were turned to - saying: When I was given glad tidings of repentance, I came intending the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the people kept meeting me in groups congratulating me on Allah's turning to me, until when I entered the mosque, Talhah ibn Ubaydullah stood to me until he shook my hand."
"17185","104 - Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, part of my repentance is that I strip myself of my wealth, and that I abandon the abode of my people in which I committed the sin. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: A third of that suffices you."
"17186","105 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Man ibn Isa al-Qazzaz narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from Kab that when he was given glad tidings of his repentance he prostrated, and gave him who gave him glad tidings his two garments."
"17187","Chapter. 106 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ibn Shihab narrated to me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to him, from his father Abdullah ibn Kab, and from his uncle Ubaydullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from Kab ibn Malik who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not return from a journey except by day in the forenoon; when he returned he began with the mosque, prayed two rakahs in it, then sat in it."
"17188","107 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr al-Misri narrated to us, Said ibn Ufayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Uqayl and Rashdin, from Uqayl and Qurrah, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from Kab who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from a journey he began with the mosque and glorified (prayed) two rakahs in it, then greeted and sat in his prayer place, and the Muslims came to him greeting him."
"17189","108 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, my uncle informed me that his father Kab used to say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from a journey he came to the mosque and bowed two rakahs in it, then sat for the people in their fatwas and questions."
"17190","109 - Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid al-Bayruti narrated to us, Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Namir narrated to us, from al-Zuhri that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to him that his father Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he returned from a journey, began with the mosque and bowed two rakahs in it, then sat for the people in their fatwas and questions, as much as seemed fit to him to sit."
"17191","110 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us. And Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us - both said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rarely went out on a journey except on Thursday."
"17192","Chapter. 111 - Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari narrated to us, al-Muafa ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When Egypt is opened, treat the Copts well, for they have covenant and kinship - meaning that the mother of Ismail was of them."
"17193","112 - Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi narrated to us, my father narrated to us. And Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar and Duhaym narrated to us, both said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from Kab that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you open Egypt, treat the Copts well, for they have covenant and kinship."
"17194","113 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you open Egypt, treat the Copts well; for they have covenant and kinship."
"17195","Chapter. 114 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Zaidah Zakariya ibn Yahya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, Umar ibn Habib al-Qadi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: We used to pray Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then come to Banu Salimah while we could see the places of our arrows fall in Banu Salimah at the farthest part of Madinah."
"17196","115 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Bara narrated to us, al-Muafa ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, Ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to me, from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Maghrib, and men of Banu Salimah prayed with him while they could see the places of the arrows."
"17197","116 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Uthman ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us - both said: Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Isa ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Maghrib, then the people returned to their families while they could see the places of the arrows when shot with them."
"17198","117 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, he informed him that men of Banu Salimah used to attend Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then depart to their families while they could see the places of the arrows. Mamar and Ibn Jurayj did not say in this hadith: from Ibn Kab, from his father."
"17199","118 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab that a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him that they used to pray with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then depart to their families and return to Banu Salimah while they could see the places of the arrows when shot with them. Thus Yunus narrated it, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Kab: a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me."
"17200","Chapter. 119 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik who said: Umm Mubashshir said to Kab when he was attended (by death): Convey greeting to my son - meaning Mubashshir. So Kab said: May Allah forgive you, O Umm Mubashshir, have you not heard what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said? He said: The soul of the believer is in a bird feeding in the trees of Paradise until Allah returns it to its body on the Day of Resurrection. She said: I seek Allah's forgiveness."
"17201","120 - Idris ibn Jafar al-Attar narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Malik, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The souls of the believers are in birds suspended in Paradise until Allah returns them to their bodies on the Day of Resurrection."
"17202","121 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Mansur ibn Abi Muzahim narrated to me, Abu Uways narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik that Kab ibn Malik used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The soul of the believer is but a bird feeding in the trees of Paradise until Allah returns it in its body on the day He raises it."
"17203","122 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us. And Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us - both from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Harith ibn Fudayl, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik who said: When death attended Kab ibn Malik, Umm Bishr bint al-Bara ibn Marur entered upon him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, if you meet my son, convey to him greeting from me! He said: May Allah forgive you, O Umm Bishr, we are busier than that! She said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, have you not heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The souls of the believers are in the bellies of birds feeding upon the trees of Paradise? He said: Yes. She said: Then it is thus."
"17204","123 - Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Ishaq narrated to us, al-Awzai narrated to us, al-Zuhri narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab narrated to me, from Kab who said: When death attended him, Umm Mubashshir came to him and said: Convey greeting to my son! He said to her: Have you not heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The soul of the believer is a bird feeding in the trees of Paradise until it is raised on the Day of Resurrection? She said: Yes, but I forgot."
"17205","124 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Said narrated to us, Umar narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Salih ibn Kaysan narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab narrated to me that it reached him that Kab ibn Malik said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The soul of the believer when he dies is a bird feeding in the trees of Paradise until Allah returns it to its body on the Day of Resurrection."
"17206","125 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that a man of the Ansar died, so Umm Mubashshir said: Convey to my son Mubashshir greeting from me! So Kab said: Not thus did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only said: The souls of the martyrs are in green birds feeding wherever they wish. She said: Then I seek Allah's forgiveness. He said: Thus I heard it."
"17207","Chapter. 126 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us. And Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us - both said: Waki narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Salih, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was pressing a man for two uqiyyahs, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the man: Thus you reduce by half. The man said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Pay him what remains of his due."
"17208","127 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Yunus narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik that his father informed him that he demanded from Ibn Abi Hadrad a debt owed to him in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, until their voices rose, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard them while in his house, until he came out to them, uncovering the curtain of his chamber until he saw Kab. He said: O Kab ibn Malik! He said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Reduce half of this debt of yours. He said: I have reduced, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Stand and pay him."
"17209","128 - Harun ibn Kamil al-Misri narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me that Kab ibn Malik informed him that he demanded from Ibn Abi Hadrad a debt owed to him in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, and their voices rose until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard them while in his house. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to them until he uncovered the curtain of his chamber, and called Kab ibn Malik, saying: O Kab. He said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. So he signaled to him with his hand to reduce half of his debt. So Kab said: I have done, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand and pay him."
"17210","129 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to me that Kab ibn Malik informed him that he demanded from Ibn Abi Hadrad a debt owed to him in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, and their voices rose until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard them - and he mentioned the like."
"17211","Chapter. 130 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Zurarah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Unaysah, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage with women."
"17212","131 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Wasil ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Dinar, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mutah marriage and the flesh of domestic donkeys."
"17213","132 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Jazari narrated to us, Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, Umar ibn Qays narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: I bear witness upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade on the day of Khaybar the flesh of domestic donkeys."
"17214","Chapter. 133 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Ismail narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from the guide of Kab ibn Malik, from Kab ibn Malik who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was pleased, you saw his face as a piece of the moon."
"17215","134 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Sad ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, my uncle narrated to me, the nephew of al-Zuhri narrated to us, from his uncle, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab narrated to us that Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik - and he was the guide of Kab among his sons when he went blind - said: I heard Kab ibn Malik saying: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was pleased with a matter, his face shone as a piece of the moon until that was known of him."
"17216","135 - Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me, from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when pleased, his face shone as a piece of the moon."
"17217","136 - Abdullah ibn Najiyah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his uncle Ubaydullah ibn Kab - and he was the guide of Kab after his sight went - from Kab ibn Malik who said: When a matter pleased the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his face shone as the disc of the moon."
"17218","137 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Bakr al-Balisi narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab, from his father that a Jewish woman gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a roasted sheep at Khaybar. He said to her: What is this? She said: A gift, fearing to say it was of charity. He ate and his Companions ate, then he said to them: Hold back. Then he said to the woman: Have you poisoned this sheep? She said: Who told you? He said: This bone - for its leg, or it was in his hand. She said: Yes. He said: Why? She said: I said: If you are a liar, the people will be relieved of you; and if you are a prophet, it will not harm you. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cupped upon the shoulder, and ordered his Companions and they cupped, but some of them died. al-Zuhri said: And the woman embraced Islam, and they mentioned that he killed her."
"17219","Chapter. 138 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Bakr al-Balisi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musab narrated to us, al-Awzai narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: Mulaib al-Asinnah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a gift, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered him Islam but he refused to embrace Islam. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not accept the gift of a polytheist."
"17220","139 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: Mulaib al-Asinnah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a gift, so he offered him Islam but he refused to embrace Islam. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not accept the gift of a polytheist. He said: Then send to the people of Najd whomever you wish, and I am their protector. So he sent to them people among whom was al-Mundhir ibn Amr, and he was the one called al-Mutaq (the freed), who freed at death. So Amir ibn al-Tufayl roused Banu Amir against them but they refused to obey him and refused to betray Mulaib al-Asinnah. So he roused Banu Sulaym against them and they obeyed him, following them with nearly a hundred archers. They caught them at Bir Maunah and killed them except Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri."
"17221","140 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik and others that Amir ibn Malik ibn Jafar, called Mulaib al-Asinnah, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while a polytheist. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered him Islam, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not accept the gift of a polytheist! So Amir ibn Malik said: Send, O Messenger of Allah, whomever of your messengers you wish, and I am their protector. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a group among whom was al-Mundhir ibn Amr al-Saidi, called Ataq to die, a watcher among the people of Najd. Amir ibn al-Tufayl heard of them, so he mobilized Banu Sulaym for them, and they went forth with him and killed them at Bir Maunah except Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri. Amir ibn al-Tufayl seized him and released him. When he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from among them - and among them was Amir ibn Fuhayrah - Urwah claimed to me that he was killed that day and his body was not found when they buried him; Urwah says: They thought the angels buried him. So Hassan said, inciting against Amir ibn al-Tufayl: Dhawaib. Then Rabiah ibn Amir ibn Malik stabbed Amir ibn al-Tufayl in his protection of Amir ibn Malik, in his thigh, a stab that cut him. Yunus did not say in his hadith: from his father."
"17222","141 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Every important matter not begun with praise is cut off or severed."
"17223","142 - Musa ibn Isa ibn al-Mundhir al-Himsi narrated to us, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us. And Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us - both said: Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people will be raised on the Day of Resurrection, and I and my ummah shall be upon a hill, and my Lord Mighty and Majestic will clothe me in a red hullah, then I shall be permitted and I shall say what Allah wills me to say; that is the Praised Station."
"17224","Chapter. 143 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi and Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, both said: Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Yusuf ibn al-Safar narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I alighted in the quarter of Banu so-and-so, and the most harmful to me is the nearest to me in neighborhood. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Bakr and Umar and Ali to come to the mosque, stand at its door and call out: Verily forty houses are neighbors, and none enters Paradise whose neighbor fears his evils."
"17225","144 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to me, Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that he had sheep grazing at Sal; a sheep of them fell, so the slave-girl descended and slaughtered it. So Kab asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he saw no harm in it."
"17226","Chapter. 145 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the group he sent to Ibn Abi al-Huqayq to kill him from killing women and children."
"17227","146 - Ibrahim ibn Duhaym narrated to us, my father narrated to us. And Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us - both said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from Kab ibn Malik that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade those who killed Ibn Abi al-Huqayq, when they went out to him, from killing women and children."
"17228","147 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us. And Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Hafs ibn Amr al-Rabbali narrated to us - both said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon us not to kill a child nor a woman."
"17229","148 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah - or Ubaydullah ibn Kab, and he was the guide of Kab - from Kab who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon us not to kill a child nor a woman."
"17230","149 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah - or Ubaydullah ibn Kab, and he was the guide of Kab - from Kab who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon us - and he mentioned the like."
"17231","150 - Ibrahim ibn Suwayd al-Shibami narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, al-Zuhri informed me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab informed me, from his father, from his uncle, from Kab that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing women and children."
"17232","Chapter. 151 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that he said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah has revealed concerning poetry what He revealed. He said: The believer strives with his sword and his tongue; by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it is as if you hurl at them the scattering of arrows."
"17233","152 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that he said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think concerning poetry? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer strives with his sword and his tongue; by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it is as if you sprinkle them with arrows."
"17234","153 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, my brother narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Muhammad ibn Abi Atiq, from Ibn Shihab who said: Bashir ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik said: Kab ibn Malik used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it is as if you sprinkle them with arrows in what he says to them of poetry."
"17235","Chapter. 154 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Haywah ibn Shurayh informed me, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to me that Kab ibn al-Ashraf the Jew was a poet, and he lampooned the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions and incited the disbelievers of Quraysh against them in his poetry. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had come to Madinah while it was mixed: among them Muslims gathered by the call of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them polytheists who worshipped idols, among them Jews, and among them people of arms and fortresses, allies of the two tribes Aws and Khazraj. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, he wished to reconcile them and make truce with them. A man would be Muslim while his father was polytheist, a man would be Muslim while his brother was polytheist. The polytheists and Jews of the people of Madinah, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, harmed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions most severely. Allah commanded His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Muslims to be patient over that and pardon them. Concerning them Allah Almighty revealed: {You will surely hear from those given the Book before you and from those who associate, much harm} to His saying: {of resolute matters}. And concerning them Allah Almighty revealed: {Many of the People of the Book wish to turn you back after your faith into disbelievers} to His saying: {until Allah brings His command}. When Kab ibn al-Ashraf refused to desist from harming the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and harming the Muslims, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Sad ibn Muadh and Muhammad ibn Maslamah al-Ansari, then al-Harithi, and Abu Isa ibn Habr al-Ansari, and al-Harith son of the brother of Sad ibn Muadh, among five men. They came to him in the evening in his مجلس at al-Awali. When Kab ibn al-Ashraf saw them he disapproved of their affair, and he was wary of them. He said to them: What has brought you? They said: A need of yours has brought us. He said: Then let some of you draw near to me to tell me it. So some of them drew near to him and said: We have come to sell you coats of mail of ours to spend their price. He said: By Allah, if you do, you have been strained since this man alighted among you. He promised them to come to him at nightfall when people leave him. So they came to him and a man of them called him. He rose to go out to them, but his wife said: They have not visited you at this hour of theirs with anything you love! He said: Yes, they have told me their tale! So he went out to them, and Muhammad ibn Maslamah embraced him, saying to his companions: Let him not outrun you, even if you kill me and him together! Some of them stabbed him with the sword in his flank. When they killed him, the Jews and the polytheists with them took fright, and in the morning they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Our companion was visited last night, a chief of our chiefs, and killed treacherously. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned to them what he used to say in his poems and harm them with. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called them to write between him and them and the Muslims generally a document containing the collective affair of the people, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote it."
"17236","155 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Abu al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik that Kab ibn al-Ashraf the Jew was a poet, and he lampooned the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions and incited against them. He said: Who is for Kab? When he refused to desist from harming the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and harming the Muslims, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Sad ibn Muadh and Muhammad ibn Maslamah and Abu Isa ibn al-Harith, son of the brother of Sad ibn Muadh, among five, and they came to Kab - and he mentioned the like."
"17237","156 - Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Ammar ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bless my ummah in its early morning."
"17238","157 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Judhuyi al-Qadi and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us - both said: al-Hasan ibn Amr ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muslim, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father Kab ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the slaughter of the fetus, who said: Its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother."
"17239","158 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Husayn ibn Numayr narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab, from his father who said: The last sermon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached to us, he said: O company of Emigrants, you have come to increase, while the Ansar have ended; they are my coffer to which I resorted, so honor their doer of good and overlook their doer of evil."
"17240","159 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father - and he was one of the three who were turned to - that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood as a preacher, praised Allah and extolled Him, and sought forgiveness for the martyrs killed on the day of Uhud, saying: You, O company of Emigrants, increase, while the Ansar do not increase; the Ansar are my coffer to which I resort, so honor their noble and overlook their doer of evil."
"17241","160 - Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi narrated to us, my father narrated to us. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us - both said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Marzuq ibn Abi al-Hudhayl narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab, from his uncle Ubaydullah ibn Kab, from Kab ibn Malik that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from pursuing al-Ahzab, he removed his armor, washed and used incense. Duhaym added in his hadith: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gabriel, peace be upon him, appeared to me and said: Excuse you from fighting; do I not see you have laid down the armor, while we have not laid it down yet! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sprang up startled, and resolved upon the people not to pray Asr except at Banu Qurayzah. He wore arms and they went out, not reaching Banu Qurayzah until the sun set. The people disputed in raiding them concerning the Asr prayer: some said: It has been resolved upon us not to pray Asr until we come to Banu Qurayzah; we are under a resolve of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so there is no sin upon us. One party of them prayed Asr in faith and reckoning, and another party did not pray until they came to Banu Qurayzah after the sun had set, praying it in faith and reckoning. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not blame either of the two parties."
"17242","161 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Musa al-Ashari, hearing him recite: Your brother has been given of the مزامير of the family of Dawud. Yunus did not say in this hadith: from his father."
"17243","162 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muqatil al-Marwazi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from Kab that Amir ibn Malik, called Mulaib al-Asinnah, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Tabuk; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered him Islam but he refused, and he gifted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We do not accept the gift of a polytheist. Then he said: Send whomever you wish to the people of Najd, for I am their protector. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a group among whom was al-Mundhir ibn Amr, and he was the one called: Ataq to death. Amir ibn al-Tufayl heard of them - and he mentioned the hadith."
"17244","Chapter. 163 - Jafar ibn Sulayman al-Nawfali al-Madani narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is your chief, O Banu Salimah? They said: al-Jadd ibn Qays, though we suspect him of miserliness! He said: What disease is worse than miserliness?! They said: Then who is our chief, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Bishr ibn al-Bara ibn Marur."
"17245","164 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab that Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is your chief, O Banu Salimah? They said: Our chief is al-Jadd ibn Qays. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why do you make him chief? They said: He has most wealth of us, though we suspect him of miserliness. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What disease is worse than miserliness?! He is not your chief. They said: Then who is our chief, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Your chief is Bishr ibn al-Bara ibn Marur."
"17246","165 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Yahya, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab, from his father, from his grandfather that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which people are best? He said: A believer between two noble ones."
"17247","166 - Muhammad ibn Abi Zurah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Khalid al-Azraq narrated to us, Yusuf ibn al-Safar narrated to us, al-Awzai narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer is guileless, noble, while the wicked is deceitful, base."
"17248","167 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ansari, al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who saw the slaying of Hamzah? A man said: May Allah honor you, I saw his slaying. He went and stood over Hamzah and saw him with his belly split and mutilated. He said: O Messenger of Allah, he has been mutilated. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked looking at him. He stood among the slain and said: I am a witness over these; wrap them in their blood, for no wounded is wounded in the way of Allah but his wound shall come on the Day of Resurrection bleeding, its color the color of blood and its scent the scent of musk. Advance the most Quran of the people and place him in the lahd."
"17249","168 - Abu Abd al-Malik Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Qurashi al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Shuayb ibn Shabur narrated to us, Isa ibn Abdullah informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abdullah ibn Kab, from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited al-Bara ibn Marur when quinsy seized him, and ordered one to lance him with fire until he faced him."
"17250","169 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a slaughter slaughtered by a woman with a stone, and he saw no harm in it."
"17251","170 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Abdullah al-Wasiti narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of a man: He is of the people of the Fire! The people kept awaiting his affair, until on the day of Hunayn he fought valiantly. They informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that, and he said: He is of the people of the Fire! The man was wounded, so he took an arrow from his quiver and slew himself. They said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has confirmed your word! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand, O Bilal, and call out that none enters Paradise except a believer, and that Allah Mighty and Majestic supports the religion with the wicked man."
"17252","171 - Hajjaj ibn Imran al-Sadusi narrated to us, Abu Salamah Yahya ibn Khalaf al-Jubari narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sadaqah narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like."
"17253","Anas ibn Abi al-Qasim from Ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father. 172 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisî narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Anas ibn Abi al-Qasim, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as I think, concerning His saying: {It is the same to us whether we rage or endure, we have no escape}. He said: The people of the Fire say: Come, let us endure! He said: So they endure five hundred years; when they see that does not benefit them, they say: Come, let us rage! He said: So they weep five hundred years; when they see that does not benefit them, they say: {It is the same to us whether we rage or endure, we have no escape}."
"17254","Abdullah ibn Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Ibn Kab ibn Malik. 173 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Amr ibn Muhammad al-Anqazi narrated to us, Khallad al-Saffar narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out in severe heat and ordered raiding to Tabuk. I was then a believer in Allah and His Messenger, save that my soul yearned for shade and ripe dates. I was then young and strong, my soul telling me, with two camels of mine: You shall excuse yourself to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! And my soul telling me: Stay behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! Thus I was, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went forth in the morning. I went out to the market intending to prepare, as if held by my hand. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marched until, two farsakhs from Madinah, he halted, and a rider caught up with him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be Abu Khaythamah - and it was Abu Khaythamah. In Madinah were eighty-seven hypocrites, and I, Hilal ibn Umayyah and Murarah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked Abu Khaythamah: What has Kab ibn Malik done? He said: I left him walking in the lanes of Madinah! So Muadh said: He, by Allah - as I knew him - loves Allah and His Messenger! A group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted beside us, and some of them said: By Allah, they are the most desirous of us in bellies, the most cowardly at meeting, the weakest in hearts! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ammar ibn Yasir and said: Go to these men and say to them: What did you disparage? If you ask them they will say: We were but engaging and playing. So he said to them: You have burned, may Allah burn you. And was revealed: {If you ask them, they will say: We were but engaging and playing. Say: Was it Allah and His signs and His Messenger you mocked?} A man came who was not of them but had heard; he clung to the leg of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I did not support them, but I heard their saying! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marched, he clinging to the leg, excusing himself to him, marching with him until blood flowed from his heels. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from his raid, and Hilal ibn Umayyah and Murarah ibn Rabiah came to him, and he seated us aside. It was said to Kab ibn Malik: By Allah, he was not pleased with your two companions; see by what you excuse yourself? I said: I shall help what I did with lying; I find nothing better than truthfulness. So I came to him and said: Peace upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings! He said: And upon you; what kept you back, O Kab? I said: By Allah, I stayed not behind from weakness nor need, but trial! He said: Sit with your two companions. Then he said to his Companions: Do not sit with these men, nor speak to them, nor trade with them! He sent to their women: Let them not approach you! The wife of Hilal ibn Umayyah sent to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that Hilal is an old man; will he permit her to give him something without speaking to him? So he permitted her. The wife of Kab sent that the wife of Hilal ibn Umayyah had sought permission to hand him something, shall she seek his permission concerning you? I said: With what will you excuse? Will you say I am an old man? By Allah, I am young. Will you say I am sick? By Allah, I am sound - so he sent to her not to do so. Umm Salamah was an excellent intercessor when her night came; she said: O Messenger of Allah, Hilal ibn Umayyah, you speak concerning him among us. One night he said: Have you felt that Allah has turned to the three? She said: Shall I not send to their families to give them glad tidings? He said: Then the people will not let us sleep this night; but wake. In the morning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed dawn, then turned to his Companions and said: Have you felt that Allah has turned to the three? Two men hastened to Kab ibn Malik: a man mounted a horse and took the valley bottom, and a man walked on his feet until he climbed the mountain. He said: O Kab ibn Malik, have you felt that Allah has turned to you? So I fell prostrate. When he drew near me I threw to him my cloak, then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: From you or from Allah? He said: From Allah Mighty and Majestic."
"17255","Mabad ibn Kab from his brother. 174 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Mabad ibn Kab ibn Malik ibn al-Qayn, brother of Banu Salimah, narrated to me, from his brother Ubaydullah ibn Kab, from his father Kab ibn Malik who said: We went out in the Hajj in which we pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Aqabah, with the polytheists of our people, and with us al-Bara ibn Marur, our elder and chief. When we were at the outskirts of al-Bayda he said: O people, you know I have seen a view, I know not whether you agree with me on it or not? We said: What is it, O Abu al-Bishr? He said: I think I shall pray toward this Building and not turn my back to it! We said: No, by Allah, do not do so; what reached us is that our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prays only toward Sham. He said: By Allah, I shall pray toward it! When prayer came he faced the Kabah while we faced Sham, until we came to Makkah. Al-Bara ibn Marur said to me: O son of my brother, let us go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to ask him about what we did in this journey of ours, for I found in myself from your opposing me. So we went out asking for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a man met us at al-Abtah. We said to him: Will you guide us to Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Muttalib? He said: Do you know him when you see him? We said: No, by Allah, we do not know him - we had never seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Do you know al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib? We said: Yes - we knew him, he used to come to us trading. He said: When you enter the mosque, look at al-Abbas, for he is the man with him. So we entered the mosque with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and al-Abbas sitting in a side of the mosque. We greeted and sat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know these two men? When he turned he said: Yes, this is al-Bara ibn Marur chief of his people, and this is Kab ibn Malik. By Allah, I never forget the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The poet? He said: Yes. Al-Bara ibn Marur said to him: O Messenger of Allah, I saw in this journey of mine a view I wished to ask you about, to tell me about what I did therein. He said: What is that? He said: I thought not to turn my back to this Building, so I prayed toward it! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You were upon a qiblah, had you been patient toward it. He returned to the qiblah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his family saying: He died upon it, and we know better of him; he had returned to the qiblah of the Messenger and prayed with us toward Sham. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised us the middle of the days of Tashriq, we seventy men, for his pledge, with us Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Haram Abu Jabir, though upon his idolatry. We said: O Abu Jabir, by Allah, we dislike you dying upon what you are upon, to be fuel for this Fire tomorrow; Allah Mighty and Majestic has sent a Messenger commanding His oneness, and men of your people have embraced Islam, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has promised us for the pledge. So he embraced Islam, purified his garments and attended it with us, and he was a naqib. On the night the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised us at Mina, early night, with our people, when people grew heavy in sleep, we slipped from our beds as qata birds slip until we gathered at al-Aqabah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us with his uncle al-Abbas, none other with him, he upon his idolatry, but he loved to attend the affair of his nephew. He was the first speaker. He said: O company of Khazraj - the Arabs used to call this tribe of the Ansar, its Aws and its Khazraj, Khazraj - O company of Khazraj, Muhammad is of us where you know; he is in protection of his people and land, and we have defended him from those of like our view of him. He refuses but to join you and what you called him to. If you think you will fulfill to him what you promised him, then you and what you know; if you fear from yourselves abandonment, leave him among his people, for he is in protection of his clan and people. We said: We have heard what you said; speak, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke, calling to Allah Mighty and Majestic, reciting Quran and urging Islam. We answered him in faith in him and belief in him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, take for your Lord then for yourself. He said: I pledge you upon that you defend me as you defend your sons and women. Al-Bara ibn Marur answered him, saying: Yes, by the One Who sent you with truth; pledge us, O Messenger of Allah, for we by Allah are people of wars and people of arms, inherited from elder to elder! Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan interposed in the talk, saying: O Messenger of Allah, between us and people are bonds, and we shall cut them; may it be, if Allah makes you victorious, you return to your people and leave us? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather blood is blood, and ruin is ruin; I am of you and you are of me; I make peace with whom you make peace, and war whom you war. Al-Bara ibn Marur said: Stretch your hand, I pledge you! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring out to me of you twelve naqibs - so they brought them out. The naqib of Banu al-Najjar was Asad ibn Zurarah; the naqib of Banu Salimah was al-Bara ibn Marur and Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Haram; the naqib of Banu Saidah was Sad ibn Ubadah and al-Mundhir ibn Amr; the naqib of Banu Zurayq was Rafi ibn Malik ibn al-Ajlan; the naqib of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj was Abdullah ibn Rawahah and Sad ibn al-Rabi; the naqib of Banu Awf ibn al-Khazraj was Ubadah ibn al-Samit; the naqib of Banu Abd al-Ashhal was Usayd ibn Hudayr and Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan; the naqib of Banu Amr ibn Awf was Sad ibn Khaythamah. They were twelve: nine of Khazraj, three of Aws. Al-Bara ibn Marur took the hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and struck upon it; he was the first to pledge. The people followed and pledged. Satan screamed at al-Aqabah with the farthest voice I ever heard, saying: O people of al-Jabajib, have you aught with Muhammad and the Sabians with him gathered upon your war? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Disperse to your camping places! So we returned to our camping places and lay upon our beds. In the morning a company of Quraysh came, among them al-Harith ibn Hisham, a young lad wearing two new sandals, until they came to us in our camping places, saying: O company of Khazraj, you have come to our companion to take him out from among us; by Allah, none of the Arabs is more hateful to us that war break out between us and him than you! Those of our people of the polytheists there rose swearing by Allah nothing of this happened and we did not do it, while I looked at Abu Jabir ibn Abdullah ibn Haram silent, and I silent. When the people stirred to depart, I said a word as if sharing speech with them: O Abu Jabir, you are of our chiefs and an elder of our elders; can you not take like the sandals of this lad of Quraysh! The lad heard it, so he took off his sandals and threw them to me, saying: By Allah, you shall wear them! So Abu Jabir said: Gently; by life of Allah you have shamed the man - saying you embarrassed him - return his sandals to him. I said: By Allah, I shall not return them; I hope to despoil him."
"17256","175 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qati'i narrated, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated, my father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Ma'bad ibn Ka'b narrated to me, from his brother Ubaydullah ibn Ka'b who said: We set out from Medina heading toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and pilgrims of our people from among the polytheists set out with us, then he mentioned something similar."
"17257","Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 176 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Muhammad ibn Abi Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf narrated to me, from his father, Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik narrated to me who said: I used to lead my father when his sight was gone, and whenever I went out to Friday prayer and he heard the call to it, he would seek forgiveness for Abu Umamah As'ad ibn Zurarah. I kept hearing that from him for a while, then I said: I must ask him about this. So I took him out as I used to, and when he heard the call to Friday prayer he sought forgiveness for him, so I said: O my father, what about your seeking forgiveness for As'ad ibn Zurarah every time you hear the call to Friday prayer? He said: O my son, As'ad was the first to lead us in Friday prayer in Medina before the arrival of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the lowland of the lava field of Banu Bayadah, at Naqi' al-Khadimat. I said: And how many were you on that day? He said: Forty men."
"17258","Abdur-Rahman al-A'raj from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 177 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad an-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from al-A'raj, that Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik al-Ansari informed him, from Ka'b, that he had wealth owed to him by Abdullah ibn Abi Hadrad. He met him and pressed him, and they spoke until voices were raised. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by them and said: O Ka'b - and he gestured with his hand as if meaning half - so he took half of what was owed to him and left half of it."
"17259","178 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated to me, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Hurmuz, from Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from Ka'b, that he had wealth owed to him by Abdullah ibn Abi Hadrad, and he demanded it until voices were raised. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by them and said: O Ka'b - and he gestured with his hand as if meaning half - so he took half of what was owed to him and left half."
"17260","Yazid ibn Khusayfah from Ka'b ibn Malik. 179 - Umar ibn Hafs as-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, Abu Ma'shar al-Barra' narrated, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you feels pain, let him place his hand where he feels his pain, then say: I seek refuge in the might of Allah and His power over all things from the evil of what I feel - seven times."
"17261","Ka'b ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b from his father. 180 - Abu Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Qays ibn ar-Rabi' narrated, from Muhammad ibn Dirham, from Ka'b ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, from his grandfather Ka'b ibn Malik, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by some people of the Ansar building a mosque, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Widen your mosque, you will fill it."
"17262","181 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Talq ibn Ghannam narrated, Muhammad ibn Dirham narrated, from Ka'b ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, from his grandfather Ka'b ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to some people of the Ansar who were laying pebbles in a mosque, and he said: Widen it, you will fill it."
"17263","Sa'd ibn Ibrahim from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 182 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abdur-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated, from Sufyan, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b, from his father who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) licking his three fingers from food."
"17264","183 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated, Sufyan narrated. And Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated, from Sufyan ath-Thawri, Sa'd ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of the believer is like the tender stalk of a crop which the winds bend, straightening it at times and laying it down at others, and the example of the disbeliever is like the firm cedar upon its root until its drying up comes all at once."
"17265","184 - Umar ibn Hafs as-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, al-Mas'udi narrated, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of the believer is like the tender stalk of a crop which the winds bend, laying it down at times and straightening it at times until his term comes to him, and the example of the disbeliever is like the firm cedar upon its root, nothing bends it until its drying up comes all at once."
"17266","185 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik narrated to me, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of the believer is like the tender stalk of a crop which the wind turns, laying it down at times and straightening it at others, and the example of the disbeliever is like the firm cedar upon its root, nothing bends it until its drying up comes all at once."
"17267","Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah from Ibn Ka'b. 186 - Abu Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Abdus-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, that when his excuse was revealed he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), took his hand and kissed it."
"17268","Hisham ibn Urwah from Ibn Ka'b. 187 - Abu Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Qays and Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat food and then lick his fingers."
"17269","188 - Abu Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated who said: I read to Abu Shurayh, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Ibn Ka'b, from Ka'b, that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eating with three fingers, then I saw him lick his three fingers when he wanted to wipe them."
"17270","Muhammad ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn As'ad ibn Zurarah from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 189 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated. And Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qitrani narrated to us, Abu ar-Rabi' az-Zahrani narrated, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah, Muhammad ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn As'ad ibn Zurarah narrated, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two fierce wolves sent among sheep are not more harmful to them than a man's greed for wealth and honor is to his religion."
"17271","Nafi', freedman of Ibn Umar, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 190 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated. And Abu Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated - both said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from Hajjaj, from Nafi', from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, that a black slave girl of theirs slaughtered a sheep of theirs with a stone, so he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that and he ordered us to eat it."
"17272","Abu az-Zubayr from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 191 - Ahmad ibn Ali al-Barbahari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated, from Abu az-Zubayr, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him and Aws ibn al-Hadathan during the days of at-Tashriq announcing: Verily none shall enter Paradise except a believer, and the days of Mina are days of eating and drinking."
"17273","al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 192 - Shu'ayb ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah az-Zubayri narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Sufyan ibn Hamzah narrated, from Kathir ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik who said: Ka'b ibn Malik said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was reciting poetry, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, O Ka'b ibn Malik! Then Ka'b said something, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, O Ka'b."
"17274","Zayd ibn Aslam from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 193 - Muhammad ibn Asim al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated, Dawud ibn Abdullah al-Ja'fari narrated, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated, from Ibn Ajlan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: A slave girl grazing some sheep of mine came, and a wolf ate a sheep, so I struck the slave girl's face and regretted it. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, if only I knew she was a believer I would free her! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the slave girl: Who am I? She said: The Messenger of Allah! He said: Then who is Allah? She said: The One Who is in heaven! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Free her, for she is a believer."
"17275","Ayyub ibn Nu'man from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 194 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Umayyah al-Wasiti narrated, Ya'qub ibn Muhammad az-Zuhri narrated, Abdul-Aziz ibn Imran narrated, from al-Harith ibn Nu'man, from Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to mark the boundaries of the preserve."
"17276","Abdur-Rahman ibn Sa'd al-Muq'ad from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 195 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Sa'd, that Abdullah ibn Ka'b informed him, from Ka'b, that he narrated to them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to eat with three fingers, and when he finished he would lick them."
"17277","196 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisî narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated, Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi az-Zinad narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Sa'd, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat food and then lick his three fingers with which he had eaten."
"17278","Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata' from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 197 - al-Husayn ibn as-Samayda' al-Antaki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak as-Suri narrated, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Abdul-Aziz ibn Ubaydullah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata', from Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Some people who hear the call on Friday and then do not come to it shall surely cease, or Allah shall surely seal their hearts, then they shall surely be among the heedless."
"17279","198 - Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla ad-Dimashqi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Abdul-Aziz ibn Ubaydullah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata', from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Some people shall surely cease raising their eyes to the sky, or their sight shall surely be snatched away."
"17280","Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. 199 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Khalid ibn Yazid al-Umari narrated, Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah narrated, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever seeks knowledge for one of three things: to dispute with fools by it, or to boast by it against scholars, or to attract the faces of people toward himself - then he said severe words about him."
"17281","Umayrah bint Ubaydullah ibn Ka'b from her father. 200 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated, Ya'qub ibn Muhammad az-Zuhri narrated, Musa ibn Shaybah narrated, from Amr ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik who said: Umayrah bint Ubaydullah ibn Ka'b narrated to me, from her father, from Ka'b who said: When it was the day of Uhud and we reached the mountain pass, I was the first to recognize him, so I said: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! He gestured to me with his hand to be silent, then he dressed me in his armor and wore my armor, and I was struck until I was wounded twenty - or he said: a dozen or so - wounds, and everyone who struck me thought I was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"17282","Ma'bad ibn Ka'b ibn Malik from his father. 201 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi al-Malih narrated, Ya'qub ibn Muhammad az-Zuhri narrated, Asim ibn Suwayd ibn Amir al-Ansari narrated, Abdur-Rahman ibn Ma'bad ibn Ka'b ibn Malik narrated, my father narrated to me, from Ka'b ibn Malik who said: When twelve men came from al-Aqabah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had ordered them that seventy men should meet him the following year, we stayed a year, each of us walking to his companion with pledges of hearing, luggage and food, until seventy men of us met him."
"17283","Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah from Ka'b ibn Malik. 202 - al-Abbas ibn Hamdan al-Hanafi al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ad-Dawraqi narrated, Isma'il ibn Ulayyah narrated, Ibn Awn informed, from Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah who said: Ka'b said: I was never in an expedition with easier mounts and provisions than I was in that expedition. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out I said: I shall prepare tomorrow and then catch up with him! So I busied myself with my preparations but by evening I had not finished. Then I said: I shall exert myself in my preparations tomorrow while the people are still near, and catch up with them, but by evening I had not finished. When it was the third day I busied myself with my preparations but by evening I had not finished, so I said: Far from it, the people have marched for three days! So I stayed behind. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned, people began excusing themselves to him, so I came until I stood before him and said: I was never in an expedition with easier mounts and provisions than I was in this expedition. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from me and ordered the people not to speak to us, and ordered our wives to keep away from us. One day I climbed over a wall, and there was Jabir ibn Abdullah, so I said: O Jabir, I adjure you by Allah, did you ever know me to cheat Allah and His Messenger even once? He stayed silent and would not speak to me. Then one day while I was sitting I heard a man on the mountain path calling: Ka'b, Ka'b! He drew near me and said: Give glad tidings to Ka'b!"
"17284","Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn from Ka'b ibn Malik. 203 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Isa at-Tanukhi narrated, Yahya ibn Ya'la narrated, Yunus ibn Khabbab narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Ka'b ibn Malik who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was pressing a debtor of mine, so he gestured to me with his hand and said: O Ka'b, take half and leave half."
"17285","Umar ibn al-Hakam ibn Thawban from Ka'b ibn Malik. 204 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Mu'addib narrated to us, Surayj ibn an-Nu'man al-Jawhari narrated, Abu Ma'shar narrated, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hakam who said: Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm visited Umar ibn al-Hakam when he was ill, and Abu Bakr said: O Abu Hafs, narrate to us a hadith about which there is no disagreement! He said: Ka'b ibn Malik narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever visits a sick person wades into mercy, and when he sits with him he becomes immersed in it."
"17286","Ali ibn Abi Talhah from Ka'b ibn Malik. 205 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated. And Abu Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated - both said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Ali ibn Abi Talhah, from Ka'b ibn Malik, that he wanted to marry a Jewish or Christian woman, so he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he forbade him and said: She will not keep you chaste."
"17287","Umm Humaydah bint Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik from Ka'b. 206 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Harish narrated, Abu Dawud narrated, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated, Humaydah bint Abdullah ibn Ka'b narrated to me, from her mother, from Ka'b ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit and said: I ascended it having known Laylat al-Qadr, then it was made forgotten to me, so seek it in the last ten nights, in the odd nights."
"17288","Ka'b ibn Ujrah al-Ansari, kunya Abu Muhammad. 207 - Abu az-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated who said: Ka'b ibn Ujrah died in the year fifty-two, at the age of seventy-seven years."
"17289","208 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated who said: Ka'b ibn Ujrah died in the year fifty-two, at the age of seventy-five years."
"17290","What Abdullah ibn Umar narrated from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 209 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated, Abu Ma'shar narrated, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ka'b ibn Ujrah: Perhaps the vermin of your head have harmed you? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Shave your head, and offer a cow as sacrifice, marking it or garlanding it."
"17291","210 - Abu Zur'ah Abdur-Rahman ibn Amr ad-Dimashqi narrated to us, Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated, Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated who said: Abdul-Wahhab ibn Bukht said: Nafi' informed me, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that Ka'b ibn Ujrah shaved his head, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to ransom, and he ransomed himself with a cow."
"17292","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 211 - Abu az-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated, Maymun ibn Yahya ibn Muslim narrated to me, Makhramah ibn Bukayr narrated, from his father who said: I heard Amr ibn Shu'ayb say: I heard Shu'ayb narrating from Abdullah ibn Amr who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ka'b ibn Ujrah while he was in ihram: Have the creatures of your head harmed you? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Then shave and ransom, either by fasting three days or feeding six poor persons, each poor person a sa' of dates, or else sacrifice a sheep."
"17293","Tariq ibn Shihab from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 212 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qatawani narrated - both said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated, from Ghalib ibn Yahya, from Ayyub ibn A'idh at-Ta'i, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: I seek refuge for you with Allah, O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from rulers who will come after me. Whoever frequents their doors, believes them in their lies and helps them in their injustice is not of me and I am not of him, and he shall come to me at the Basin. O Ka'b, prayer is a proof, charity extinguishes sin as water extinguishes fire, fasting is a strong shield. O Ka'b, no flesh grown from unlawful earnings shall enter Paradise. O Ka'b, no flesh grows from unlawful earnings except that the Fire is more deserving of it."
"17294","Abu Wa'il Shaqiq ibn Salamah from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 213 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humayd ar-Razi narrated to me, Harun ibn al-Mughirah narrated, Amr ibn Abi Qays narrated, from az-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Abu Wa'il who said: I met Ka'b ibn Ujrah in the market and asked him about shaving his head, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me and wiped my head and said: This is enough as harm - so the lice scattered. He said: Go, shave, and give charity upon six poor persons."
"17295","Zayd ibn Wahb from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 214 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Hurayth al-Marwazi narrated to me, al-Fadl ibn Musa as-Sinani narrated, from Ubaydah ad-Dabbi, from Abu Malik al-Ansari, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship or eases for him, Allah shall shade him in His shade on a day when there is no shade except His shade."
"17296","Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Ka'b ibn Ujrah; Mujahid from Abdur-Rahman. 215 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisî narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Abi Abbad al-Makki narrated, Muslim ibn Khalid narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Mujahid, Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him while his vermin were falling onto his face, and said: Do your vermin harm you? He said: Yes. So he ordered him to shave while they were at al-Hudaybiyah, and it was not yet clear to them that they would shave there while they still hoped to enter Mecca. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down the ransom, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to feed a faraq among six poor persons, or offer a sheep, or fast three days."
"17297","216 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi and Abu Yazid al-Qaratisî narrated to us, both said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated. And Abdur-Rahman ibn Mu'awiyah al-Utbi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated - both said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated, Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Aban ibn Salih, that Mujahid ibn Jabr al-Makki narrated to him, that Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla al-Ansari - of the people of Kufa - narrated to him, that Ka'b ibn Ujrah, brother of Banu Salim, suffered harm in his head while he was at al-Hudaybiyah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), besieged, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to fast three days, or feed six poor persons, or slaughter a sheep, and concerning him this verse was revealed."
"17298","217 - Ahmad ibn Ali al-Barbahari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated, from Abu az-Zubayr, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah al-Ansari, that he narrated to him that he entered ihram in Dhu al-Qa'dah and his head became lousy, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him while he was kindling fire under a pot of his, and said to him: It seems the vermin of your head harm you? He said: Yes! He said: Shave and offer a sacrificial animal. He said: I cannot find a sacrificial animal! He said: Then feed six poor persons. He said: I cannot! He said: Then fast three days - so I shaved and fasted."
"17299","218 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Khuza'i al-Asbahani narrated to us, Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, concerning: Then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice - similar to the hadith of Abdullah ibn Ma'qil from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. Abu al-Qasim said: We did not write it except from al-Khuza'i."
"17300","219 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated - both said: Hushaym narrated, Abu Bishr narrated, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: This verse was revealed concerning me; we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Hudaybiyah in ihram, besieged by the polytheists, and I had thick hair, and vermin began falling onto my face. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed and said: Do the vermin of your head harm you? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Then shave. He said: Then this verse was revealed: Then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice. He said: Fasting is three days, charity is upon six poor persons, and the sacrifice is a sheep."
"17301","220 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Mutarrif ibn Abdullah al-Madani narrated. And Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated. And Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated. And Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Mus'ab ibn Abdullah az-Zubayri narrated to me. And Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated - all of them from Malik ibn Anas, from Humayd ibn Qays, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps your vermin have harmed you! I said to him: Yes, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Shave your head, and fast three days, or feed six poor persons, or sacrifice a sheep."
"17302","221 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Mutarrif ibn Abdullah al-Madani narrated. And Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated. And Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated. And Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated. And Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Mus'ab ibn Abdullah az-Zubayri narrated - all of them from Malik, from Abdul-Karim ibn Malik al-Jazari, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and lice harmed him in his head, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to shave his head, and said: Fast three days, or feed six poor persons, two mudds for each person, or sacrifice a sheep; whichever of these you do will suffice for you."
"17303","222 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated, from Abdul-Karim, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me during an umrah when he saw lice in my head: O Ka'b, do your vermin harm you? I said: Yes! He said: Then shave, and slaughter a sheep, or fast three days, or feed six poor persons."
"17304","223 - Hafs ibn Umar ar-Raqqi narrated to us, Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17305","224 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad az-Ziyadi narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated, Shibl ibn Abbad narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me at al-Hudaybiyah while I was cooking a pot and lice were falling onto my face, and he said: Do the vermin of your head harm you? I said: Yes! He said: Then shave, then fast three days, or slaughter a sheep, or feed a faraq among six poor persons."
"17306","225 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Abdul-Warith narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Ka'b ibn Ujrah while the vermin of his head had harmed him, so he ordered him to shave, then revelation came to him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you wish, fast three days, and if you wish, give a faraq among six poor persons, and if you wish, slaughter a sheep."
"17307","226 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated, Warqa' narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him while his lice were falling onto his face, and said: Do your vermin harm you? He said: Yes! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to shave at al-Hudaybiyah, while it was not yet clear to them that they would shave there while they still hoped to enter Mecca. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down the ransom, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to feed a faraq among six poor persons, or offer a sheep, or fast three days."
"17308","227 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, al-Abbas ibn al-Walid an-Narsi narrated. And Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Azhar ar-Razi narrated - both said: Muslim ibn Khalid az-Zanji narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was at al-Hudaybiyah cooking a pot and lice were falling onto my face, and he said: Do the vermin of your head harm you? I said: Yes! He said: Then shave, then fast three days, or slaughter a sheep, or feed a faraq among six poor persons."
"17309","228 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Yahya ibn Khalaf Abu Salamah al-Jawbari narrated, Abu Asim narrated, from Isa ibn Maymun, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17310","229 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abdur-Razzaq narrated, Ma'mar informed, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me while my lice were falling onto my face, and said: Do these vermin of yours harm you? I said: Yes! He said: Then he ordered me to shave my head while they were at al-Hudaybiyah, while it was not yet clear to them that they would shave there while they still hoped to enter Mecca. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down the ransom, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to feed a faraq among six poor persons, or fast three days, or slaughter a sheep."
"17311","230 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Bishr ibn al-Fadl narrated, Ibn Awn narrated, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: Concerning me this verse was revealed. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: Come near! So I drew near - two or three times - and he said: Do your vermin harm you? I said: Yes! So he ordered me with fasting, or charity, or whatever sacrifice was feasible."
"17312","231 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna ibn Mu'adh al-Anbari narrated to us, my father narrated, Ibn Awn narrated, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: Concerning me this verse was revealed. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: Come near! So I drew near - two or three times - and he said: Do your vermin harm you? Ibn Awn said: And I think he said: Yes! So he ordered me with fasting, or charity, or whatever sacrifice was feasible. Ibn Awn said: I forgot what he said about fasting and charity, so I mentioned it to Ayyub as-Sakhtiyani and he said: I heard this hadith from him. I said: How is it? He said: Fasting three days, or charity upon six poor persons, or a sacrifice - whatever is feasible. And Ayyub used to say: He said: Do the vermin of your head harm you?"
"17313","232 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami and Abu ar-Rabi' az-Zahrani narrated to me. And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Khalid ibn Khidash narrated - all said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Ayyub who said: I heard Mujahid narrating from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me at the time of al-Hudaybiyah while I was kindling fire under a cooking pot of mine, and lice were scattering over me, and he said: Do the vermin of your head harm you? I said: Yes! He said: Shave, and feed six poor persons three sa's, or fast three days, or sacrifice."
"17314","233 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Ayyub, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was kindling fire under a pot, and said: Have the vermin of your head harmed you? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to shave, and fast three days, or feed a faraq among six poor persons, or sacrifice. Sufyan said: Then this verse was revealed: Then whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of his head."
"17315","234 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Sulayman ibn al-Hasan al-Attar narrated to us, both said: Abdul-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Ayyub. And Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me. And Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated - both said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated, Ayyub narrated, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me at the time of al-Hudaybiyah while I was kindling fire under a pot of mine, and lice were being scattered onto my face, and he said: Do the vermin of your head harm you? I said: Yes! He said: Then shave, and fast three days, or feed six poor persons, or sacrifice an animal - and the wording is that of the hadith of Ibn Ulayyah."
"17316","235 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abdur-Razzaq narrated, Ma'mar informed, from Ayyub, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to shave his head, and sacrifice an animal, or fast three days, or feed a faraq among six poor persons."
"17317","236 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih and Ayyub, and Humayd and Abdul-Karim, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was at al-Hudaybiyah before entering Mecca, in ihram, kindling fire under a pot of his, while lice were falling onto his face, and he said: Do these vermin of yours harm you? He said: Yes! He said: Then shave your head, and feed a faraq among six poor persons - and the faraq is three sa's - or fast three days, or sacrifice an animal. Ibn Abi Najih said: Or slaughter a sheep."
"17318","237 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Waki' narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Amr ibn Dinar and Ayyub, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was kindling fire under a pot of his, while lice were falling onto his face, and he said: Do your vermin harm you? He said: Yes! He said: Then shave your head, and sacrifice, or fast three days, or feed three sa's among six poor persons."
"17319","238 - Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn al-Harish al-Ahwazi narrated to us, Isa ibn Shadhan narrated, Abu Hammam as-Salt ibn Muhammad al-Kharaki narrated, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated, from Qatadah who said: Salih Abu al-Khalil narrated, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me at the time of al-Hudaybiyah while I was kindling fire under a pot of mine, while the vermin of my head were falling, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do the vermin of your head harm you? I said: Yes! He said: Shave, and fast three days, or feed six poor persons, or slaughter an animal."
"17320","239 - Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malati narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated, Sayf ibn Sulayman narrated who said: I heard Mujahid saying: Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over him at al-Hudaybiyah while his head was dripping with lice, and said: Do the vermin of your head harm you? He said: Yes! He said: Shave your head. Then he ordered me to fast three days, or feed six poor persons, or sacrifice whatever was feasible."
"17321","240 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn as-Sabbah ar-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Sayf ibn Sulayman, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17322","241 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Abdul-Warith narrated, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim, and bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17323","242 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn as-Sabbah al-Bazzar narrated to me, Abdul-Majid ibn Abdul-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that he said to him: Shall I not present you a gift? He said: Yes indeed. He said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17324","ash-Sha'bi from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. 243 - Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated, Dawud ibn Abi Hind informed, from ash-Sha'bi, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him while he was in ihram with thick hair, with a louse or nits at the root and top of every hair, and said: This is indeed harm. Do you have an animal to sacrifice? He said: No! He said: Then go or cut it, and if you wish fast three days, or feed six poor persons, between every two poor persons a sa' of dates."
"17325","244 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj as-Sami narrated to me. And Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Sulayman ibn al-Hasan al-Attar and Zakariyya ibn Yahya as-Saji narrated to us, all said: Abdul-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated - both said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from ash-Sha'bi, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If you wish, sacrifice an animal, and if you wish, fast three days, and if you wish, feed three sa's of dates to six poor persons."
"17326","245 - Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated to us, Mu'alla ibn Asad al-Ammi narrated, Wuhayb narrated, from Dawud, from Amir who said: Ka'b ibn Ujrah narrated to me who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me in the year of al-Hudaybiyah while I had thick hair, with lice and nits from the root of every hair to its tip, and he said: Is this not harm? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Then shave, and slaughter, or fast three days, or give charity of three sa's of dates upon six poor persons."
"17327","246 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Bishr ibn al-Fadl narrated, from Dawud, from Amir, Ka'b ibn Ujrah narrated to me who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me in the year of al-Hudaybiyah. Then he mentioned similar to the hadith of Wuhayb, and neither Wuhayb nor Bishr inserted anyone between ash-Sha'bi and Ka'b ibn Abi Layla."
"17328","247 - Yusuf the judge narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated, Zuhayr ibn Ishaq narrated, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from ash-Sha'bi who said: Ka'b ibn Ujrah narrated to me who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by at al-Hudaybiyah. Then he mentioned similar to it."
"17329","248 - Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated, Abdur-Razzaq narrated, Ma'mar informed, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from ash-Sha'bi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it. And Ma'mar added in his hadith: Between every two poor persons a sa'."
"17330","249 - al-Hasan ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Affan narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Dawud, from ash-Sha'bi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah - with this hadith."
"17331","Abu Qilabah from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 250 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Sa'id and Muhammad ibn Amr ibn al-Abbas al-Bahili narrated to me - both said: Abdul-Wahhab ibn Abdul-Majid ath-Thaqafi narrated, from Khalid al-Hadhdha'. And Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Shabib al-Assal al-Asbahani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Amr al-Bajali narrated, al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Ansari narrated, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilabah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him while the vermin of his head had harmed him: Slaughter a sheep as a sacrifice, or fast three days, or give charity of three sa's upon six poor persons."
"17332","251 - Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn al-Musawir al-Jawhari narrated to us, Affan narrated, Wuhayb narrated, from Khalid, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me at the time of al-Hudaybiyah while I had abundant hair, and said: It seems the vermin of your head harm you? I said: Yes! He said: Shave it, and slaughter a sheep, or fast three days, or give charity of three sa's among six poor persons."
"17333","252 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abdul-A'la narrated, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilabah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him while the vermin of his head had harmed him: Slaughter an animal, or fast three days, or give charity of three sa's upon six poor persons."
"17334","253 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, both said: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated, Khalid informed, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him at the time of al-Hudaybiyah and said: Have the vermin of your head harmed you? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Shave, then slaughter a sheep, or fast three days, or feed three sa's of dates upon six poor persons."
"17335","254 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hushaym narrated, Khalid al-Hadhdha' informed, from Abu Qilabah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: I became lousy until I thought that upon every hair of my head there were lice from its root to its tip, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me when he saw me to shave, and this verse was revealed, and he said: Feed six poor persons with three sa's of dates."
"17336","Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abdur-Rahman from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 255 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated, Abdur-Rahman ibn Abdul-Aziz al-Umami informed, Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abdur-Rahman narrated to me, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw vermin on his face and said: I see, O Ka'b, that the vermin of your head have harmed you? He said: Yes, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Shave your head, and feed six poor persons, or fast three days, or sacrifice a sheep."
"17337","Ata' al-Khurasani from Ibn Abi Layla from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 256 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated. And Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Mus'ab ibn Abdullah az-Zubayri narrated to me. And Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated - all of them from Malik, from Ata' ibn Abdullah al-Khurasani, a man in the Zam market at Kufa narrated to me, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me while I was blowing under a pot for my companions while my head and beard had filled with lice, so he took hold of my forehead, then said: Shave this, and fast three days, or feed six poor persons. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew that I had nothing with which to sacrifice."
"17338","al-Hakam ibn Utaybah from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. Chapter. 257 - Muhammad ibn Hashim al-Ba'labakki narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated, Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he passed by him during the umrah of al-Hudaybiyah while the vermin of his head had multiplied, and said to him: O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, this is indeed harm! He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, so do I have a concession? He said: Sacrifice an animal, or fast three days, or feed six poor persons."
"17339","258 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Sa'id narrated, my uncle narrated, my father narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Aban ibn Salih narrated to me, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah al-Ansari then of Banu Salim who said: Vermin afflicted me in my head while I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of al-Hudaybiyah until I feared for my sight. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down: Then whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of his head, then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called me and said: Shave your head, and fast three days, or feed six poor persons a faraq of raisins, or sacrifice a sheep. So I shaved my head and then sacrificed."
"17340","259 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn as-Sabbah ar-Raqqi narrated to us, Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Mansur, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Glorifications following prayers whose sayer shall not be disappointed: Glory be to Allah thirty-three times, praise be to Allah thirty-three times, and Allah is Most Great thirty-three times - at the end of every obligatory prayer."
"17341","260 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, both said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated, Amr ibn Qays narrated, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glorifications following prayers whose sayers shall not be disappointed: glorify Allah at the end of every prayer thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-three times, and magnify Him thirty-four times."
"17342","261 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Ja'far ibn Awn narrated, from Ibn Layla, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17343","262 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Sahl al-Ahwazi narrated, Abu Ahmad az-Zubayri narrated, Hamzah az-Zayyat narrated, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glorifications following prayers whose sayer or doer shall not be disappointed: thirty-three glorifications, thirty-three praises, and thirty-four magnifications - at the end of every prayer."
"17344","263 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated, Ibrahim ibn Uthman narrated, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glorifications following prayers whose sayer or doer shall not be disappointed: thirty-three times glorification, thirty-three times praise, and thirty-four times magnification - at the end of every prayer."
"17345","264 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated, Ibrahim ibn Uthman Abu Shaybah narrated, al-Hakam informed me who said: I heard Ibn Abi Layla saying: I heard Ka'b ibn Ujrah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Glorifications following prayers whose sayer or doer shall not be disappointed: glorify Allah thirty-three times, praise Allah thirty-three times, and magnify Allah thirty-four times - at the end of every prayer."
"17346","265 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Muhammad ibn Khalid ar-Rasibi narrated to us, both said: Muhammad ibn Hassan al-Azraq narrated, Shu'ayb ibn Harb narrated, Shu'bah and Hamzah az-Zayyat and Malik ibn Mighwal narrated, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah; as for Malik and Hamzah, they raised it as marfu', both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glorifications following prayers whose sayer or doer shall not be disappointed: glorify at the end of every prayer thirty-three times, praise thirty-three times, and magnify thirty-four times."
"17347","Chapter. 266 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ad-Dabari narrated to us, from Abdur-Razzaq, from ath-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17348","267 - Muhammad ibn an-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated, Za'idah narrated, from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, this greeting of peace upon you we have known, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17349","268 - Allan ibn Abd as-Samad narrated to us, his uncle narrated, al-Qasim ibn Dinar narrated, Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated, Shayban narrated, from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17350","269 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to me, Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated, from Amr ibn Qays, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it. Abdullah ibn Ahmad said: Uthman erred in this; this is rather the hadith: Glorifications following prayers whose sayers shall not be disappointed."
"17351","270 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi narrated to us, Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated. And Ahmad ibn al-Qasim al-Jawhari narrated to us, Affan narrated. And Yusuf the judge narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated. And Umar ibn Hafs as-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated - all said: Shu'bah narrated. And Uthman ibn Umar ad-Dabbi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated, Shu'bah informed, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that he said: Shall I not present you a gift? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and we said: O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And the wording is that of the hadith of Sulayman ibn Harb."
"17352","271 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Khallad ibn Yahya narrated, Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah who said: I heard Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla saying: I heard Ka'b ibn Ujrah say: When this verse was revealed: Verily Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet; O you who believe, send blessings upon him and greet him with peace, we said: O Messenger of Allah, this greeting of peace we have known, how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are the Praiseworthy, the Glorious, and send blessings upon us along with them, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are the Praiseworthy, the Glorious, and bless us along with them, and peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings."
"17353","272 - Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malati narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated. And Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, ar-Rabi' ibn Yahya al-Ushnani narrated - both said: Malik ibn Mighwal narrated, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: Shall I not present a gift? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying. I said: Yes indeed. He said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, we know - or he said: You taught us - how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17354","273 - Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated. And Muhammad ibn Mu'adh al-Halabi narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated - both said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Messenger of Allah, this greeting of peace upon you we have known, so how do we send blessings? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17355","274 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Imran ibn Abi Layla narrated, my father narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: When: Verily Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet was revealed, I said: O Messenger of Allah, the greeting of peace upon you we have known, so how do we send blessings upon you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17356","275 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ad-Dabari narrated to us, from Abdur-Razzaq, from Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17357","276 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated, Abadah ibn Sulayman narrated. And al-Husayn ibn Ja'far al-Qattat narrated to us, Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated, Ali ibn Mushir narrated. And Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated. And Abdur-Rahman ibn Salm ar-Razi narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated - both said: Waki' narrated - all of them from Mis'ar, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17358","277 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud al-Misri narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi narrated, Mis'ar ibn Kidam and Hamzah az-Zayyat narrated. And Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Sahl narrated, Abu Ahmad az-Zubayri narrated, Hamzah az-Zayyat narrated, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17359","278 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated, Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated, from al-Ajlah, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: When: Verily Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet was revealed, we said: The greeting of peace upon you we have known, so how do we send blessings upon you? Then he mentioned similar to it."
"17360","279 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Jundaysaburi narrated, my father narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abi Ubaydah, from his father - meaning Mujja'ah ibn az-Zubayr - from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17361","280 - Hafs ibn Umar as-Sabbah narrated to us, Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated, Sufyan narrated. And Bakr ibn Ahmad ibn Muqbil narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khalaf at-Taymi al-Kufi narrated, Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, this greeting of peace upon you we have known, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17362","281 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqah al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Khali al-Himsi narrated, my father narrated, Salamah al-Awsi narrated, from Ali ibn Salih, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that he said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): This greeting of peace upon you we have known, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And he did not mention Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla in the hadith."
"17363","282 - Muhammad ibn Mu'adh al-Halabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Hammam narrated, Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: A man passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw his strength and energy, and said: O Messenger of Allah, if only this were in the path of Allah! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he went out striving for his small children, then he is in the path of Allah, and if he went out striving for two aged elderly parents, then he is in the path of Allah, and if he strives for himself to keep it chaste, then he is in the path of Allah, and if he went out for show and boasting, then he is in the path of Satan."
"17364","Abdullah ibn Isa ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. 283 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn A'ishah narrated, Abdul-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated, Abu Farwah Muslim ibn Salim narrated, Abdullah ibn Isa ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla who said: Ka'b ibn Ujrah met me and said: Shall I not present you a gift? I said: Yes indeed, present it to me! He said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, how do we send blessings upon you, people of the House? For Allah the Mighty and Majestic has taught us how to greet you with peace? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17365","Abdullah ibn Abdullah ar-Razi from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. 284 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad Abu Ja'far at-Tirmidhi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Salim al-Qazzaz narrated, Ubaydah ibn al-Aswad narrated, from al-Qasim ibn al-Walid, from Abdullah ibn Abdullah ar-Razi, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, who said to him: Shall I not present you a gift? I said: Yes indeed. He said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. az-Zubayr ibn Adi from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla."
"17366","285 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn as-Sabbah narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated, Sufyan narrated, from az-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17367","Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. 286 - Hafs ibn Umar ibn as-Sabbah ar-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: When: Verily Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet was revealed - the hadith."
"17368","287 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me. And Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated - both said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated. And Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abu Khaythamah Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to me. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated - both said: Jarir narrated, both from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: When this verse was revealed: Verily Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet; O you who believe, send blessings upon him and greet him with peace, we asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about sending blessings upon him. We said: We know the greeting of peace upon you, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim and upon the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. He said: And we say: And upon us along with them."
"17369","288 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated. And Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, both said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated - both said: Hushaym narrated, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17370","289 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to me, Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17371","290 - Abu Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17372","Abu Sa'd al-Baqqal from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. 291 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Muharibi narrated, Ali ibn Hashim narrated, from Abu Sa'd al-Baqqal, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: We said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The greeting of peace upon you we have known, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17373","Isma'il ibn Abdur-Rahman as-Suddi from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 292 - Abdullah ibn Ali al-Jarudi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Hafs narrated, my father narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated, from as-Suddi, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, this greeting of peace upon you we know how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim. Verily You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"17374","Abu Miskin al-Ansari from Ibn Abi Layla. 293 - Abu Amir Muhammad ibn Ibrahim an-Nahwi as-Suri narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abdur-Rahman ad-Dimashqi narrated, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, from Isa ibn Musa, from Urwah ibn Ruwaym al-Lakhmi, Abu Miskin al-Ansari narrated, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: One day we sat in front of the houses of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, in a group of us from the Ansar, a group of the Muhajirun, and a group of Banu Hashim, and we disputed about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - which of us is closest to him and most beloved to him? We said: We, the Ansar, believed in him, followed him, fought alongside him with his squadron against his enemy, so we are closest to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and most beloved of them to him! Our Muhajir brothers said: We are those who emigrated to Allah and His Messenger, leaving tribes, families and wealth, and we were present at what you were present at and witnessed what you witnessed, so we are closest of people to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and most beloved of them to him! Our brothers of Banu Hashim said: We are the clan of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); we were present at what you were present at and witnessed what you witnessed, so we are closest to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and most beloved of them to him! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and turned to us and said: You are saying something. So we repeated our saying, and he said to the Ansar: You have spoken the truth; who can refute this against you? We informed him of what our Muhajir brothers had said, and he said: They spoke the truth and did well; who can refute this against them? We informed him of what Banu Hashim had said, and he said: They spoke the truth and did well; who can refute this against them? Then he said: Shall I not judge among you? We said: Yes indeed, may our fathers and mothers be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: As for you, O Ansar, I am but your brother. They said: Allah is Most Great, we have taken him, by the Lord of the Ka'bah! As for you, O Muhajirun, I am but of you. They said: Allah is Most Great, we have taken him, by the Lord of the Ka'bah! As for you, Banu Hashim, you are of me and toward me. So we stood, all of us pleased and delighted with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"17375","Asim al-Adawi from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 294 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated. And Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated. And Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, both said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated - all said: Sufyan narrated, from Abu Husayn, from ash-Sha'bi, from Asim al-Adawi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were sitting upon a cushion of leather, and said: There shall be rulers after me. Whoever enters upon them, believes them in their lies and helps them in their injustice is not of me and I am not of him, and shall never come to me at the Basin. Whoever does not believe them in their lies nor help them in their injustice is of me and I am of him, and shall come to me at the Basin."
"17376","295 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Shabib al-Assal al-Asbahani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Amr al-Bajali narrated, Qays ibn ar-Rabi' narrated, from Abu Husayn, from ash-Sha'bi, from Asim al-Adawi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while I was the seventh of seven, and said: There shall be rulers after me - then he mentioned similar to it."
"17377","296 - Hafs ibn Umar ar-Raqqi narrated to us, Fayd ibn al-Fadl narrated, Mis'ar ibn Kidam informed, from Abu Husayn, from ash-Sha'bi, from Asim al-Adawi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were nine, five of the Arabs and four of the non-Arabs, and said: Listen, have you not heard that there shall be rulers after me? Whoever enters upon them, believes them in their lies and helps them in their injustice is not of me and I am not of him, and shall not come to me at the Basin. Whoever does not enter upon them nor help them in their injustice is of me and I am of him, and shall come to me at the Basin."
"17378","297 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Dinar narrated, from al-Fadl ibn Muwaffaq. And Abu Hanifah Muhammad ibn Hanifah al-Wasiti narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Sahl al-Ahwazi narrated, Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Kufi narrated - both said: Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated, from Abu Husayn, from ash-Sha'bi, from Asim al-Adawi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us - then he mentioned similar to it."
"17379","298 - Abdullah ibn al-Abbas at-Tayalisi and Abdullah ibn Ali al-Jarudi an-Naysaburi narrated to us, both said: Ahmad ibn Hafs narrated, my father narrated to me who said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated, from Aqil - a man of Banu Ja'dah - from Abu Ishaq, from Asim al-Adawi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah protect you from rulers who will come after me! I said: And what is that? He said: Whoever enters upon them, believes them in their lies and helps them in their injustice is not of me and I am not of him, and shall never come to me at the Basin. Whoever does not enter upon them, nor believe them in their lies, nor help them in their injustice is of me and I am of him, and shall come to my Basin. No flesh grown from unlawful earnings shall enter Paradise, and every flesh grown from unlawful earnings, the Fire is more deserving of it. People go out in the morning in two ways: one wasting himself and destroying it, and one ransoming himself and freeing it. Prayer is a proof, fasting is a shield, and charity extinguishes sin as water extinguishes fire."
"17380","Abdullah ibn Ma'qil ibn Muqarrin al-Muzani from Ka'b. 299 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated. And Umar ibn Hafs as-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated. And Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Khuza'i al-Asbahani narrated to us, Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi narrated. And Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated who said: Abu al-Walid at-Tayalisi narrated - all said: Shu'bah narrated, from Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani who said: I heard Abdullah ibn Ma'qil narrating from Ka'b ibn Ujrah concerning this verse: Then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice. He said: I was carried to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while lice were scattering onto my face, and he said: I did not think the ailment had reached with you what I see. Do you have a sheep? I said: No. He said: Then fast three days, or feed such and such number of poor persons, each poor person half a sa' of wheat."
"17381","300 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Abu Awanah narrated, from Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil who said: We were sitting in the mosque when Ka'b ibn Ujrah sat with us and said: Concerning me this verse was revealed: Then whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of his head, then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice. I said: How was your affair? He said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in ihram, and lice fell into my head, beard and mustache until they reached my eyebrows. That was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: I did not think this had reached with you. Call the barber. So he called the barber and he shaved his head. He said: Do you have an animal to sacrifice? I said: No. He said: Then fast three days, or feed six poor persons, between every two poor persons a sa' - it was revealed concerning me specifically and for people generally."
"17382","301 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Qays ibn ar-Rabi' narrated, from Abdur-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar to it."
"17383","302 - Abdur-Rahman ibn Salm narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated, from his father. And Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, both said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah who said: Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani narrated, Abdullah ibn Ma'qil narrated to me, that Ka'b ibn Ujrah narrated to him that he set out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in ihram, and his head and beard became lousy. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent for him, called the barber and he shaved his head. Then he said: Do you have an animal to sacrifice? He said: I cannot afford it. So he ordered him to fast three days, or feed six poor persons, for each poor person a sa'. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down concerning him specifically: Then whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of his head, then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice. Then it became for Muslims generally."
"17384","303 - Abu Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Qays ibn ar-Rabi' narrated, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar. And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated, Ash'ath ibn Sawwar narrated. And Abdur-Rahman ibn Salm ar-Razi narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated, Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from ash-Sha'bi, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: I entered ihram from al-Hudaybiyah with thick hair, became lousy and nits seized me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me and said: Shave, shave! So I shaved. He said: Do you have a sacrificial animal? I said: I cannot find it! He said: It is whatever is feasible. I said: I cannot find it! He said: Then fast three days, or feed six poor persons half a sa' of wheat. He said: Concerning me this verse was revealed: Then whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of his head. And the wording is that of the hadith of Ibn Fudayl."
"17385","304 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Fudayl al-Jazari narrated, Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Dawud al-Harrani narrated, my father narrated to me, from Abdul-Karim al-Jazari, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that a blind man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I hear the call to prayer, but perhaps I find no guide! He said: When you hear the call, answer the caller of Allah."
"17386","305 - Abdullah ibn Sa'id ibn Yahya ar-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Farwah Yazid ibn Muhammad ibn Sinan ar-Rahawi narrated, my father narrated to me, from his father, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: A blind man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I hear the call to prayer, but perhaps I find no guide and it is hard for me. Shall I take a place of prayer in my house? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does the call reach you? He said: Yes. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you hear the call, go out."
"17387","Ibrahim, keeper of Abdul-Hamid's kitchen, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 306 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Zubayd, from a man called Ibrahim, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There shall be rulers over you."
"17388","Amir ash-Sha'bi from Ka'b ibn Ujrah. 307 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Yazid at-Tahhan narrated. And Ali ibn Ishaq al-Wazir al-Asbahani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ali as-Suddi narrated - both said: Sa'd ibn Khuthaym narrated, from Muhammad ibn Khalid ad-Dabbi, from as-Sari ibn Isma'il, from ash-Sha'bi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of your men among the people of Paradise? They said: Yes indeed, O Messenger of Allah! He said: The Prophet is in Paradise, the martyr is in Paradise, the truthful is in Paradise, the newborn is in Paradise, and a man who visits his brother in a distant part of town is in Paradise. Shall I not inform you of your women among the people of Paradise? They said: Yes indeed, O Messenger of Allah! He said: The loving, fertile one who, if she wrongs or is wronged, says: This is my forelock in your hand; I shall not taste sleep until you are pleased."
"17389","308 - Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated. And Yusuf the judge narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated. And Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Mu'addib narrated to us, Surayj ibn an-Nu'man narrated - all said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated, from Zubayd, from ash-Sha'bi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while I was the seventh of seven, five of us from Quraysh, and said: Will you not listen? He said it three times - There shall be rulers after me. Whoever enters upon them, believes them in their lies and helps them in their injustice is not of me and I am not of him, and I am free of him. Whoever does not enter upon them, nor believe them in their lies, nor help them in their injustice is of me and I am of him, and shall meet me and be with me."
"17390","309 - al-Husayn ibn Alluyah al-Qattan narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Isa al-Attar narrated, Tahir ibn Hammad narrated, from Sufyan, from Khalid, from ash-Sha'bi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were seven upon a cushion, and said: O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, I seek refuge for you with Allah from the rule of fools! I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the rule of fools? He said: Rulers who will come after me. Whoever enters upon them, believes them in their lies and helps them in their injustice is not of me and I am not of him, and shall never come to me at the Basin. Whoever does not come to them, nor believe them in their lies, nor help them in their injustice is of me and I am of him, and shall come to me at the Basin. O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, no flesh grown from unlawful earnings shall ever enter Paradise; the Fire is more deserving of it. O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, prayer is light, charity is proof, fasting is a shield, and people go out in the morning in two ways: one going out selling himself and freeing his neck, and one going out selling himself and destroying his neck."
"17391","310 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Sahl narrated, Yazid ibn Harun narrated, Ubayd ibn Mughith informed, from ash-Sha'bi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There shall be rulers over you. Whoever frequents their doors, helps them in their injustice and believes them in their lies is not of me and I am not of him, and shall never come to me at the Basin. Whoever does not frequent their doors, nor believe them in their lies, nor help them in their injustice is of me and I am of him, and shall come to me at the Basin."
"17392","311 - Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Husayn as-Sabuni at-Tustari narrated to us, Zurayq ibn as-Suht narrated, Abu an-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated, Isa ibn al-Musayyab al-Bajali narrated, from ash-Sha'bi, Ka'b ibn Ujrah informed me who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were seven men, four of our freedmen and three of our Arabs, leaning our backs against his mosque, and said: What has seated you? We said: We sat waiting for prayer. He said: He paused briefly, then turned to us with his face and said: Do you know what your Lord says? We said: No. He said: Your Lord says: Whoever prays the prayer at its time, guards it and does not neglect it belittling its right, has a covenant from Me that I shall admit him to Paradise. Whoever does not pray it at its time nor guard it, belittling its right, has no covenant from Me; if I wish I shall punish him, and if I wish I shall forgive him."
"17393","312 - Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated, from Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad, from as-Sari ibn Isma'il, from ash-Sha'bi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: What seats you? We said: We await prayer. He said: Do you know what your Lord says? We said: No. He said: He says: Whoever prays the prayers at their time and does not neglect them belittling their right has from Me that I shall admit him to Paradise. If he does not pray them at their time and neglects them belittling their right, he has no covenant with Me; if I wish I shall punish him, and if I wish I shall pardon him."
"17394","313 - Abu Ubaydah Abdul-Warith ibn Ibrahim al-Askari narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Attar al-Askari narrated, Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated, from Miskin ibn Salih, from ash-Sha'bi, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us to the mosque while we were seven men, four of the Arabs and three of the freedmen, and sat and said: Do you know what your Lord said? We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Whoever prays the prayer at its time and does not abandon it belittling it shall meet Me on the Day of Resurrection with a covenant from Me to admit him to Paradise. Whoever does not pray it at its time and abandons it belittling it shall meet Me on the Day of Resurrection with no covenant from Me; if I wish I shall punish him, and if I wish I shall forgive him."
"17395","Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah from his father Ka'b. 314 - Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated, Abdur-Rahman ibn an-Nu'man al-Ansari narrated, Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah narrated, from his father, from Ka'b who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were seven, three Arabs and four of our freedmen. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us from one of his rooms until he sat with us, and said: What seats you here? We said: We await prayer. He tapped the ground with his fingers, then lowered his head for a while, then raised his head to us and said: Do you know what your Lord the Mighty and Majestic says? We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: He says: Whoever prays the prayers at their time and establishes their limits has a covenant that I shall admit him to Paradise. Whoever does not pray the prayers at their time nor establish their limits has no covenant with Me; if I wish I shall admit him to the Fire, and if I wish I shall admit him to Paradise."
"17396","315 - Ali ibn Abdul-Aziz narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated. And Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated - both said: Muhammad ibn Hilal - freedman of Banu Jumah - al-Madini narrated, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from his father, from his grandfather Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day to the pulpit, and when he ascended a step he said: Amin, then ascended the next and said: Amin, then ascended the third and said: Amin. When he descended from the pulpit and finished, we said: O Messenger of Allah, we heard words from you today we never heard before today! He said: And did you hear it? They said: Yes. He said: Jibril (peace be upon him) appeared to me when I ascended a step and said: Far removed is one who reaches his parents in old age, or one of them, yet they do not admit him to Paradise! He said: I said: Amin. And he said: Far removed is one in whose presence you are mentioned yet he does not send blessings upon you! So I said: Amin. Then he said: Far removed is one who reaches Ramadan yet is not forgiven! So I said: Amin."
"17397","316 - Ja'far ibn Sulayman an-Nawfali narrated to us, Abdul-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Awsi narrated, Sulayman ibn Salim, freedman of the family of Jahsh, narrated, Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah narrated, from his father, from his grandfather who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition, and said: If Allah grants them safety, I shall surely thank Him - or he said: It is upon me, if Allah grants them safety, to thank Him - so they gained spoils and returned safe. He said: O Allah, to You is praise with thanks, and to You is favor with grace - people waited for him to do something but saw nothing, so they said: O Messenger of Allah, you said what you said. He said: Did I not say: O Allah, to You is praise with thanks and to You is favor with grace?"
"17398","317 - Ibrahim ibn Duhaym ad-Dimashqi narrated to us, my father narrated. And Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated - both said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated, Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah narrated to me, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ka'b ibn Ujrah: There shall be rulers after me, and he described them with injustice. Whoever enters upon them, believes them in their lies and helps them in their wickedness is not of me and I am not of him, and shall never come to me at the Basin. Whoever does not believe them in their lies nor help them in their wickedness is of me and I am of him, and shall come to me at the Basin. O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, flesh grown from unlawful earnings rightfully shall not enter Paradise; the Fire is more deserving of it and the Fire is more rightful for it."
"17399","318 - Muhammad ibn Ali as-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated, Qudamah ibn Muhammad al-Ashja'i narrated, Dawud ibn al-Mughirah narrated, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, people go out in the morning in two ways: one purchasing himself and freeing it, and one selling himself and destroying it. O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, prayer is a proof, fasting is a shield, and charity extinguishes the anger of the Lord as water extinguishes fire. O Ka'b ibn Ujrah, seek refuge from the rule of fools! I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the rule of fools? He said: There shall be rulers who narrate and lie, and act and do wrong. Whoever comes to them, believes them in their lies and helps them in their injustice is not of me and I am not of him, and shall not come to me at the Basin. Whoever does not come to them, nor believe them in their lies, nor help them in their injustice is of me and I am of him, and shall come to me at the Basin."
"17400","319 - Ibrahim ibn Duhaym ad-Dimashqi narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Yahya ibn Yazid ibn Abdul-Malik an-Nawfali narrated, from his father, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution, perfects it, then heads to the mosque of Quba, intending nothing else, with nothing driving him in the morning except prayer in the mosque of Quba, and prays therein four rak'ahs reciting in each rak'ah the Mother of the Book, shall have the reward of one performing umrah to the House of Allah."
